const PROJECTS = [{"id":1,"name":"Reaction-time game (Sandy's first build)","difficulty":"absolute beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from your laptop to the Uno (5V via the Uno's USB-B port). No batteries, no wall plug.","description":"**What it is**: A little red light blinks on after a surprise pause. You slap the button as fast as you can, and your reaction time pops up on the laptop screen in milliseconds.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Race your dad, your brother, anyone who walks past, and see who has the fastest thumb in the house. Most grown-ups land around 250 ms, so if you beat that you get to brag about it forever.\n\n**Goal:** Arduino lights an LED after a random 2-5 second pause. Player slaps the button as fast as possible after the LED comes on. Reaction time prints to the Serial Monitor in milliseconds. Aiming for sub-250 ms on a good day.\n\n**First electronics project, age 10, no soldering needed.**\n\n## Parts list (allocated below)\n\n- Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone)\n- Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm\n- 1x red 5mm LED\n- 1x 220R resistor (colour bands: red-red-brown-gold)\n- 1x tactile push-button, 4-pin (3x6x3.1mm)\n- 4x M-M jumper wires\n- USB cable for the Uno - check the clone (USB-B \"square printer\", mini-USB, or USB-C; grab one in advance)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nSee the diagram below for the circuit. When building it on the breadboard:\n\n- The breadboard's two long side rails are usually power and ground. Use one rail as GND.\n- The short rows in the middle are joined under the plastic; same row = electrically the same.\n- Plug the LED so its long leg (+) and short leg (-) end up in different rows.\n- The 220Ω resistor goes between the LED's short-leg row and the GND rail.\n- The push-button has 4 legs but only 2 are \"the switch\". If pressing doesn't register, rotate the button 90° and re-seat.\n- Run one M-M jumper from the Arduino GND pin to the GND rail so the whole rail is grounded.\n\n## Talking points while he wires it up\n\nDrip-feed one at a time, not all at once.\n\n- **LEDs have a long leg (+) and a short leg (-).** Backwards = no light, but no damage.\n- **The resistor stops the LED getting too much current** and burning out. Garden-hose analogy: too much water blows the nozzle.\n- **A breadboard's short rows are joined under the plastic.** The two long rails on the sides are usually power and ground.\n- **The code's `loop()` runs forever**, top to bottom, then starts again. That's why the game keeps coming back round.\n- **Average human reaction time is about 250 ms.** That's the benchmark to beat.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\nGive him the goal, let him hunt for the change in the code.\n\n- Make the random pause longer or shorter.\n- Cheat-detect: if the button is pressed *before* the LED lights, print \"Too early!\" instead of a time.\n- Best-of-five mode: track and average over 5 rounds.\n- Add a second LED that turns on when he beats his previous best time.\n\n## Prep checklist for Andy\n\n- [ ] Install the Arduino IDE from [arduino.cc](https://arduino.cc) - free, Windows.\n- [ ] Plug the Uno in once yourself first. Windows will prompt for a CH340 USB driver - let it install.\n- [ ] Open the IDE: **Tools -> Port** should show a COM port. Set **Tools -> Board -> Arduino Uno**.\n- [ ] Confirm the USB cable type the clone uses.\n- [ ] Pre-count one 220R resistor (red-red-brown-gold) so he isn't squinting at colour bands on the first try.\n\n## Next project once he's hooked\n\n**Wave-controlled lamp.** Same Uno, same breadboard, swap the button for the HC-SR04 ultrasonic distance sensor. LED brightens as your hand gets closer, dims as you move it away. Two extra wires and one extra component beyond this build.","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1,"notes":"Brain of the project"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":4,"notes":"4 of the 40 used for this build"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"Output: lights up = GO"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Wiring base, no soldering"},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current limit for the LED"},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x3.1mm (4-pin, black/red)","qty":1,"notes":"Player input button"}]},{"id":22,"name":"Bedroom Temperature Dashboard","difficulty":"absolute beginner","power_supply":"USB-C cable from a laptop or USB charger to the XIAO (5 V on the USB rail, regulated to 3.3 V on-board)","description":"**What it is**: Plug in a tiny chip the size of your thumbnail. Open a web browser on any phone or laptop in the house, type in its address, and a real web page pops up showing a live temperature number that refreshes itself every 5 seconds.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You just built a website. Not on the internet, on YOUR desk - a thing smaller than a stick of gum is hosting a page your dad can open on his phone from the couch, and you made it.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x XIAO ESP32-C3\n- 1x USB-C cable (data-capable, plugged into a laptop or any 5 V USB charger)\n- GeeFam WiFi (2.4 GHz) within range of where the XIAO sits\n\nThat is the entire bill of materials. No breadboard, no jumpers, no sensor module - the chip measures its own internal temperature.\n\n**Before flashing:** open the sketch and replace `PASSWORD_HERE` on the `WIFI_PASSWORD` line with the real GeeFam WiFi password. The sketch will compile either way, but it cannot join the network without the real password.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThere is no wiring to do. Plug the USB-C cable into the XIAO and into a laptop or a phone charger. The 5 V on the USB rail powers the XIAO; the on-board regulator drops it to 3.3 V for the ESP32-C3.\n\nTwo practical notes:\n\n1. Use a short, known-good data cable plugged directly into the laptop (not through a hub). The XIAO is known to brown out on thin cables when the WiFi radio first kicks in.\n2. The first time you upload, you may need to hold the BOOT button (next to the USB-C connector) while plugging in, to force the bootloader. After that, normal uploads should \"just work\".\n\n## Talking points\n\n- This is the first project where the XIAO does two things at once: it joins WiFi AND it answers HTTP requests from a browser. The Arduino core handles the messy networking parts so the sketch stays short.\n- **Honest framing of the \"sensor\":** every ESP32 has a small thermal diode used internally for thermal management, and `temperatureRead()` exposes it as a Celsius value. But the chip warms itself by running, so the reading is the *chip* temperature, not the *room* temperature - it will typically sit at 40-55 C even in a cool room. The web page says this in a yellow note so nobody is confused. Honesty about what a sensor is actually measuring is a real engineering skill.\n- The web page is one self-contained HTML string built in the sketch. It uses `<meta http-equiv='refresh' content='5'>` to tell the browser to reload itself every 5 seconds. No JavaScript, no separate JSON endpoint - the simplest possible \"live\" web page.\n- Why no breadboard / resistor / external sensor? Because the goal of this project is to *just* learn how to host a web page on a microcontroller, with nothing else in the way. The next project adds the real sensor.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add a real external temperature sensor like a DHT22 or BME280 to measure the *room* instead of the chip. Now it is a useful bedroom thermometer (and the page title can honestly say \"Bedroom Temperature\").\n- Once a real sensor is in, keep the last N readings in memory and draw a small graph in the browser. (That is the natural next step up in difficulty - it adds a ring buffer, JSON, and HTML canvas drawing.)\n- Have the XIAO publish each reading over MQTT to the Orange Pi (once the Pi is booted). That is the bridge from \"one device with a webpage\" to \"a real home-automation network\".\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nThis is the smallest possible \"real\" IoT project: a device on the network that you open in a browser like any other website. Once Andy sees a temperature reading updating in Chrome that is being served by a thumbnail-sized chip on his desk, the rest of the kit makes sense - the ESP32s are tiny web servers / data sources, and the Orange Pi will eventually be the always-on hub that collects from all of them. It also reuses the already-working WiFi connect logic from `XIAO_ESP32C3/Wifi/connect-wifi.ino`, so the only new idea is \"host a page\".","allocations":[{"item_name":"Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3","qty":1,"notes":"Hosts the tiny web server and reads its own internal chip temperature."}]},{"id":23,"name":"Mood Meter (touch the dot, change your face)","difficulty":"absolute beginner","power_supply":"USB-C cable from laptop to the XIAO ESP32-C3 (5V in, regulated to 3.3V on-board; 3.3V powers the OLED and the TTP223B via the breadboard rails)","description":"**What it is**: A little screen shows a face - happy, sad, surprised, sleepy. Touch the metal dot with your finger and the face flips to the next mood. No buttons, no switches, just tap.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It feels like magic the first time - you barely brush the pad and the face changes, like it can read your mind. Stick it on your bedroom door and make it match your mood so your brother knows whether to knock or run.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x XIAO ESP32-C3 (the brain - runs the sketch, drives the screen)\n- 1x 0.96\" SSD1306 OLED, I2C version (4 pins: VCC, GND, SCL, SDA)\n- 1x TTP223B capacitive touch module (3 pins: VCC, GND, I/O)\n- 1x breadboard (shared 3.3V and GND rails)\n- 8x F-M jumper wires (XIAO header to breadboard rails and modules)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nEverything in this project runs on 3.3V. The XIAO ESP32-C3 is a 3.3V board, the SSD1306 OLED works happily on 3.3V (it actually prefers it), and the TTP223B is rated for 2-5.5V. Do NOT use the 5V pin on the XIAO for any of this.\n\nPower rails on the breadboard:\n- Red rail: XIAO 3V3 pin -> red (+) rail\n- Black rail: XIAO GND pin -> blue (-) rail\n\nOLED (I2C):\n- VCC -> red rail (3.3V)\n- GND -> blue rail\n- SDA -> XIAO D4 (GPIO 6)\n- SCL -> XIAO D5 (GPIO 7)\n\nTTP223B touch module:\n- VCC -> red rail (3.3V)\n- GND -> blue rail\n- I/O (sometimes labeled SIG or OUT) -> XIAO D0 (GPIO 2)\n\nThe TTP223B's I/O pin sits LOW when nothing is touching the pad and goes HIGH when a finger lands on the metal area on top of the module. No pull-up or pull-down resistor needed - the chip handles that internally.\n\nTwo tiny gotchas worth knowing:\n1. The OLED address is usually 0x3C but a few clones are 0x3D - if the screen stays blank, that's the first thing to swap in the code.\n2. The first time the TTP223B powers on it spends ~0.5s calibrating \"what counts as not-touched\". Don't keep your finger on the pad while you press reset.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- I2C uses just two wires (SDA = data, SCL = clock) and can talk to up to ~120 devices in theory. The OLED listens on address 0x3C; if we added a second I2C gadget (temperature sensor, real-time clock, another screen) it would share the same two wires with a different address.\n- \"Capacitive touch\" means the chip is measuring how much electrical charge the pad can hold. A finger nearby adds a tiny bit of capacitance, the chip notices the change, and flips the output pin. No moving parts, no wear.\n- The face is drawn from primitives: `drawCircle`, `drawLine`, `fillCircle`, plus a couple of stray pixels for the smile curl. It's the same toolbox an old 8-bit game would have used.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add more moods. Adding a fifth case is one enum entry, one name string, one extra `case` in `drawFace`, and bumping the modulo from 4 to 5.\n- Make the eyes blink every few seconds when idle (timer + extra draw call).\n- Connect to WiFi (SSID `GeeFam`, password `PASSWORD_HERE`) and pull the real time from an NTP server so the log timestamp is a clock time instead of seconds-since-boot.\n- Hold-to-confirm: instead of cycling on every tap, require a 300ms hold so accidental brushes don't change the face.\n- Once the Orange Pi is running Mosquitto, publish each mood change as an MQTT message - first cross-device milestone for the kit.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nTwo of the most useful \"building block\" skills for everything else in the kit live in this project: talking to an I2C peripheral, and reacting to a non-button input. Once OLED graphics feel familiar, every future sensor reading (temperature, distance, humidity) has somewhere nice to show up. And capacitive touch is the gateway to projects that don't look like electronics at all - hidden touch points behind wood, glass, or a 3D-printed shell.","allocations":[{"item_name":"Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3","qty":1,"notes":"Brains of the project; drives the OLED over I2C and reads the touch pad"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":8,"notes":"XIAO header pins to breadboard rails and modules (power, GND, SDA, SCL, touch signal)"},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1,"notes":"Draws the animated emoji face and the mood log line"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Common rails for 3.3V and GND; lets us share power across modules without soldering"},{"item_name":"TTP223B Capacitive Touch Module","qty":1,"notes":"Touch input - cycles through the four moods"}]},{"id":24,"name":"Theremin Lite","difficulty":"absolute beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from laptop to the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (5V via the micro-USB port, regulated on-board to 3.3V for the chip and broken out as VIN/5V for the ultrasonic sensor)","description":"**What it is**: Wave your hand over the breadboard. The red LED blinks slowly when your hand is far away, then pulses faster and faster as you move it closer, like a heartbeat speeding up. No touching anything - just air.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You are literally controlling a light with your hand from across the desk, like a wizard. Challenge your dad to guess the distance just by watching the blink speed - then move your hand and prove him wrong.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the brain - reads the sensor, blinks the LED)\n- HC-SR04 ultrasonic distance sensor (the \"eye\" that bounces sound off your hand)\n- 5mm red LED (the visible heartbeat)\n- 220R resistor (current limiter for the LED - keeps it from burning out)\n- 1K resistor + 2.2K resistor (voltage divider on the Echo line - see Wiring notes)\n- Breadboard, 400 tie-points\n- A handful of male-male jumper wires\n- A USB cable from your laptop to the ESP32 (power and code-upload in one)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n**The HC-SR04 runs on 5V, but the ESP32 is a 3.3V chip.** Powering the sensor from the ESP32's VIN pin (which mirrors the USB 5V rail on most NodeMCU-32S boards - some silkscreens label this pin `5V` or `VBUS` instead of `VIN`, and on a few revisions VIN sits behind a Schottky diode so it reads ~4.7V; any of those is fine for the HC-SR04) is the easy option. But the sensor's Echo pin will swing up to 5V, and that's too much for the ESP32 GPIO. The fix is a tiny voltage divider made of two resistors:\n\n- Sensor Echo -> 1K resistor -> ESP32 GPIO 18 (this junction is what the ESP32 reads)\n- From that same junction -> 2.2K resistor -> GND\n\nThat divider pulls the 5V Echo signal down to roughly 3.4V at the ESP32 pin. Trig is an output FROM the ESP32 to the sensor, so 3.3V is usually enough to drive it - no divider needed there. **Caveat:** a small minority of HC-SR04 clones (especially newer ones with stricter input thresholds) won't trigger reliably from a 3.3V Trig signal. The symptom is that distance reads -1 (\"no echo\") even when an object is clearly in front of the sensor. The fix is to drive Trig through a level shifter, or use a 5V-tolerant logic source - but try the simple wiring first.\n\n**LED side:** the long leg of the LED (anode, +) goes through the 220R resistor to GPIO 19. The short leg (cathode, -) goes to a GND rail. The resistor can be on either side of the LED - it's there to limit the current, the LED doesn't care which side.\n\n**Pins used:**\n\n- GPIO 5  -> HC-SR04 Trig\n- GPIO 18 -> HC-SR04 Echo (through the voltage divider)\n- GPIO 19 -> LED anode (through the 220R resistor)\n- 5V (VIN) -> HC-SR04 VCC\n- GND -> HC-SR04 GND, LED cathode, and the bottom of the voltage divider\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **How ultrasonic distance works:** the sensor chirps a 40kHz \"click\" your ears can't hear, then listens for the echo. The ESP32 times how long the click takes to come back. Sound travels about 343 metres per second in air, so distance in cm = duration in microseconds * 0.0343 / 2 (the \"divide by 2\" is because the sound made a round trip).\n- **Why the LED gets faster when your hand gets closer:** the code maps distance to a blink delay. Far away -> long delay -> slow blink. Close -> short delay -> rapid pulse. That mapping is a simple linear interpolation (lerp).\n- **Why three resistors for one LED:** one limits LED current (220R), the other two protect the ESP32 from 5V on its Echo input. Two different jobs.\n- **Why GPIO 5/18/19:** these are \"safe\" output pins on the ESP32. Some pins (like 0, 2, 12, 15) are tied to boot behaviour and pulling them HIGH or LOW at startup can prevent the board from booting. Sticking to 5, 18, 19, 21, 22, 23 avoids that whole class of headache.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Swap the LED for the onboard blue LED on GPIO 2 (one less wire on the breadboard).\n- Print distance to the Serial Monitor (115200 baud) so you can see numbers next to the blinking.\n- Add a buzzer in parallel with the LED for an actual Geiger-counter sound effect.\n- Use `ledcWrite` (PWM) instead of digital ON/OFF to make the LED smoothly fade brighter as your hand approaches, instead of blinking.\n- Hook three LEDs (green / yellow / red) and light them like a traffic light based on distance bands.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nThis is the smallest possible bridge between the physical world and code. There are no buttons, no switches - you wave your hand in mid-air, sound waves bounce, and a light reacts. That's the same loop every smart device on Earth runs: sense,","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"Main controller; reads ultrasonic sensor and drives the LED"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Male-to-male jumpers between ESP32 header, sensor, and breadboard rails"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"The visible heartbeat that speeds up as your hand gets closer"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Solderless wiring surface for the whole circuit"},{"item_name":"Resistor 1K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Top half of the voltage divider on the sensor's 5V Echo line"},{"item_name":"Resistor 2.2K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Bottom half of the divider; drops Echo to a safe ~3.4V for the ESP32"},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current-limiting resistor in series with the LED"},{"item_name":"HC-SR04 Ultrasonic Distance Sensor","qty":1,"notes":"The distance 'eye' - bounces 40kHz sound off your hand"}]},{"id":25,"name":"Don't-Touch-The-Wire steady-hand maze","difficulty":"absolute beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from laptop to the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S micro-USB port (5V in; ESP32 runs on 3.3V on-board, TM1637 display fed from the 5V VIN pin)","description":"**What it is**: Guide a little metal loop along a bendy wire track without letting them touch. Every time you bump the wire, the red light flashes, a number on the display ticks up, and your strike count climbs. Hold steady and the green light stays on instead.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It's the classic carnival \"buzz wire\" game, but you built it yourself, and the screen keeps score so you can finally settle who in the family has the steadiest hand. Bend the track into a tougher shape every round to mess with whoever goes next.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- **ESP32 NodeMCU-32S** - the brain. Watches the wand and updates the score.\n- **4-digit 7-segment Display Module (TM1637, 4-pin: VCC/GND/CLK/DIO)** - shows the strike count. Powered from 5V (see wiring notes).\n- **LED 5mm Red + 220R resistor** - lights while the wand is touching the wire.\n- **LED 5mm Green + 220R resistor** - lights while the wand is in the clear.\n- **Tactile Push-button Switch (3x6x4.3mm, white pin)** - reset-to-zero button.\n- **Stiff bare wire (~30 cm)** bent into a wavy track. **Wand loop (~3 cm)** of the same wire on a short insulated handle. (Both physical-craft parts, not on the board.)\n- **Heat-shrink tubing / electrical tape / pen barrel** - the insulated handle for the wand so the player's hand doesn't complete the circuit.\n- **Breadboard + jumper wires** to hold everything together.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe whole game is one big switch: the bent maze wire goes to GPIO 23 with the ESP32's internal **pull-up** enabled, so the pin idles HIGH (3.3 V). The wand loop goes straight to **GND**. The instant the loop touches the maze wire, GPIO 23 is dragged to LOW - that's our \"you touched it!\" signal.\n\n- Maze wire -> long jumper to GPIO 23 (INPUT_PULLUP).\n- Wand loop -> long jumper to a GND rail on the breadboard.\n- Red LED: GPIO 18 -> 220R -> LED anode -> LED cathode -> GND.\n- Green LED: GPIO 19 -> 220R -> LED anode -> LED cathode -> GND.\n- Reset button: one leg to GPIO 5 (INPUT_PULLUP), the leg diagonally opposite to GND. The other two pins are the internal short; ignore them.\n- 7-segment module (TM1637): **VCC -> VIN (5 V from the USB rail)**, GND -> GND, CLK -> GPIO 22, DIO -> GPIO 21.\n\n**Why VIN, not 3V3, for the TM1637:** these modules are spec'd for 5 V. At 3.3 V many clones run dim or flicker. The NodeMCU-32S exposes the USB 5 V rail on its VIN pin, which is the right home for the display. The CLK/DIO lines on the module idle at 5 V through pull-ups - the ESP32 is rated as 5 V-tolerant on these GPIOs in practice, and using a TM1637 this way is the standard beginner wiring.\n\n**Heads-up on GPIO 5:** it's an ESP32 \"strapping pin\" - the chip looks at it during boot. With INPUT_PULLUP and the button wired to GND, it idles HIGH (released), which matches what the chip wants at boot. Safe. Just don't later wire something to GPIO 5 that holds it LOW at power-on, or the board will refuse to start and you'll wonder why.\n\nThe ESP32 itself is powered by the USB cable from the laptop; the display takes 5 V from VIN, and the LEDs / button take their references from GND.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **Pull-ups in one sentence:** \"The pin is held HIGH by a tiny resistor inside the chip until something outside drags it LOW. We use the wand-to-maze contact as the thing that drags it LOW.\" If you skip the pull-up, the pin is *floating* - touching nothing, but picking up stray noise from the air - and you'll see random ghost strikes with the wand sitting still. The pull-up is what makes \"not touching\" a definite, repeatable state.\n- **Why a resistor on every LED:** an LED is basically a short-circuit with a glow, so the resistor sets the current. A red LED drops about 2.0 V, so across a 220R resistor on a 3.3 V GPIO the current is (3.3 - 2.0) / 220 = about **6 mA**. That's gentle - plenty visible as an indicator, well under the ESP32's ~20 mA-per-pin comfort limit. If you ever want it brighter, drop to 150R for about 8.7 mA, or move the LED to the 5 V VIN rail (still through the resistor) for around 13 mA.\n- **Edge detection:** we only count a strike on the *moment* of contact - the HIGH-to-LOW transition - not every single loop where the pin happens to be LOW. That way, holding the wand against the wire doesn't rack up hundreds of strikes; one touch = one strike, no matter how long you linger.\n- **Debouncing (the lighter version):** real metal contacts are jittery and can register as a few touches in 1 ms. After the edge fires we wait 20 ms and re-check; if the pin is still LOW it was a","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"Brain: reads contact, drives LEDs and display"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"ESP32 pins to breadboard, and long leads to maze wire + wand"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Board-to-board jumpers"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1,"notes":"All-clear indicator on GPIO 19"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"Strike indicator on GPIO 18"},{"item_name":"4-digit 7-segment Display Module","qty":1,"notes":"Strike counter (TM1637, VCC=5V/VIN, CLK=GPIO22, DIO=GPIO21)"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds LEDs, resistors, button, common rails"},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":2,"notes":"Current limiter, one per LED (~6 mA at 3.3 V)"},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x4.3mm (white pin)","qty":1,"notes":"Reset on GPIO 5 (strapping pin, idle-HIGH is safe)"}]},{"id":26,"name":"Temperature Mood Dial","difficulty":"absolute beginner","power_supply":"USB-C cable from laptop (or any 5V USB charger) to the XIAO ESP32-C3; the XIAO's 5V pin feeds the SG90 servo, and the XIAO's 3V3 pin feeds the DHT11.","description":"**What it is**: A little cardboard dial with a pointer that swings to show how warm or cold the room is. When the air is freezing it points at BRRR, when it's hot it swings all the way to LAVA, and it keeps re-checking every couple of seconds on its own.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Breathe on the sensor and watch the needle creep toward TOASTY in real time, or stick it by a window and catch the moment the sun comes out. You built a thing that feels the world and reacts - no buttons, no app, just your dial quietly paying attention.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3 (the brain - reads the sensor, drives the servo).\n- 1x DHT11 Temperature & Humidity Sensor module (3-pin breakout: VCC / DATA / GND).\n- 1x SG90 Servo (the moving needle).\n- 1x Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (gives the XIAO and DHT11 a home and shared power rails).\n- 1x 470uF electrolytic capacitor (sits across 5V and GND near the servo to absorb its current spikes).\n- 1x Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc pack (servo lead and DHT11 module both need female-ended jumpers to reach the breadboard).\n- 1x Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc pack (a few short wires for the 5V, 3V3 and GND rails on the breadboard).\n- Craft supplies (not in inventory, grab from a drawer): a square of cardboard, a marker, a thin cardboard strip for the needle, sticky tape.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe XIAO has two power-out pins: `5V` (passthrough from the USB-C jack) and `3V3` (the on-board regulator). It matters which one we use for each part:\n\n- The SG90 servo really wants 5V. Its motor and coil are spec'd for 4.8-6V; on 3.3V it would be weak and twitchy.\n- The DHT11 module is happy anywhere from 3.0V to 5.5V, so we run it from `3V3`. That keeps its DATA line at 3.3V, which matches the XIAO's GPIO. If we powered the DHT11 from 5V, its DATA pin would idle at 5V too and we'd be pushing 5V into a 3.3V-only GPIO - that's the level-shifting problem the talking points mention. Running the sensor at 3V3 sidesteps it entirely with zero extra parts.\n\n1. Plug the XIAO into the breadboard so each row of pins lands on its own column.\n2. Run M-M jumpers to build three rails:\n   - XIAO `5V` -> red (+) rail (for the servo only).\n   - XIAO `3V3` -> orange tie-strip a few rows over (for the DHT11 only). Most breadboards only have two power rails, so just pick an unused row and call it the 3V3 rail - the DHT11 is the only thing that touches it.\n   - XIAO `GND` -> blue (-) rail (shared by everything).\n3. DHT11 module pins:\n   - `VCC` (or `+`) -> the **3V3** rail (NOT the red 5V rail).\n   - `GND` (or `-`) -> blue rail (GND).\n   - `DATA` (or `OUT`) -> XIAO `D2` (GPIO 4).\n4. SG90 servo wires:\n   - Red -> red rail (5V).\n   - Brown (or black) -> blue rail (GND).\n   - Orange (or yellow) -> XIAO `D3` (GPIO 5).\n5. The 470uF capacitor. Look for the stripe with a \"-\" on the side - that's the negative leg. Plug the negative leg into the blue (GND) rail and the other leg into the red (5V) rail, as close to where the servo's red and brown wires plug in as possible. This little reservoir tops up the rail during the brief instant the servo motor jerks and would otherwise sag the 5V supply.\n6. Tape a thin cardboard arrow onto the servo horn. Draw a half-circle gauge on a bigger piece of cardboard and label five equal-width zones across the sweep: BRRR / CHILLY / NICE / TOASTY / LAVA. Tape the servo behind the gauge so the arrow points through a slot.\n\nIf the servo still twitches at startup and the XIAO browns out (the on-board LED blinks oddly or the serial monitor restarts), check that the capacitor's negative leg really is on GND, then try a known-good USB cable plugged directly into the computer rather than a hub. The cap handles the spike; the cable handles the steady draw.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The DHT11 is \"one-wire-ish\": you ask, and one wiggling pin gives back both temperature and humidity. The Adafruit DHT library does all the timing for us.\n- A servo isn't told a speed - it's told an angle. We `map()` Celsius into degrees so 15 C -> 0 deg, 30 C -> 180 deg.\n- The DHT11 is slow. It only updates about once a second and only in whole degrees (no half-steps), so we read it once every two seconds and slowly nudge the servo toward the new angle. That avoids the dial jittering on tiny readings.\n- Why a capacitor near the servo? When the servo motor starts moving, it grabs a big gulp of current for a split second. A thin USB cable can't deliver that fast enough, so the 5V rail dips, the XIAO sees a brown-out, and the whole board can reset. The cap is a tiny b","allocations":[{"item_name":"Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3","qty":1,"notes":"Brain - reads DHT11, drives servo PWM"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"Reaches the DHT11 module and servo header from the breadboard"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Short hops on the breadboard power rails"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 470uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1,"notes":"Bridges 5V and GND rails near the servo to absorb stall-current spikes"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Hosts the XIAO and shares 5V / 3V3 / GND rails"},{"item_name":"SG90 Servo (9g micro)","qty":1,"notes":"Moves the cardboard needle across the gauge"},{"item_name":"DHT11 Temperature & Humidity Sensor","qty":1,"notes":"Temperature input for the gauge (powered from 3V3, in spec)"}]},{"id":27,"name":"Shake-to-roll electronic D6 dice","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from laptop to the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S micro-USB port (5V in, regulated to 3.3V on board for all modules)","description":"**What it is**: Shake the breadboard like a pair of dice in your hand. A little green light flashes, numbers blur across the screen, and then it stops on your roll - 1 through 6, just like a real die.\n\n**Why it's fun**: No more lost dice under the couch on board game night. Plus the numbers slow down as they land, so every shake feels like a real tumble - your brain totally falls for it.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the brain - reads the shake sensor and drives the display)\n- SW-420 vibration switch module (digital \"did something shake?\" sensor)\n- 4-digit 7-segment display module (TM1637 driver - only needs two data pins)\n- 1x 5mm green LED + 1x 220 ohm resistor (lights up while the dice is \"rolling\")\n- Breadboard + DuPont jumper wires\n- Micro-USB cable from your laptop to power the ESP32\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe ESP32 sits across the breadboard's centre gutter. Both side rails get jumpered to the ESP32: one rail is 3.3V, the other is GND.\n\n**Power rails**\n- ESP32 `3V3` pin -> red rail on the breadboard\n- ESP32 `GND` pin -> blue (ground) rail on the breadboard\n\n**SW-420 vibration module** (3 pins: VCC, GND, DO)\n- VCC -> 3.3V red rail\n- GND -> blue ground rail\n- DO  -> ESP32 GPIO27\n\nGPIO27 is a regular general-purpose pin on the ESP32. We deliberately avoided GPIO12, GPIO14 and a couple of others called \"strapping pins\" - those control how the chip boots, and a sensor pulling them around at power-on can confuse the boot process.\n\nThe SW-420 is essentially a digital switch. It sits HIGH normally and flickers LOW when the little spring inside gets jostled. The board has a sensitivity trimpot you can twist with a small screwdriver if it's too touchy or not touchy enough.\n\n**TM1637 4-digit display** (4 pins: CLK, DIO, VCC, GND)\n- VCC -> 3.3V red rail\n- GND -> blue ground rail\n- CLK -> ESP32 GPIO18\n- DIO -> ESP32 GPIO19\n\nThese modules are happy on 3.3V even though most listings say 5V. Two data pins is all it takes - the TM1637 chip on the back of the module handles the actual segment timing.\n\n**Green LED** (the \"rolling\" indicator)\n- Long leg (anode) -> 220 ohm resistor -> ESP32 GPIO26\n- Short leg (cathode) -> blue ground rail\n\nThe resistor goes BEFORE the LED, on the GPIO side. The order doesn't matter electrically, but it's a good habit.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **Why the SW-420 is \"noisy\":** one shake makes the spring inside rattle on and off many times. The code handles this with a classic two-step debounce: read once, wait 50 ms for the bouncing to settle, read again. If both reads agree, it was a real shake. The exact same trick works for noisy push buttons.\n- **Why the dice slows down:** the animation array `delays[]` has shorter delays at the start and longer ones at the end. That alone makes it feel like a real tumbling die. No physics needed.\n- **Where the randomness comes from:** `esp_random()` uses the ESP32's hardware random number generator. On an Arduino Uno you'd have to \"seed\" the random function with noise from a floating pin - on the ESP32 you get true hardware randomness for free.\n- **Why 3.3V is fine for all of this:** the TM1637 chip is happy from 3.3V to 5.5V, the SW-420 just needs enough voltage to drive a logic-level signal, and the LED is comfortable too. Worth knowing: at 3.3V drive with a 220 ohm resistor and a typical green LED (forward voltage ~2V), the LED current is about (3.3 - 2) / 220 = 6 mA, which is visible but not super bright. If you want the announce-blink to really pop, swap to a 150 ohm resistor - that pushes the current up to ~9 mA, still well under the LED's limit.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Twist the sensitivity trimpot on the SW-420 so it triggers from a firm shake but ignores you bumping the table.\n- Show the previous roll in the leftmost digits and the new roll on the right - the display has 4 digits, you're only using 1.\n- Add a second LED that lights up only when you roll a 6 (a \"critical hit\" indicator).\n- Replace the dice with a 2d6 simulator (two dice at once, one per pair of digits) for board games.\n- Add a piezo buzzer that beeps faster and faster as the roll slows down.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nYou're replacing a physical object (a die) with code, but the *feel* of using it is what makes it work - the spin-down animation tricks your brain into accepting it as a real roll. That's the core lesson of human-computer interaction in one tiny project: the math is trivial (`random() % 6 + 1`), but the perceived experience is everything.\n\nIt also introduces a","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"Brain of the dice; reads shake sensor, drives display and indicator LED"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":10,"notes":"Wires between ESP32 pins, breadboard rails, and the three modules"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1,"notes":"Status indicator; lights up while the dice is 'rolling'"},{"item_name":"4-digit 7-segment Display Module","qty":1,"notes":"TM1637-based display that shows the roll animation and final number"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the ESP32 and provides power/ground rails for all modules"},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current-limiting resistor for the green LED"},{"item_name":"SW-420 Vibration Switch Module","qty":1,"notes":"Detects the shake that triggers a new roll"}]},{"id":28,"name":"Motion-Triggered Jump Scare Box","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from laptop to the ESP32 NodeMCU (5V via the USB port; the 5V rail feeds the PIR and the servo, the 3.3V GPIO drives signals). A 470 uF capacitor sits across the servo's 5V/GND to soak up inrush spikes.","description":"**What it is**: Set the little cardboard box on a shelf and walk away. When someone wanders past, the lid flips open and a paper spider pops out at them.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Hide it outside your sister's bedroom door and wait for the scream. The box sits perfectly still until the exact second someone walks by - it feels less like electronics and more like the box is alive.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S - the brain. Reads the PIR, blinks the LED, twitches the servo.\n- 1x HC-SR501 PIR Motion Sensor - the \"eye\". Detects warm bodies moving past.\n- 1x SG90 Servo (9g micro) - the muscle. Tape it under the lid; horn flips the lid open.\n- 1x 5mm Red LED - \"armed\" indicator so you know the trap is live.\n- 1x 220R resistor - current-limiter for the LED so it does not burn out.\n- 1x 470 uF electrolytic capacitor - soaks up the servo's start-up current spike so the ESP32 does not brown out and reset when the lid snaps. Long leg (+) to the 5V rail, short leg (-) to GND. Polarity matters - backwards can pop the cap.\n- 1x Breadboard - holds the LED, resistor, capacitor, and power rails.\n- Jumper wires (F-M for the PIR/servo headers, M-M for breadboard hops).\n- USB cable from laptop to power the ESP32.\n- (Not in the inventory) a small cardboard box and a paper spider on a spring or rubber band.\n\n## One-time software setup\n\nBefore this sketch will compile, install the **ESP32Servo** library:\n\n1. Arduino IDE -> Tools -> Manage Libraries...\n2. Search for `ESP32Servo` (by Kevin Harrington / John K. Bennett).\n3. Click Install. Done - this is a one-time step.\n\nIf you skip this you will get `ESP32Servo.h: No such file or directory` when you try to upload.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n- The HC-SR501 is a 5V module. Wire its VCC to the ESP32 `VIN` pin (the raw rail coming back out of the USB port), and GND to any ESP32 GND. Its OUT pin only swings up to ~3.3V, so it is safe to feed straight into ESP32 GPIO 13 - no level shifter, no resistor divider.\n- The SG90 also runs from 5V. Red wire to `VIN`, brown wire to GND, orange (signal) to **GPIO 25**. The ESP32 GPIO is 3.3V, but the SG90 signal pin reads a 3.3V pulse just fine.\n- Place the 470 uF capacitor on the breadboard right next to where the servo's red and brown wires plug in. Long leg goes into the 5V rail row, short leg goes into the GND rail row. This is the single most important non-obvious part of the build - skipping it is what makes \"lid snaps, ESP32 reboots, sketch starts over\" happen.\n- The LED has a long leg (anode, +) and a short leg (cathode, -). Long leg goes through the 220R resistor to GPIO 27. Short leg goes to a GND rail.\n- The PIR has two orange potentiometers on the back. Start with **sensitivity** turned roughly to the middle and **time delay** turned all the way counter-clockwise (shortest hold, ~3 seconds). There is also a jumper on the side with positions **H** and **L**:\n  - **L** (single trigger / non-retriggerable) - the PIR fires once per motion event, then forces a quiet period. This is what we want so the box does not keep firing while you walk past.\n  - **H** (repeatable / retriggerable) - the PIR keeps re-asserting HIGH as long as it sees movement, restarting its hold timer each time. Useful for \"porch light stays on while someone is there\" but wrong for a one-shot scare. Set the jumper to **L**.\n- The PIR needs ~30-60 seconds to \"warm up\" after power-on. During warm-up it can output false HIGHs - the sketch deliberately ignores motion for the first 60 seconds and the LED blinks fast to tell you it is warming up.\n\n## A note on USB power\n\nThe ESP32's `VIN` pin is *not* a clean 5V. After the USB connector and the board's protection diode, you typically see 4.5-4.7 V under load. That is fine for an LED and a PIR, and the SG90 will move on it, but the servo will be a little weaker than its datasheet rating and the PIR can occasionally false-trigger on the supply dip. If the lid feels gutless or the box keeps firing on its own, the upgrade is a separate 5V phone-charger-style supply feeding the servo (with its GND tied to the ESP32's GND). For learning on the desk, USB-from-laptop plus the 470 uF cap is fine.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- A PIR (Passive InfraRed) sensor does not emit anything. It just watches for *changes* in infrared (heat) hitting its dome. If a warm body crosses one zone but not the next, the chip flags motion.\n- A hobby servo does not turn freely - it goes to a specific *angle* between 0 and 180 degrees and holds it. So \"open the lid\" is \"go to 120 degr","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"Brain: reads PIR, drives servo and LED."},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":6,"notes":"ESP32 header to breadboard, PIR header, servo header."},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":4,"notes":"Breadboard rail hops for LED, capacitor, and ground."},{"item_name":"Capacitor 470uF 16V (electrolytic)","qty":1,"notes":"Across servo 5V/GND on the breadboard to absorb SG90 inrush and prevent ESP32 brown-out."},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"Armed indicator on GPIO 27."},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the LED, resistor, capacitor, and shared 5V/GND rails."},{"item_name":"SG90 Servo (9g micro)","qty":1,"notes":"Flips the lid; signal on GPIO 25 (non-strapping), power from VIN."},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current limiter in series with the red LED."},{"item_name":"HC-SR501 PIR Motion Sensor","qty":1,"notes":"Detects motion; 5V VCC, ~3.3V digital OUT into GPIO 13. Jumper on L (single trigger)."}]},{"id":29,"name":"Hidden-Magnet Treasure Hunter","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from laptop to the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (5V via the micro-USB port; ESP32 regulates down to 3.3V on board)","description":"**What it is**: Hide a magnet anywhere - under a couch cushion, inside a book, behind a wall. Wave the sensor near it and a red LED lights up while a little screen counts higher and higher the closer you get.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It is a real treasure detector that sees through stuff your eyes cannot. Make your dad hide the magnet, time how fast you find it, then swap and try to stump him.\n\n## Parts list\n\nFrom the kit:\n\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the brain - reads the Hall sensor, blinks the LED, drives the display)\n- 1x KY-024 Linear Magnetic Hall Sensor (4-pin: A0 analog out, D0 digital out, +, -). This is the *linear* Hall sensor - its analog output voltage swings up or down depending on the magnet's pole and how close it is. We only use the A0 (analog) pin.\n- 1x 4-digit 7-segment Display Module (TM1637-based, 4 pins: VCC, GND, DIO, CLK)\n- 1x LED 5mm Red (any colour works, red is most visible against couch cushions)\n- 1x Resistor 220R 1/8W 1% (the LED's bodyguard - keeps it from burning out)\n- 1x Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)\n- 1x Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc bundle (for breadboard-to-breadboard hops)\n- 1x Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc bundle (for ESP32 header pin to breadboard, and to the KY-024 / TM1637 modules)\n\nYou supply:\n\n- 1x small neodymium magnet - the \"treasure\". A fridge magnet works in a pinch; a small neodymium is much more dramatic.\n- 1x USB cable (laptop to ESP32 NodeMCU-32S, micro-USB).\n\n## Libraries to install\n\nOpen Arduino IDE > Tools > Manage Libraries... and install:\n\n- **TM1637Display** by Avishay Orpaz - drives the 4-digit display.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe ESP32 NodeMCU-32S has plenty of pins. We're using:\n\n- GPIO 34 -> KY-024 A0 (analog signal). GPIO 34 is an input-only ADC pin, perfect for reading an analog voltage. We ignore the D0 (digital) pin entirely.\n- 3.3V -> KY-024 + (the KY-024 is happy on 3.3V)\n- GND -> KY-024 -\n- GPIO 2 -> 220 ohm resistor -> LED anode (long leg), then LED cathode to GND.\n- GPIO 22 -> TM1637 CLK\n- GPIO 21 -> TM1637 DIO\n- 3.3V -> TM1637 VCC (these modules run fine on 3.3V)\n- GND -> TM1637 GND\n\n**About GPIO 2:** GPIO 2 is also the onboard blue LED, so you'll see the onboard one mirror the external red one - free bonus. GPIO 2 is also a \"strapping pin\" the ESP32 reads at power-on to decide its boot mode. The pin must be LOW (or floating) at boot for normal program execution. The sketch deliberately calls `digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW)` *before* `pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT)` in `setup()` so that the moment the pin becomes an output it's already low, which keeps the strapping condition correct. Don't reorder those two lines.\n\nWith no magnet near the KY-024, the analog reading sits around the midpoint (~1900-2100 on a 0-4095 scale). As a magnet approaches, the value swings up OR down depending on which pole of the magnet faces the sensor's flat side. The sketch tracks the absolute distance from the resting baseline, so either pole works.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- Magnetic fields are invisible but the LED makes them visible - that's the whole magic moment.\n- The sensor uses the \"Hall effect\": moving electrons in the sensor get pushed sideways by a magnetic field, which produces a tiny voltage. The ESP32 reads that voltage as a number from 0 to 4095.\n- One pole of the magnet pushes the voltage up, the other pole pushes it down. Flipping the magnet over flips the direction. That's why we measure distance from baseline rather than just \"high\" or \"low\".\n- The 4-digit display shows the raw \"strength\" number so you can watch it climb as you get closer. Great for the kid - it's a literal number going up.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add a buzzer alongside the LED for a metal-detector beep.\n- The sketch already auto-calibrates the baseline on startup - try holding the magnet *near* the sensor at power-on and see how that messes things up. Good lesson in why calibration matters.\n- Make it wireless: post each \"find\" to MQTT once the Orange Pi hub is alive.\n- Swap the single LED for a strip and light up more LEDs the closer you get (Cylon eyes).\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nIt's a sensor that detects something humans literally cannot perceive. Andy's son can hide the magnet under a book, in a cushion, behind a wall, and the ESP32 will still find it. That moment of \"wait, it can see through stuff?\" is the hook. It also teaches the analog-to-digital pipeline (real world -> voltage -> number -> behaviour) which is the foundation of basically every interesting sensor project that comes later.","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"Reads KY-024 analog output, blinks LED, drives TM1637 display"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"Bundle - female ends plug onto ESP32 header pins and KY-024 / TM1637 module pins"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Bundle - used for breadboard rail hops and LED leg routing"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"Blinks faster as the sensor approaches the magnet"},{"item_name":"4-digit 7-segment Display Module","qty":1,"notes":"TM1637-based; displays live field strength (0-9999)"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the LED + resistor and provides power/ground rails"},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current-limiting resistor for the red LED on GPIO 2"},{"item_name":"KY-024 Linear Magnetic Hall Sensor","qty":1,"notes":"Linear/analog Hall sensor; A0 pin feeds GPIO 34 ADC"}]},{"id":30,"name":"Plant SOS","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB-C cable from laptop to the XIAO ESP32-C3 (5V); the XIAO's onboard regulator powers the OLED from its 3.3V rail, and the soil sensor's VCC is gated by a GPIO (D2) so it only sees 3.3V during the ~10 ms read window.","description":"**What it is**: Stick the two metal prongs into your plant's dirt. A tiny screen shows a happy face when the soil is wet, a sleepy face when it's getting dry, and a crying face when the plant is thirsty - and a red LED blinks at you until you water it.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Your plant basically gets a face and tells you how it feels. Spit on the prongs, dunk them in a glass of water, hold them in the air - the little face changes right in front of you like the plant is reacting to what you do.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3 (the brain - reads the sensor, drives the OLED and LED)\n- 1x Soil Moisture Sensor (the two-prong probe + small comparator board; we only use the analog \"AO\" pin)\n- 1x 0.96\" OLED Display (I2C) (the little blue/white screen; talks over 2 wires)\n- 1x LED 5mm Red (the \"thirsty\" warning blinker) - the legs plug directly into the breadboard, no jumper needed\n- 1x Resistor 220R (current limit for the red LED - never wire an LED straight to a GPIO) - leads plug directly into the breadboard, no jumper needed\n- 1x Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (no soldering - everything plugs in)\n- Jumper wires M-M (for the OLED and sensor connections - not for the LED itself)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nPower rails first:\n\n- XIAO **3V3** pin -> breadboard red (+) rail\n- XIAO **GND** pin -> breadboard blue (-) rail\n\nOLED (I2C, 4 wires - it shares the same 2 data lines no matter how many I2C things you add later):\n\n- OLED **VCC** -> red rail (3.3V)\n- OLED **GND** -> blue rail\n- OLED **SDA** -> XIAO **D4** (GPIO 6, the default SDA on XIAO ESP32-C3)\n- OLED **SCL** -> XIAO **D5** (GPIO 7, the default SCL on XIAO ESP32-C3)\n\nMost 0.96\" SSD1306 modules answer at I2C address **0x3C**, but a meaningful minority ship at **0x3D** (sometimes selectable by a resistor jumper on the back of the board). The sketch tries 0x3C first, then 0x3D, and prints which one worked over Serial - so you don't have to guess.\n\nSoil sensor (analog - we read a voltage that changes with moisture):\n\n- Sensor **VCC** -> XIAO **D2** (GPIO 4) - **not** the 3.3V rail. The sketch turns this pin on for ~10 ms around each reading and off in between. Continuously powering a resistive probe electrolyses the metal prongs and they corrode in weeks; gating the supply slows that down dramatically.\n- Sensor **GND** -> blue rail\n- Sensor **AO** -> XIAO **D0** (GPIO 2, an ADC-capable pin)\n- The sensor's **DO** pin is left disconnected - that one is just an on/off threshold and we want the smooth analog value instead.\n- Most common 4-pin LM393 boards happily accept 3.3V on VCC, which is what D2 supplies. A rare variant has an on-board 3-pin regulator and insists on 5V - if your board's silkscreen says \"5V only\", wire VCC to the XIAO's **5V** pin instead and accept the corrosion trade-off (or use a transistor to gate the 5V line).\n\nRed LED (active-high - \"HIGH = on\"):\n\n- XIAO **D1** (GPIO 3) -> one leg of the 220R resistor\n- Other leg of the 220R -> long leg (anode, +) of the red LED\n- Short leg (cathode, -) of the LED -> blue rail (GND)\n- The LED legs and the resistor leads plug straight into the breadboard tie-points. No jumper wires for this part.\n\nSanity check before plugging in USB: LED short leg goes to GND, and there is exactly one resistor in series with the LED.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- Soil sensors work by measuring how easily electricity flows between the two prongs. Wet soil conducts; dry soil resists. The little board on the sensor converts that into a voltage on the AO pin. On the most common (LM393) boards a wet probe gives a **low** number and a dry probe gives a **high** number, but a few clones (and most capacitive replacements) flip that. The code has a `SENSOR_WET_IS_LOW` flag at the top - watch the Serial Monitor while you dunk the probe in water vs. hold it in air, and flip the flag if the relationship is reversed. The thresholds below it stay readable either way.\n- The ESP32-C3 ADC turns that voltage into a number between **0 and 4095** (12 bits). We map \"raw number\" -> \"happiness\" with two thresholds.\n- I2C is wonderful: SDA + SCL + power, and you can chain more sensors on the same two wires later (each with a unique address). Each device has its own address - that's why we needed to find the OLED at 0x3C or 0x3D.\n- The face is drawn with three primitives: two filled circles for eyes, and either the bottom half of a circle (smile), a straight line (sleepy), or the top half of a circle plus two tear drops (crying). The \"half a circle\" trick uses `drawCircleHelper`, wh","allocations":[{"item_name":"Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3","qty":1,"notes":"Microcontroller - reads soil ADC, drives OLED over I2C, blinks LED, gates sensor power on D2"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Pack used for XIAO-to-OLED and XIAO-to-sensor connections (not for the LED/resistor)"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"Thirsty-warning indicator; legs plug directly into breadboard tie points"},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1,"notes":"SSD1306 face display, I2C 0x3C or 0x3D (auto-detected)"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Solderless assembly; carries 3.3V and GND rails"},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current limit in series with the red LED; leads plug directly into breadboard"},{"item_name":"Soil Moisture Sensor","qty":1,"notes":"Two-prong probe; analog AO read by XIAO, VCC gated by GPIO to limit corrosion"}]},{"id":31,"name":"Earthquake Cam","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from laptop to the ESP32 (5V in). The ESP32's VIN pin re-exposes that 5V to power the MAX7219 matrix; the ESP32's onboard 3V3 regulator powers the SW-420 sensor and drives the GPIO logic.","description":"**What it is**: Thump the table next to it. A yellow lamp lights up while it counts your shakes, then a tiny 8x8 grid of red lights draws a bar graph showing how big your \"earthquake\" was, with a label that scrolls past like \"TINY TREMOR\" or \"GODZILLA\".\n\n**Why it's fun**: It is basically a Richter scale for whacking the desk. Take turns with your dad or your brother and see who can pound out the biggest quake without knocking the breadboard off the table.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU (the brain)\n- 1x SW-420 vibration switch module (the \"earthquake\" sensor)\n- 1x 8x8 LED matrix with MAX7219 driver board (the display)\n- 1x yellow LED (the warning lamp that lights while a quake is being measured)\n- 1x 220 ohm resistor (current-limit for the yellow LED)\n- 1x breadboard, ~12 DuPont jumpers\n- 1x USB cable (laptop to ESP32; this is also where the 5V for the matrix comes from)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nTwo power rails this time, not one. Bring the ESP32's `3V3` pin to one red breadboard strip, and the ESP32's `VIN` pin (which is the USB 5V passing through the board) to a *separate* red strip. Tie `GND` to the blue strip - both rails share ground.\n\n- **8x8 LED matrix (MAX7219):** `VCC` to the **5V / VIN** rail, NOT 3V3. The MAX7219 datasheet wants 4.0 to 5.5 V; running it on 3.3 V is folk wisdom and can brown out the ESP32's onboard regulator when the panel lights up. `GND` to GND. `DIN` to `GPIO 23` (MOSI), `CS` to `GPIO 5`, `CLK` to `GPIO 18` (SCK). The MAX7219 reads anything above ~2 V as HIGH, so the ESP32's 3.3 V logic signals drive it fine directly.\n- **SW-420 module:** `VCC` to **3V3**, `GND` to GND, `DO` (digital out) to ESP32 `GPIO 27`. The little blue trim-pot on the module sets the trigger threshold - if it triggers constantly even when still, turn that pot clockwise until the on-board \"trigger\" LED only blinks when you actually tap the board.\n- **Yellow LED:** long leg (anode) through the 220 ohm resistor to `GPIO 26`. Short leg (cathode) to GND. The resistor stops the LED from burning out.\n- Keep the SW-420 mounted firmly on the breadboard - if it dangles on jumper wires, the wires themselves will jiggle and trigger false quakes.\n\n## Safety notes\n\nNothing in this build runs above 5 V, so the mains-safety warnings that apply to the 2-channel relay project explicitly do NOT apply here. The only real hazards are: (a) backwards LED polarity will simply not light, no damage; (b) shorting 5V to GND on the breadboard will trip the laptop's USB port protection but not hurt the ESP32. Unplug USB before re-wiring anything.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The SW-420 isn't really a \"sensor\" in the analog sense - it's a tiny spring around a pin that briefly closes a circuit when shaken. So we don't measure shake *strength*, we count shake *pulses* per unit time. Lots of pulses in 10 seconds = bigger quake.\n- We use a hardware *interrupt* on the sensor pin. Interrupts let the chip drop whatever it's doing the instant a pulse arrives and bump a counter. That way we never miss a bump even while the matrix is busy drawing. The interrupt is armed only *after* the boot animation, so bumps during startup don't trick the board into thinking a quake started before you arrived.\n- The bar graph is drawn column by column. Eight columns, eight \"Richter\" levels. Each level also gets a label that scrolls across afterwards using a small built-in font.\n- 25 ms debounce: a single physical tap can ring the spring several times within a few milliseconds. We ignore extra pulses inside a 25 ms window so one tap counts as one bump.\n- We deliberately stick to the low-level `MD_MAX72XX` library and write the scrolling text by hand instead of mixing in the higher-level `MD_Parola`. Mixing the two on the same panel can cause one library's internal buffer to overwrite the other's drawing - confusing to debug as a beginner.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- If the matrix shows garbled, scrambled, mirrored or upside-down output, change `HARDWARE_TYPE` in the code. Try `GENERIC_HW`, then `PAROLA_HW`, then `ICSTATION_HW` until it looks right. This is the single biggest gotcha with cheap matrix modules.\n- Lower the SW-420 sensitivity pot (turn clockwise) to make it harder to trigger - then you have to really thump the table to reach \"GODZILLA\".\n- Add a second LED (red) for \"BIG ONE\" and above, so the warning lamp escalates.\n- Replace the verdict labels with funny ones aimed at Andy's son.\n- Once the Orange Pi is online, publish each quake's count + level to MQTT so t","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"Brain: reads vibration switch, drives the 8x8 matrix and warning LED"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":12,"notes":"ESP32-to-breadboard and breadboard-to-matrix connections"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Yellow","qty":1,"notes":"Warning lamp that lights while a 'quake' is being measured"},{"item_name":"8x8 LED Dot-matrix Display (red)","qty":1,"notes":"MAX7219-driven display; VCC must come from the 5V/VIN rail, not 3V3"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the sensor, LED, resistor and shared power/ground rails"},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current-limit for the yellow LED"},{"item_name":"SW-420 Vibration Switch Module","qty":1,"notes":"Tilt/shake sensor; pulses LOW when knocked"}]},{"id":32,"name":"Simon Says - 4-color memory game","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from laptop to the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (5V in via the board's USB port, 3.3V on the GPIO header)","description":"**What it is**: Four colored LEDs flash a pattern at you - red, yellow, green, blue. You press the buttons underneath in the exact same order. Get it right and the pattern grows by one. Get it wrong and the game is over.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It is the classic Simon Says toy, but YOU built it. Challenge your dad to see who can remember the longest sequence, and watch the flashes get faster and faster until your brain melts.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the brain - runs the state machine and the randomizer)\n- 1x Red, 1x Yellow, 1x Green, 1x Blue 5mm LED (the four \"Simon\" pads)\n- 4x 220 ohm resistors (one in series with each LED so the LED does not burn out)\n- 4x tactile push-buttons (one under each LED)\n- 1x breadboard, ~18 DuPont jumpers\n- USB cable from laptop to the ESP32 (also how power gets in)\n\nNo external pull-up resistors are needed because every button GPIO chosen here (32, 33, 13, 19) supports the ESP32's internal pull-up.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nLEDs (one per color, repeat the same pattern four times):\n\n- ESP32 GPIO -> 220 ohm resistor -> LED anode (long leg)\n- LED cathode (short leg, with the flat side) -> breadboard GND rail\n- GPIOs used: Red = 25, Yellow = 26, Green = 27, Blue = 14\n\nButtons (also one per color):\n\n- One leg of the button -> an ESP32 GPIO\n- The opposite leg -> breadboard GND rail\n- No external pull-up resistor needed - the sketch turns on the ESP32's internal pull-up, so the GPIO sits HIGH at rest and reads LOW when pressed\n- GPIOs used: Red = 32, Yellow = 33, Green = 13, Blue = 19\n\nPower rails:\n\n- ESP32 `GND` -> breadboard \"-\" rail. All four LED cathodes and all four button ground legs come here.\n- The `3V3` pin is not needed for this project - every GPIO already provides its own pull-up internally.\n\nPin notes (worth knowing):\n\n- GPIO 34, 35, 36, 39 are INPUT-ONLY on the ESP32 and have NO internal pull-up. They look tempting because there are four of them, but a button wired only between an input-only pin and GND will float when released and give random readings. If you ever want to use one anyway, add an external 10 k ohm pull-up resistor from the pin to 3V3.\n- Avoid GPIO 0, 12, 15 for buttons - they are boot-strapping pins and can stop the board from starting if held LOW or HIGH at power-on.\n- GPIO 2 is the on-board blue user LED on this board. Do not reuse it for a button - the LED and the button would fight each other.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The \"memory\" of the game is just an array of numbers. Each new round appends a fresh random value (0-3) and replays the whole array from the start.\n- Debouncing: when you press a tactile button the metal contacts bounce for a few milliseconds, so the GPIO sees several rapid LOW/HIGH/LOW transitions. The sketch reads the pin, waits a tiny moment (`DEBOUNCE_MS`), and reads again to confirm.\n- `INPUT_PULLUP` flips your intuition: pressed = LOW, not pressed = HIGH. That is because the internal resistor pulls the pin to 3.3 V by default, and the button shorts it to GND when you press it. This is why pin choice matters - GPIOs without a built-in pull-up have nothing to flip back to HIGH.\n- The speed-up: every successful round trims the gap between LED flashes. That is the difficulty curve, baked into one variable.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add a tiny passive buzzer on another GPIO and play a different tone per colour (real Simon does this).\n- Save the high score to flash with the `Preferences` library so it survives a reset.\n- Add a 2-player mode: alternate who has to repeat the sequence.\n- Replace the four discrete LEDs with a single WS2812 strip and use colour directly.\n- Make it WiFi-aware and POST the high score to the Orange Pi hub (once the Pi is online).\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nPure software project - no sensors, no analog, no I2C. Everything interesting happens in the code: the array, the random call, the loop that plays it back, the loop that reads the player. It is the perfect introduction to a finite state machine (SHOW_SEQUENCE -> WAIT_FOR_PLAYER -> CHECK -> WIN/LOSE) without any new electrical concepts. And the game is genuinely fun, which keeps a 10-year-old engaged long enough to ask \"but how does it remember?\" - which is exactly the question worth answering.","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"Main controller running the game state machine"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":18,"notes":"ESP32 to breadboard wiring (4 LED GPIOs, 4 button GPIOs, GND from ESP32, plus short jumpers between rails and component legs)"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Blue","qty":1,"notes":"Color 4 in the sequence, driven by GPIO 14"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1,"notes":"Color 3 in the sequence, driven by GPIO 27"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"Color 1 in the sequence, driven by GPIO 25"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Yellow","qty":1,"notes":"Color 2 in the sequence, driven by GPIO 26"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the LEDs, buttons and resistors"},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":4,"notes":"Current-limiting resistor, one per LED"},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x3.1mm (4-pin, black/red)","qty":4,"notes":"One per color. All four sit on pull-up-capable GPIOs (32, 33, 13, 19) and use the ESP32's internal pull-ups, so no external pull-up resistors are required."}]},{"id":33,"name":"Stopwatch + Lap Memory on Serial Monitor","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from laptop to the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (5V in via USB, the onboard regulator makes 3.3V for the chip; no external parts so nothing else needs feeding).","description":"**What it is**: Plug the ESP32 into your laptop and open the Serial Monitor. Tap the BOOT button to start a stopwatch, tap again to stop, hold it down to save a lap time, and a tiny blue light blinks once a second while the clock is running.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Race your dad down the hallway, time how long you can hold your breath, or see who can stack ten cups the fastest, and every lap gets saved so you can compare runs. One little button does three different jobs depending on how you press it, which feels a bit like a secret code your fingers know.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the entire project - nothing else needed)\n- 1x USB-C cable from your laptop\n\nThat is the whole shopping list. This version of the project was deliberately re-scoped so it can be built with **only the hardware already in Andy's kit**. An earlier draft used a TM1637 4-digit display, an SSD1306 OLED, two tactile push-buttons, a breadboard, and jumpers - none of which are in the kit yet. When those parts arrive, see \"Things to change once it works\" for the upgrade path.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThere is no wiring. Plug the USB-C cable from your laptop into the ESP32. That is it. The two pieces of \"hardware UI\" we use are both already on the board:\n\n- **BOOT button** (next to the USB-C jack). It is wired to GPIO 0 with a built-in pull-up. Pressed = LOW, released = HIGH. We use it as our one user button.\n- **Built-in blue LED** on GPIO 2 (the small LED next to the WiFi shield, not the red power LED). It blinks once a second while the stopwatch is running and is off when stopped, so you can see the state at a glance without looking at the screen.\n\nThe \"display\" is the Arduino IDE Serial Monitor at 115200 baud. Open it with `Tools > Serial Monitor` (or Ctrl+Shift+M) after upload.\n\n**Do not press the EN button while timing** - EN is a hardware reset, it reboots the chip and you lose the run. Only BOOT is safe to press.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **Why `millis()` instead of `delay()`.** `delay(1000)` would freeze the chip for a whole second, during which it cannot read the button or update the LED. `millis()` just asks \"how many milliseconds since boot?\" without stopping anything, so the same `loop()` can poll the button, blink the LED, and print the time all at once.\n- **One button, three actions, via a small state machine.** A short tap (< 600 ms) toggles start/stop. A long press (>= 600 ms while held) records a lap if running, or resets everything if stopped. The function `readButton()` measures how long the button was held and reports a `SHORT_PRESS` or `LONG_PRESS` event. The `loop()` then decides what that event means based on whether the stopwatch is currently running. That separation - \"low-level: was it short or long?\" vs. \"high-level: what does that mean right now?\" - is the core idea of a state machine, and it shows up in almost every embedded project later.\n- **Active-LOW buttons.** When BOOT is *pressed*, GPIO 0 reads `LOW` (0V), not `HIGH`. That is because the pin is pulled up to 3.3V by default, and pressing the button shorts it to ground. This is the single most common thing beginners get backwards, so it is worth re-reading: pressed = LOW.\n- **Debouncing.** Real metal contacts bounce for a few milliseconds when you press them - the pin flickers HIGH/LOW/HIGH/LOW before settling. `readButton()` ignores any change that happens within 25 ms of the last change, which filters that out so one press is one event.\n- **Why GPIO 0 is \"free\" to use as a button at runtime.** GPIO 0 is sampled at *boot* to decide between normal-run mode and flash-upload mode (that is why you sometimes have to hold BOOT during upload). Once the sketch is running, GPIO 0 is just a regular input - so we can re-use the BOOT button as our user button without any extra hardware.\n- **Why the built-in LED is GPIO 2 on this board.** A few clones put it on GPIO 5 or GPIO 22 instead. If the LED never blinks, change `PIN_LED` to 5 or 22 - the rest of the sketch will still work.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- **Upgrade to a real display.** When a TM1637 4-digit display arrives, replace the `Serial.print` calls in `showBigTime()` with `TM1637Display::setSegments()` and keep everything else identical. Add an SSD1306 OLED for the lap list. (That is the original \"big stopwatch face + scrolling lap history\" project, deferred until the parts are in hand.)\n- **Add a buzzer.** When the relay board is wired up to switch a piezo, beep once per lap so Andy's son can time himself wit","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"The whole project runs on this single board: BOOT button is the user input, built-in LED on GPIO 2 is the running heartbeat, Serial Monitor is the display."}]},{"id":34,"name":"Family Doorbell Web Page","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from a laptop or USB wall charger into the ESP32's USB-C port (5V, the board's onboard regulator drops it to 3.3V for the chip)","description":"**What it is**: Press the button on the breadboard. Every phone in the house that has the web page open goes \"Ding!\" and the counter jumps up by one.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It is a real doorbell you built yourself, and Dad's phone buzzes the second you push the button from the other room. Sneak up, mash it five times, and watch every screen in the house freak out at once.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the brain - hosts the web page and watches the button)\n- 1x push button (the doorbell itself)\n- 1x breadboard (holds the button without soldering)\n- 4x DuPont jumper wires (one to GPIO 4, one to GND, two spare)\n- 1x USB cable (powers the ESP32 from a laptop or USB wall charger)\n\nNo resistor is needed for the button: we turn on the ESP32's internal pull-up resistor in code (`INPUT_PULLUP`). The LED that lights up on each press is the blue one already built into the NodeMCU board, so it has its own resistor on the PCB.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe button has 4 legs but they are wired in pairs internally. Sit the button across the breadboard's centre gap. Use diagonally opposite legs - that way you can't accidentally pick two legs that are already shorted together.\n\n- One leg of the button -> ESP32 `GPIO 4`\n- Diagonally opposite leg of the button -> ESP32 `GND`\n\nThat's all the wiring. When the button is open, GPIO 4 reads HIGH (pulled up internally to 3.3V). When pressed, GPIO 4 is shorted to GND and reads LOW. The code watches for that transition.\n\nPower comes from the USB cable on the ESP32 - no separate power supply, no battery, nothing to plug into mains.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The button is just a switch. No \"smartness\" in it. The ESP32 is what turns \"switch closed\" into \"tell every phone in the house\".\n- The web page doesn't get pushed to; it asks the ESP32 every second \"has the count gone up?\". That's called polling. It's the simplest approach that works on every phone browser without extra libraries.\n- We count rings instead of just storing \"is ringing\". A counter means two phones loading the page at slightly different times still both notice the same press.\n- Debouncing: a physical button's metal contacts bounce when they touch, so one press can look like 3-5 presses electrically. The 400 ms timer in the code ignores those echoes.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Make the page beep or vibrate the phone (a tiny bit of Web Audio API or `navigator.vibrate`).\n- Add a mDNS name (`ESP32.local`) so you don't have to memorise the IP.\n- Push instead of poll: switch to Server-Sent Events (`/events` endpoint) so the page updates instantly with no 1-second lag.\n- Log the last 10 rings with timestamps so you can see who rang while you were out.\n- Move from the breadboard button to a real doorbell button by the front door (still 2 wires).\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nThis is the smallest possible \"real\" IoT loop: a thing in the physical world (a button) makes a thing on a screen change (a web page), with nothing in between except WiFi. Andy gets to feel the whole shape of an event-driven system in one afternoon - sensor -> microcontroller -> network -> UI - and his son gets immediate, obvious feedback (Dad's phone goes \"Ding!\" when he pushes the button). It's also the natural feeder project for the Orange Pi hub later: replace the polling page with an MQTT publish and the doorbell becomes one of many sensors on the home bus.","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"WiFi microcontroller hosting the web server and watching the button"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":4,"notes":"Two for the button (signal + ground), spares for power rails"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Breadboard for the doorbell button wiring"},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x3.1mm (4-pin, black/red)","qty":1,"notes":"Physical doorbell trigger pressed by the visitor"}]},{"id":35,"name":"Garage Light Web Switch (with Relay)","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB-A to USB-C cable into the NodeMCU-32S (5V, also feeds the relay coil via the JDVcc-bridged jumper), plus a separate 12V DC barrel-jack supply for the LED strip on the switched side of the relay.","description":"**What it is**: Open a web page on your phone and tap a big button. CLICK - a light across the room turns on. Tap it again, CLICK, and the light turns off.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Your phone is now a magic wand for a real light, and you can hear the little click every time you tap. Show your friends - they will think you wired up the whole house.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the brains and web server)\n- 2-ch Songle relay board (only channel 1 is used here)\n- 12V LED strip, short piece, or any 12V low-voltage lamp\n- 12V DC power supply for the LED strip (separate from the ESP32 USB power)\n- Breadboard (holds the ESP32 only)\n- DuPont jumper wires (M-F from ESP32 header to relay header, M-M for the 12V load side)\n- USB-A to USB-C cable (the NodeMCU-32S has a USB-C connector)\n\nHeads-up: only the ESP32 and the relay board are confirmed on hand from the kit list in CLAUDE.md. The 12V strip, 12V supply, breadboard, jumpers, and USB-C cable are not listed there. Confirm these are actually in the box before starting, or we will be stuck halfway through wiring.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nTwo power worlds meet in this project. Keep them straight.\n\nESP32 side (5V from USB):\n\n- ESP32 `5V` pin -> relay header `VCC`\n- ESP32 `GND` -> relay header `GND`\n- ESP32 `GPIO 26` -> relay header `IN1`\n\nThe breadboard is just a perch for the ESP32 and a convenient place to start the three jumpers that run to the relay header. The relay board already has its own logic pin header, so nothing else is wired on the breadboard rails.\n\nThe relay's JDVcc jumper stays in its default position (bridged to Vcc), so the relay coil is powered from the ESP32's 5V USB rail. This is the simplest wiring and fine for a small DC load.\n\n12V side (the load being switched):\n\n- 12V supply `+` -> relay channel 1 `COM` screw terminal\n- Relay channel 1 `NO` screw terminal -> LED strip `+`\n- LED strip `-` -> 12V supply `-`\n\nThe 12V supply ground does NOT need to touch the ESP32 ground. That is the whole point of the relay: the high-side wire is broken and remade by a mechanical contact, with no electrical connection back to the ESP32.\n\nActive-LOW gotcha: the Songle board energizes the coil when IN1 is pulled LOW. The code drives the pin HIGH before calling `pinMode(..., OUTPUT)` so the relay does not click ON during boot.\n\n3.3V trigger caveat (per CLAUDE.md, `Relay_2CH_Songle/` section): most Songle clones will trigger fine off a 3.3V ESP32 GPIO because the optocoupler LED only needs a small forward current. A few stubborn ones really do want a 5V swing - if relay 1 clicks unreliably, only sometimes, or only after the board warms up, that is the first thing to suspect. Workaround is a tiny NPN transistor or a level-shifter feeding IN1; ask before reaching for those, it is rarely needed.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The relay is a remote-controlled light switch. The ESP32 cannot handle 12V directly, so it tells a tiny electromagnet (the relay coil) to physically push two metal contacts together.\n- Hear the click? That is the contacts closing. That click is also why you should not switch a relay thousands of times a second.\n- The web page is just HTML the ESP32 builds as a string and sends back when your phone asks for it. The big button is a link to `/on` or `/off`. The ESP32 sees the request, flips the pin, and redirects you back to `/` so the page updates.\n- `digitalWrite(pin, LOW)` turns the light ON here. That feels backwards, and it is. Most opto-isolated relay boards use \"low-level trigger\".\n- Because the button is a plain link with a 303 redirect, every tap reloads the whole page. That is fine for learning, but it will feel a little sluggish on a phone - half a second of white flash between taps. The first upgrade below fixes that.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Replace the link-style buttons with a `fetch()` call so the page does not fully reload on every tap (the current 303-redirect design forces a full reload, which is why it feels slow).\n- Add channel 2 with a second button for a second light.\n- Add a `/status` endpoint that returns JSON so a phone app or Home Assistant can poll it.\n- Once the Orange Pi hub is up, switch from \"web page\" to MQTT: ESP32 subscribes to `home/garage/light/set`, publishes state to `home/garage/light/state`.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nThis is the first project where a tap on a phone moves a thing in the real world. Everything before this was the ESP32 talking to its own onboard LED. Now there is a 12V load, a separate power supply, and a","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"WiFi web server and relay driver"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"ESP32 to relay header, plus relay COM/NO to LED strip (CONFIRM ON HAND - not in CLAUDE.md kit list)"},{"item_name":"2-channel Songle relay module","qty":1,"notes":"Switches the 12V LED strip; only channel 1 used. JDVcc jumper stays bridged."},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the ESP32 and gives tie-points for the three jumpers running to the relay header (CONFIRM ON HAND - not in CLAUDE.md kit list)"}]},{"id":36,"name":"WiFi Signal Strength Meter on OLED","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB-C cable from a power bank (user-supplied) to the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S USB-C port (5V). The board powers the OLED off its 3.3V rail.","description":"**What it is**: Plug it in and a tiny screen lights up with a bar that grows and shrinks as you walk around the house. The fatter the bar, the stronger the WiFi where you're standing.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You become the WiFi detective. Hunt down the dead zones, find the magic spot in the back bedroom where the signal finally comes back, and prove to your dad once and for all which room has the worst internet.\n\n## Parts list\n\nFrom Andy's inventory:\n\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the \"brain\" - reads WiFi signal strength and drives the screen)\n- 1x 0.96\" OLED Display (I2C) - SSD1306, 128x64 monochrome (the little screen that shows the bar graph)\n- 1x Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points) (for tidy wiring without solder)\n- 4x Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc (we use 4 of the 40-pack: VCC, GND, SDA, SCL between ESP32 and OLED)\n\nNot in the inventory - grab from around the house:\n\n- USB power bank (any 5V \"phone charger\" pack with a USB output)\n- USB-C cable to go from the power bank to the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S's USB-C port\n\nThese two are treated as user-supplied because they aren't catalogued in the electronics stock - any spare phone-charging gear will do.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe OLED is an \"I2C\" device, which means it talks to the ESP32 over just two signal wires plus power and ground - four wires total. I2C is great because you can hang several different I2C devices off the same two pins later without needing more GPIO.\n\n- OLED `VCC` -> ESP32 `3V3` pin (the OLED is happy with 3.3V; do NOT use the 5V pin or the screen can be damaged)\n- OLED `GND` -> ESP32 `GND` pin\n- OLED `SDA` -> ESP32 `GPIO 21` (the default I2C data pin on the ESP32)\n- OLED `SCL` -> ESP32 `GPIO 22` (the default I2C clock pin on the ESP32)\n\nPlug both modules into the breadboard so nothing is dangling, then run the four jumpers between them. When you finish testing on the laptop, unplug the USB cable and move it to the power bank - everything keeps running because the ESP32 is happy with any 5V USB source.\n\nBefore uploading: open `WiFiRSSIMeter.ino` and replace `PASSWORD_HERE` with the real GeeFam WiFi password. Per the project convention, credentials live inside the sketch (not in any CLAUDE.md or commit).\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **RSSI** (Received Signal Strength Indicator) is just a number in dBm that the WiFi chip reports for the access point it's connected to. -30 dBm is \"I'm sitting on the router\", -90 dBm is \"I can barely hear it\". -67 dBm or better is fine for streaming video, worse than -75 dBm is where things get flaky.\n- The bar graph is just a filled rectangle whose width is mapped from the RSSI range onto the screen width. It's the same idea as a volume bar in any music app.\n- \"I2C\" uses only two signal wires (SDA = data, SCL = clock) and an address to pick which device on the bus to talk to. The OLED's address is almost always `0x3C`.\n- Because the ESP32 reconnects to WiFi automatically, you can keep walking around even if the signal drops out briefly - the meter will recover on its own.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add a peak-hold marker: a thin vertical line that remembers the strongest reading you've seen in this room.\n- Show the SSID name and the IP address as small text above the bar, so the kids can confirm which network they're measuring.\n- Log readings to SD or send them over MQTT to the (future) Orange Pi hub so you can make a heat-map of the house.\n- Add a buzzer that beeps faster when signal gets weaker - a \"Geiger counter\" mode for finding the strongest spot.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nThis is the first project here that does something genuinely useful around the house: it answers the real question \"why is the WiFi bad in the back bedroom?\". It also introduces two big concepts in one go - reading a real-world radio measurement (RSSI) and driving a display over I2C - without needing any sensors or extra parts beyond a single screen. Once the OLED is working, every future project that needs a tiny readout (temperature, sensor values, MQTT status from the Pi) reuses exactly the same wiring and code pattern.","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"Reads WiFi RSSI and drives the OLED over I2C"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":4,"notes":"Four wires from the 40pc pack: VCC, GND, SDA, SCL between ESP32 and OLED"},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1,"notes":"SSD1306 128x64; shows numeric RSSI, quality label, and live bar graph"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds ESP32 and OLED side by side, no solder"}]},{"id":37,"name":"Distance-Triggered Treat Dispenser","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from a 5V/2A phone-style charger (or laptop port) into the ESP32 NodeMCU; VIN/5V feeds the HC-SR04 and SG90 with a 470 uF cap across the servo rail, and the HC-SR04 ECHO line is divided 1k/2k down to 3.3V before reaching the ESP32 GPIO.","description":"**What it is**: Hold your hand over the box. A motor spins a little cardboard wheel and exactly one M&M drops into your palm. A small red display ticks up so you can see how many treats it has handed out today.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You built a candy machine that obeys your hand like magic, with no button to press. Set a daily limit, race your brother to trigger it the fastest, or aim it at the dog and watch his brain explode.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- ESP32 NodeMCU (the brain - reads sensor, drives servo, updates display)\n- HC-SR04 ultrasonic distance module (looks straight up for your hand)\n- SG90 micro-servo (rotates the cardboard disk that drops one M&M)\n- TM1637 4-digit display (shows the running count of treats dispensed)\n- 1k and 2k resistors (voltage divider on the HC-SR04 ECHO line - see Wiring notes)\n- 470 uF electrolytic capacitor (across the servo's 5V/GND, prevents brownout resets)\n- Breadboard + male-to-male DuPont jumpers\n- USB cable from a 5V/2A phone-style charger (preferred) or a laptop port for power\n\nYou will also build a tiny cardboard chassis: a tube (like an empty toilet-roll tube) holds the M&Ms, a flat cardboard disk on the servo horn has one M&M-sized slot cut out of it. As the servo turns from 0 deg to 90 deg the slot lines up with a hole in the chute and exactly one M&M drops out. This is the fun part - it will probably take three tries to get the disk geometry right.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n- The HC-SR04 needs 5V on Vcc and its ECHO pin outputs 5V logic. The ESP32's GPIOs are 3.3V (not 5V tolerant on the datasheet), so we drop ECHO with a simple 1k / 2k divider: ECHO -> 1k -> GPIO 18, and GPIO 18 -> 2k -> GND. That gives 5 V x 2k / (1k + 2k) = ~3.3 V at the GPIO. TRIG goes the other way (ESP32 out to sensor in) and a 3.3 V high is enough to trigger the HC-SR04, so no divider needed there.\n- The SG90 servo also wants 5V. Both 5V devices share the ESP32's VIN/5V pin (which on the NodeMCU board comes straight from the USB connector). The servo can briefly draw 500-700 mA when it stalls, and that dip on the shared rail can reset the ESP32. We park a 470 uF electrolytic capacitor right across the servo's 5V and GND on the breadboard rail to absorb those dips. Mind the polarity: the striped leg is the negative side and goes to GND. Use a 5V/2A USB charger rather than a laptop port if you can.\n- The TM1637 display is happy on 3.3V or 5V; we use 3.3V because the data lines are then natively at ESP32 logic level.\n- All grounds tie together on the breadboard's blue rail. This is essential - if the sensor's ground floats, distance readings will be garbage.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- \"What is an ultrasonic sensor?\" It chirps a sound too high-pitched for us to hear, then times how long the echo takes to come back. Sound travels ~343 m/s, so the code multiplies microseconds by 0.0343 cm/us and divides by 2 (because the chirp goes there and back).\n- \"Why does it only dispense one M&M, not a stream?\" Look at the `armed` variable. We disarm as soon as a hand is detected and only re-arm once the hand has moved back past 20 cm. That hysteresis pattern shows up everywhere in electronics - thermostats, button debouncing, you name it.\n- \"Why a slotted disk instead of just a flap?\" A flap can let two M&Ms through if you're unlucky. A slot exactly one M&M deep is mechanically self-limiting - this is called a \"rotary feeder\" and is how real candy machines work.\n- \"Why two resistors on the ECHO wire?\" The sensor speaks at 5 V but the ESP32 listens at 3.3 V. The two resistors form a divider that quietly drops the loud 5 V down to a polite 3.3 V before it reaches the chip - cheap insurance against frying a GPIO.\n- \"Why a fat capacitor next to the servo?\" Motors are greedy in short bursts. The cap is a tiny local battery that hands current to the servo during those bursts so the ESP32 next door does not see its supply sag and reboot.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add a tiny buzzer (active-high) that beeps once per dispense.\n- Display the time-since-last-treat instead of the count, so you can compete with your son for \"fastest reaction\".\n- Publish the count over MQTT to the Orange Pi (once it's booted) so a Grafana dashboard tracks daily M&M consumption.\n- Replace the cardboard disk with a 3D-printed one - same code, much more reliable feed.\n- Add a long-press boot button check that resets the counter to zero.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nThis is the first project in the kit that combines a sensor (input), an actuator (output), and","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"Main controller - reads ultrasonic distance, drives servo, updates display"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":12,"notes":"Jumpers between ESP32, sensor, servo, display, and the divider/cap on the breadboard"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 470uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1,"notes":"Electrolytic across the servo 5V/GND rail to absorb stall-current dips and prevent ESP32 brownout resets"},{"item_name":"4-digit 7-segment Display Module","qty":1,"notes":"TM1637 4-digit display showing the running count of treats dispensed"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Hosts the ESP32, the ECHO divider, the bulk cap, and shared 5V/GND rails"},{"item_name":"SG90 Servo (9g micro)","qty":1,"notes":"Rotates the slotted cardboard disk to drop one M&M"},{"item_name":"Resistor 1K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Top half of the ECHO voltage divider (HC-SR04 5V -> ESP32 3.3V GPIO)"},{"item_name":"Resistor 2.2K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Bottom half of the ECHO voltage divider to GND"},{"item_name":"HC-SR04 Ultrasonic Distance Sensor","qty":1,"notes":"Detects hand at ~10 cm above the dispenser"}]},{"id":38,"name":"Mailbox-Opened Pinger","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"18650 cell, charged by a TP4056 charge-only module; cell B+ goes straight to the XIAO BAT pad (3.7V, no boost stage), B- to XIAO GND","description":"**What it is**: Hide a tiny box inside your mailbox. The second the mail carrier lifts the flap, an LED inside your house blinks to tell you the mail just arrived. No more guessing, no more checking an empty mailbox five times a day.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You built a real-life notification, like a phone alert but made out of parts on your desk. The best part: the gadget sleeps for weeks on one battery and wakes up the instant something happens, like a little robot guard dog for your mail.\n\nA battery-powered XIAO ESP32-C3 sits inside the mailbox, asleep almost all the time. When the mail carrier lifts the flap, a tilt switch closes, pulls a GPIO pin HIGH, and the C3 wakes from deep sleep. It connects to WiFi, fires off an HTTP ping, blinks the indoor LED, then goes back to sleep. With the boost stage removed and the 18650 wired straight to the XIAO's battery pad, a single cell should run the project for a long time - though see the honest battery-life note below.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x XIAO ESP32-C3 (the sleeper)\n- 1x bare 2-pin Angle Tilt Switch (a tube with a metal ball that bridges two contacts when tilted - no electronics inside)\n- 1x 18650 cell + TP4056 charge-only module (power)\n- 1x 100k resistor - external pull-down on the wake pin AND the R of an RC debounce filter\n- 1x 100nF ceramic cap - the C of the same RC debounce filter\n- 1x Blue LED + 1x 100R resistor (visible \"I woke up\" indicator)\n- 1x Breadboard, ~8x M-M DuPont jumpers\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n**Power path (no boost stage - this matters for battery life):**\n\n- USB-C into the TP4056's input only when you want to charge the cell.\n- 18650 cell into the TP4056's B+ / B- pads.\n- TP4056 OUT+ goes to the XIAO's BAT pad (on the underside of the board - Seeed exposes it specifically for this use case). OUT- goes to XIAO GND.\n- The XIAO's own LDO runs directly from the 3.7V cell - no boost-to-5V-then-back-down-to-3.3V round trip, which was costing milliamps of quiescent current 24/7 in the earlier draft.\n\n**Tilt switch wiring (bare 2-pin switch + external pull-down + RC debounce):**\n\n- One leg of the tilt switch to 3V3 on the XIAO.\n- Other leg to GPIO2 (D0) AND to one leg of the 100nF cap AND to one leg of the 100k resistor.\n- Other leg of the 100nF cap to GND.\n- Other leg of the 100k resistor to GND.\n\nThat little RC network does two jobs at once:\n- The 100k pulls GPIO2 firmly LOW whenever the switch is open (including during deep sleep, when the chip's own internal pull-downs are off).\n- The 100k + 100nF together form a low-pass filter with a ~10ms time constant, which absorbs the contact bounce of the ball rolling around in the tube.\n\n**LED:**\n\n- LED long leg (anode) to XIAO D1 (GPIO3) through the 100R resistor.\n- LED short leg (cathode) to XIAO GND.\n- (220R was too large here. Blue LEDs have a forward voltage around 3.0-3.2V, so on a 3.3V GPIO the resistor sees only ~0.1-0.3V of headroom. 100R gives a few mA of forward current - bright enough to see across the room. If yours still looks dim, a direct connection through a 47R will work too; the GPIO source limit (40 mA absolute max) is what protects it at that point.)\n\n**Why GPIO2 specifically, and the strapping-pin warning:**\n\nOn the ESP32-C3, GPIO2 is one of the pins that can wake the chip from deep sleep via the GPIO wake source. It is ALSO a strapping pin: its level at the moment of reset/power-on selects boot mode. The design here is safe because the tilt switch idles OPEN (so the 100k pulls GPIO2 LOW = normal boot) when the mailbox flap is closed. If you ever wire / mount the switch so its idle position is CLOSED (GPIO2 HIGH at power-on), the chip can land in download mode on power-up and look bricked - so mount the switch so \"flap closed\" = \"switch open\".\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **Deep sleep is the headline feature.** A XIAO running normally pulls ~80 mA with WiFi on. In deep sleep the chip itself drops to roughly 40-150 microamps.\n- **But \"weeks to months\" needs an honest asterisk.** The boost-converter round-trip in the earlier draft was leaking 1-10 mA all the time, which would have dominated the chip's own 43 uA and killed the cell in days. Going straight from the 18650 to the XIAO BAT pad removes that loss. The remaining drains are the TP4056 (~20 uA when idle, no USB plugged in), the chip itself in deep sleep (~40 uA), and the 100k pull-down (only conducts while the switch is closed). So the realistic budget is in the tens-of-microamps range - genuinely weeks of life - PROVIDED the tilt switch doesn't bounce","allocations":[{"item_name":"Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3","qty":1,"notes":"Microcontroller - sleeps until tilt switch pulls GPIO2 HIGH"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":8,"notes":"Jumpers between XIAO, tilt switch, LED, RC filter, and TP4056"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Blue","qty":1,"notes":"Indoor 'mail arrived' indicator"},{"item_name":"18650 Charger / Protection Board (TP4056, USB-C)","qty":1,"notes":"Charge-only board: USB in, 18650 in, B+/B- straight to the XIAO BAT pad"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the LED, both resistors, the cap, and routes the tilt switch wires"},{"item_name":"Resistor 100K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"External pulldown on GPIO2 - REQUIRED because the C3 internal pulldown is OFF during deep sleep; also the R in the RC debounce filter"},{"item_name":"Resistor 100R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current-limiting resistor for the blue LED (small Vf headroom on 3.3V)"},{"item_name":"Angle / Tilt Switch (ball)","qty":1,"notes":"Bare 2-pin ball-bearing switch mounted on the mailbox flap"}]},{"id":39,"name":"OLED Spirit Level","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"USB-C cable from laptop to the XIAO ESP32-C3 (5V in, 3.3V out on the 3V3 pin powers the IMU and OLED)","description":"**What it is**: Tilt the breadboard and a little bubble slides around on the OLED screen, just like the bubble in a real spirit level at the hardware store. When the board is perfectly flat, the bubble lands in the center and a green LED lights up to tell you \"you're level.\"\n\n**Why it's fun**: It is a real tool you can carry around the house and use to check if shelves, picture frames, or the kitchen table are crooked. Your dad probably owns the boring plastic version, but yours has a glowing screen and a light that congratulates you when you nail it.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- XIAO ESP32-C3 (3.3 V logic, USB-C powered)\n- MPU-6500 IMU breakout (I2C, address 0x68)\n- 0.96\" SSD1306 OLED, 128x64, I2C (address 0x3C)\n- Green LED + 220R current-limiting resistor\n- Breadboard and a handful of DuPont jumpers\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThis project is the first time we put two devices on the same I2C bus. I2C is designed for exactly that: every device listens on the same two wires (SDA = data, SCL = clock) and is told apart by its address. The OLED answers to 0x3C and the MPU-6500 answers to 0x68, so there is no conflict.\n\n- Both the IMU and the OLED get **3.3 V from the XIAO's 3V3 pin**, NOT 5 V. The XIAO's GPIOs are 3.3 V tolerant only; feeding a 5 V I2C device into them can slowly damage the chip.\n- Tie all grounds together on the breadboard's blue rail.\n- XIAO `SDA` (D4 / GPIO6) goes to BOTH the IMU SDA pin and the OLED SDA pin.\n- XIAO `SCL` (D5 / GPIO7) goes to BOTH the IMU SCL pin and the OLED SCL pin.\n- The MPU-6500 and OLED breakouts already have pull-up resistors on SDA/SCL on the board, so no extra pull-ups are needed.\n- Green LED long leg (anode) to GPIO D2 (GPIO4) through the 220R resistor. Short leg (cathode) to GND.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **Why two addresses work on one bus:** I2C is like a party line. The XIAO shouts \"0x3C, are you there?\" and only the OLED answers. Then \"0x68, what's your acceleration?\" and only the IMU answers. The clock pin keeps everyone in step.\n- **Tilt from gravity:** when the board sits flat, gravity pulls straight down through the Z axis of the IMU and X / Y read ~0. Tip the breadboard and some of that 1 g leaks into X or Y. `atan2(ax, az)` and `atan2(ay, az)` give us the tilt angles in radians, which we convert to degrees.\n- **Why the bubble goes to the high side:** in a real spirit level the bubble floats to the high end because it is less dense than the liquid. We fake that by drawing the bubble offset in the same direction as the tilt (positive tilt -> bubble offset positive on screen).\n- **Why the LED has a deadband:** raw accel data jitters by a fraction of a degree even when still. If we lit the LED at \"exactly 0.0 degrees\" it would flicker. We light it only when both pitch and roll are within +/-1.0 degrees, and turn it off only above +/-1.5 degrees. That gap is called hysteresis.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add a calibration step: average 200 samples at boot and treat that as the new \"zero\" so the level reads true even if the IMU is glued in slightly crooked.\n- Show the numeric angles in the corners of the OLED.\n- Add a buzzer that beeps faster as you approach level (audible spirit level for when you can't see the screen).\n- Use the gyro as well as the accelerometer (complementary filter) so it stops jiggling when you tap the bench.\n- Make it battery-powered with a small LiPo on the XIAO's BAT pad and stick it in a 3D-printed case.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nIt is a real household tool you can hand to someone and they immediately understand what it does. Under the hood it teaches the three ideas you need for every future IMU project: sharing the I2C bus, turning raw accelerometer numbers into a physical angle, and smoothing noisy data so an output (the LED) doesn't chatter. From here, the same pattern scales up to a self-balancing robot, a step counter, or an orientation-aware game controller.","allocations":[{"item_name":"Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3","qty":1,"notes":"I2C master, runs the tilt math and drives the OLED + LED"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":10,"notes":"Power rails, shared SDA/SCL bus, LED leg to GPIO"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1,"notes":"Latches on when the board is within the level deadband"},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1,"notes":"SSD1306 128x64 display showing the live bubble (I2C addr 0x3C)"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the XIAO, IMU, OLED and LED; shares the I2C bus on the rails"},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current-limiting resistor in series with the green LED"},{"item_name":"MPU-6500 6-Axis IMU (GY-6500)","qty":1,"notes":"Accelerometer source for the gravity vector (I2C addr 0x68)"}]},{"id":40,"name":"Two-ESP32 Walkie-Talkie Lights","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"Two USB-C cables, one to each board (5V each) - the XIAO and the NodeMCU each get their own power from a laptop, phone charger, or USB hub.","description":"**What it is**: Two tiny boards on opposite sides of the room, no wires between them. Push the button on one and the red LED on the other one blinks on. Push it again, it blinks off.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It feels like magic the first time. You stand in the kitchen, your dad's in the living room, you press the button, and his light flips on. That's the same trick every smart light switch in the world uses, and you just built one.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3 (the \"remote\" - has the button)\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the \"lamp\" - has the external LED)\n- 1x Tactile push-button (wired to the XIAO)\n- 1x Red LED (wired to the NodeMCU)\n- 1x 220R resistor (current-limit for the LED)\n- 1x Breadboard (everything plugs into this, no soldering)\n- Jumper wires (M-M for breadboard rails, F-M for board pins)\n- Two USB-C cables (one per board)\n- Existing GeeFam WiFi network\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n**XIAO side (the button):**\n- One leg of the push-button -> XIAO pin `D2` (GPIO 4)\n- Other leg of the push-button -> XIAO `GND`\n- No external pull-up resistor needed - the sketch turns on the ESP32's internal pull-up (`INPUT_PULLUP`), so a press reads LOW.\n\n**NodeMCU side (the LED):**\n- NodeMCU `GPIO 16` -> 220R resistor -> long leg (anode, +) of the LED\n- Short leg (cathode, -) of the LED -> NodeMCU `GND`\n- The resistor goes in series with the LED. `GPIO -> resistor -> LED -> GND` is the easy mental model.\n- Why GPIO16 and not GPIO5? GPIO5 is a \"strapping pin\" on the classic ESP32 - it's sampled at reset and has an internal pull-up active during boot, which makes any LED on it flicker for a moment every time the board resets. GPIO16 is a plain general-purpose pin with no boot-time role, so it's the cleaner first-project choice.\n\n**Both boards plug into their own USB-C cable.** They never connect to each other physically - they talk over WiFi.\n\n## Bring-up sequence (read before flashing)\n\nThis project uses mDNS so we don't have to chase IP addresses. The NodeMCU advertises itself on the LAN as `nodemcu-lamp.local`, and the XIAO talks to that name. No hardcoded peer IP, no chicken-and-egg re-flash cycle.\n\n1. Open the sketch. Replace `PASSWORD_HERE` with the GeeFam WiFi password (there's a `// TODO` comment marking the spot).\n2. **Flash the NodeMCU first:** uncomment `#define ROLE_NODEMCU`, comment out `#define ROLE_XIAO`, upload. Open Serial Monitor (115200) and watch for `mDNS up: http://nodemcu-lamp.local/`. Optional sanity check: from any browser on the same WiFi, open `http://nodemcu-lamp.local/` and you should see the lamp's status text.\n3. **Flash the XIAO second:** flip the `ROLE_` defines (XIAO on, NodeMCU off), upload. Open Serial Monitor and you should see it print `Will talk to http://nodemcu-lamp.local/led/toggle`.\n4. Press the button on the XIAO. The NodeMCU's LED toggles. The XIAO's serial log shows `-> GET ...` and `peer replied 200 : OK on`.\n\nIf mDNS resolution misbehaves on your network, the description for the \"Things to change\" section below explains how to drop back to hardcoded IPs.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- \"Press here, light there.\" This is the heart of every smart-home gadget: a sensor on one device, an actuator on another, glued together by a network.\n- The two boards have different jobs in this build: the XIAO is purely a **client** (it sends one HTTP request when the button is pressed) and the NodeMCU is purely a **server** (it listens for requests and toggles the LED). That asymmetry keeps the sketch small. The \"make it bidirectional\" version is in \"Things to change\" below.\n- We're using plain HTTP GET requests. When the XIAO sees a button press, it sends `GET /led/toggle` to `nodemcu-lamp.local`. The NodeMCU's web server hears that request, flips the LED, and replies with \"OK\". No cloud, no app - just two boards on the same WiFi.\n- mDNS is why we don't need to type IP addresses. It's a tiny \"who is here\" announcement system that lets devices on the same LAN find each other by name (`somename.local`). The NodeMCU shouts \"I'm `nodemcu-lamp`\", the XIAO listens, done.\n- This pattern (HTTP requests between devices) scales. Once the Orange Pi is running and hosting a real MQTT broker, you replace the direct HTTP call with \"publish to topic\" and \"subscribe to topic\" - same idea, less wiring.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Make it bidirectional. Right now the XIAO is purely a remote: it has no web server and the NodeMCU never sends it anything. To go two-way, add a `WebServer` on the XIAO with a `/notify` route, and","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"The lamp: runs a web server, toggles the external LED on request."},{"item_name":"Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3","qty":1,"notes":"The remote: reads the button, sends HTTP GET to the NodeMCU over WiFi."},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"Board pin headers down to breadboard tie-points."},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Breadboard rails and tie-point-to-tie-point wiring."},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"The visible output on the NodeMCU side, driven from GPIO16."},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the button, LED, and resistor. No soldering."},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current-limit in series with the LED on NodeMCU GPIO16."},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x4.3mm (white pin)","qty":1,"notes":"Button on the XIAO side. One leg to GPIO4, other leg to GND."}]},{"id":41,"name":"Hallway Weather Window","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"USB-A to Micro-USB from laptop or 5V/1A charger into the ESP32. 3.3V rail feeds the DHT11; 5V rail feeds LCD, relay coil, and (via COM/NO) the LED strip. Cap strip at ~200 mA on this rail; longer strips need a separate 5V/2A supply, GND tied.","description":"**What it is**: Hang it in the hallway. A little screen shows the temperature inside your house and a one-word forecast pulled live from the internet (like SUNNY or RAINY). When rain is coming, a blue light strip flicks on so you see it on your way out the door.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It is basically a tiny weather robot that yells \"TAKE A COAT!\" at your family before school. Your friends have to check a phone to know if it will rain - you just glance at the wall.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the brain, WiFi-capable)\n- 1x LCD 1602 with I2C Backpack (display)\n- 1x DHT11 Temperature & Humidity Sensor (indoor reading - 3-pin breakout preferred)\n- 1x 1-channel Relay Module (switches the LED strip)\n- 1x LED 5mm Blue (status indicator while talking to the internet)\n- 1x 220R resistor (current limit for the blue LED)\n- 1x Breadboard 830-point\n- DuPont jumper wires (M-M, M-F, F-F mix)\n- 1x USB-A to Micro-USB cable (power + programming)\n\nYou also need (not in this allocation, supplied separately):\n- A short 5V LED strip (or single LED-on-leads). The relay switches its POSITIVE leg between the 5V rail and the strip - see the wiring diagram. Keep it short: about 200 mA max at 5V if you are powering everything from one USB port (see \"Power budget\" below).\n- A free Open-Meteo API endpoint (no key required) - the sketch already points at Andy's home grid square in northern England; change the lat/lon at the top to wherever your hallway is.\n- If your DHT11 is the bare 4-pin sensor (not the small breakout PCB), add a 4.7k-10k pull-up resistor from DATA to 3.3V. The blue 3-pin breakout has this on board already.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n- The LCD's I2C backpack uses only 4 wires: VCC -> 5V, GND -> GND, SDA -> GPIO 21, SCL -> GPIO 22. The ESP32's I2C default is exactly those two pins, no configuration needed.\n- The DHT11 needs 3.3V. Some clones tolerate 5V, but 3.3V is safer with an ESP32 because its GPIO is 3.3V; using 5V Vcc with a 3.3V data pin can clamp current through the protection diodes. Data goes to GPIO 4. If you bought the bare 4-pin DHT11 (no PCB), you also need a 4.7k-10k pull-up from DATA to 3.3V; the 3-pin breakout already has one.\n- The relay module is low-level trigger (active-LOW). IN -> GPIO 25. We set the pin HIGH *before* `pinMode(..., OUTPUT)` so the relay doesn't click on at boot.\n- We are doing **high-side switching of the LED strip's positive leg**: the 5V rail goes into the relay's **COM** terminal, the strip's **+** wire goes to the relay's **NO** (normally open) terminal, and the strip's **-** wire returns directly to GND. When the relay energises, COM connects to NO and the strip lights up. NC is left unused.\n- The blue status LED goes from GPIO 2 -> 220R resistor -> LED anode -> LED cathode -> GND. GPIO 2 is also the NodeMCU's built-in blue LED on most clones, so you will see two LEDs blink in sync. That is fine - it confirms your wiring matches the silkscreen.\n- **GPIO 2 strapping-pin caveat**: GPIO 2 must be LOW or floating at boot for the chip to flash-boot normally. The 220R-to-LED-to-GND path keeps it pulled low at power-up (the LED's forward voltage drop is below the strap threshold). Do NOT wire a pull-up from GPIO 2 to 3.3V - that will brick boot. Driving it HIGH from `loop()` after boot is fine.\n- Common ground! The ESP32 GND, the breadboard GND rail, the relay GND, and the LED strip GND must all be tied together. Floating grounds are the classic \"it works once then never again\" bug.\n\n## Power budget (read this before adding more LEDs)\n\nA laptop USB-A port supplies roughly 500 mA at 5V. From that the ESP32 itself takes 80-240 mA (bursty on WiFi TX), the LCD backlight 20-40 mA, and the relay coil ~70 mA when energised. That leaves a hard ceiling of around 150-200 mA for the LED strip if you want a stable system - call it 1-2 short WS2812-style segments or a couple of plain 5V LEDs in parallel with their own resistors. If you want a longer strip, switch to a **dedicated 5V/2A supply for the strip**, feed its + into the relay's COM via a separate wire, and **tie its GND to the ESP32 GND** (the relay only switches the + line; the grounds must stay common or nothing turns on).\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The LCD is talked to over **I2C**, a 2-wire serial bus. The same two pins can address up to ~120 devices because each has a unique address (the backpack is usually `0x27` or `0x3F`). The sketch probes both at boot and constructs the LCD object once it knows which address answered.\n- The weather data comes f","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"WiFi-capable controller; reads DHT11, calls Open-Meteo over HTTPS, drives LCD and relay"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Connects ESP32 to LCD, DHT11, relay module, and LED"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Blue","qty":1,"notes":"Status LED that blinks while a WiFi/HTTP request is in flight"},{"item_name":"1-channel Relay Module","qty":1,"notes":"Active-LOW. High-side switches the 5V LED strip via COM (to 5V) and NO (to strip +)."},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the DHT11, LED, resistor, and routes 3.3V / 5V / GND rails"},{"item_name":"LCD 1602 with I2C Backpack","qty":1,"notes":"Two-line display: row 0 indoor sensor, row 1 scrolling outdoor forecast"},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current limit for the blue status LED on GPIO 2 (to GND, not to 3.3V - GPIO 2 is a strapping pin)"},{"item_name":"DHT11 Temperature & Humidity Sensor","qty":1,"notes":"Indoor temp/humidity every 2s. Use the 3-pin breakout (built-in pull-up). If bare 4-pin, add 4.7k-10k from DATA to 3.3V."}]},{"id":42,"name":"MQTT Home: Pi broker + XIAO sensor publisher","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"Orange Pi One: 5V / 2A wall adapter via the 4.0/1.7mm barrel jack (NOT micro-USB). XIAO ESP32-C3: 5V over USB-C from a laptop or phone charger. DHT11 draws power from the XIAO's 3V3 rail. Router + WiFi assumed already powered.","description":"**What it is**: A tiny sensor sits on your shelf and quietly sniffs the room's temperature and humidity. Every 30 seconds it whispers the numbers across WiFi to a little computer in another room, and you watch them pop up on your laptop screen.\n\n**Why it's fun**: This is your first project where two gadgets in different parts of the house actually talk to each other - one listens, one talks, and you're the puppet master in the middle. Unplug the sensor and a \"I'm gone!\" message shows up on the laptop a few seconds later, like the gadget left you a goodbye note.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- Orange Pi One (H3) - the protagonist. Already booted into Armbian with `mosquitto` and `mosquitto-clients` installed (see the resume checklist in the OrangePi_One folder).\n- Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3 - the leaf sensor node.\n- DHT11 temperature + humidity sensor (3-pin module variant has its own pull-up; bare 4-pin DHT11 needs a 10K resistor between DATA and 3V3).\n- 1x 10K resistor (1/8W) for the DHT11 pull-up.\n- Mini breadboard + a handful of M-M jumpers to hold the XIAO and DHT11.\n- Router / WiFi on `GeeFam` (2.4 GHz) - Pi on Ethernet, XIAO on WiFi, both on the same subnet.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThis project is mostly a network wiring diagram, not a breadboard one. Three things have to be true:\n\n1. **Pi on the LAN.** Plug the Pi's Ethernet into the same router that broadcasts `GeeFam`. Power it from the 5V/2A barrel supply (NOT micro-USB - see the OrangePi_One CLAUDE notes about silent brownouts). Find its IP on the router admin page the same way you found the XIAO at `192.168.88.30`.\n2. **Mosquitto listening on the Pi.** After SSH-ing in with `ssh root@<pi-ip>`, run:\n   ```bash\n   apt update && apt install -y mosquitto mosquitto-clients\n   # Allow LAN clients (for learning only - no auth yet):\n   cat > /etc/mosquitto/conf.d/lan.conf <<'EOF'\n   listener 1883 0.0.0.0\n   allow_anonymous true\n   EOF\n   systemctl restart mosquitto\n   systemctl enable  mosquitto\n   ```\n   Confirm with `ss -tlnp | grep 1883`. You should see mosquitto bound to `0.0.0.0:1883`.\n3. **DHT11 on the XIAO.**\n   - `DHT11 VCC -> XIAO 3V3`\n   - `DHT11 GND -> XIAO GND`\n   - `DHT11 DATA -> XIAO D1 (GPIO 3)`\n   - 10K resistor between `DATA` and `3V3` (skip if your DHT11 is the 3-pin pre-pulled-up variant)\n\nUpdate `MQTT_HOST` in the sketch to the Pi's IP before flashing. Open Serial Monitor at 115200, then on the Pi run:\n\n```bash\nmosquitto_sub -h localhost -t 'home/livingroom/#' -v\n```\n\nYou should see `home/livingroom/status online` immediately, followed by temperature + humidity every 30 seconds.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **Pub/sub vs request/response.** The XIAO doesn't know or care who's listening. It pushes readings to a topic; anything that cares (a dashboard, Home Assistant, a Node-RED flow on the same Pi) subscribes. Adding a second display device later is zero changes to the publisher.\n- **Last-will testament.** `connect()` registers `home/livingroom/status = \"offline\"` as a \"send this if I die\". If the XIAO loses power or WiFi, the broker fires that message after the keepalive expires. That's how the Pi can know the node is gone without polling it.\n- **Retained messages.** Both readings are published with the retained flag. A subscriber that connects ten minutes later still gets the last known value immediately instead of waiting up to 30 seconds for the next one.\n- **The hub/leaf split is now real.** Up until now every project on the kit ran end-to-end on a single board. This one stops working unless both halves are alive on the network at the same time. That's the architectural shift that makes the rest of the kit's planned services (Home Assistant, Grafana) possible.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- **Add a second leaf.** Flash a similar sketch onto the NodeMCU-32S with topic `home/garage/temperature` and watch both streams in one `mosquitto_sub -t 'home/#' -v` window.\n- **Turn on auth.** Replace `allow_anonymous true` with a password file (`mosquitto_passwd -c /etc/mosquitto/passwd andy`), and pass the credentials into `mqtt.connect()`.\n- **Persist to disk.** `apt install sqlite3`, then run a tiny Python loop on the Pi that subscribes and inserts rows into a SQLite table. That's the foundation of the future InfluxDB + Grafana setup without committing to the heavy stack yet.\n- **Add the OLED.** Wire the 0.96\" OLED to the NodeMCU-32S and have it subscribe to `home/livingroom/temperature` so it shows the XIAO's reading on its own screen. First \"device A talks to device B via th","allocations":[{"item_name":"Orange Pi One (H3)","qty":1,"notes":"Mosquitto MQTT broker - the hub the ESP32 publishes sensor readings to"},{"item_name":"Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3","qty":1,"notes":"Sensor leaf node - reads DHT11 and publishes to broker over WiFi"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Wires between XIAO and DHT11 on the breadboard"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the XIAO and DHT11"},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Pull-up on DHT11 DATA line (some bare DHT11 modules need this; harmless if module already has one)"},{"item_name":"DHT11 Temperature & Humidity Sensor","qty":1,"notes":"Sensor source for the MQTT messages"}]},{"id":43,"name":"Joystick Doodle Pad on OLED","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"ESP32 is powered from a laptop via USB; the joystick module and OLED both run off the ESP32's 3.3V rail through the breadboard.","description":"**What it is**: Wiggle the joystick and a tiny blinking dot zips around a little screen. Click the stick down and the dot starts leaving a white trail behind it, so you can draw whatever you want one pixel at a time.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It's like having your own pocket Etch-A-Sketch that you actually built with your own hands. Draw your name, sketch a stick-figure dad, write a secret message, then hold the button for a second and watch the whole screen wipe clean so you can start over.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Joystick Module (2-axis + button) - the star: two potentiometers (X and Y) and a push-button under the cap\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S - reads the joystick and drives the screen\n- 1x 0.96\" OLED Display (I2C) - the 128x64 canvas you paint on\n- 1x Breadboard (~400 tie-points) - share 3.3V and GND between three things cleanly\n- 1x Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc - power rails on the breadboard\n- 1x Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc - female ends plug into the joystick/OLED header pins\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nEverything in this build runs off the ESP32's 3.3V rail. Most generic 0.96\" SSD1306 OLED modules accept 3.3-5V on VCC because they have an onboard regulator, but we are using 3.3V everywhere so the whole project shares one rail - cleaner and one less thing to think about.\n\nA note on the joystick's VCC pin: most of these modules are silkscreened \"+5V\". Ignore that label and wire it to 3.3V anyway. The joystick is just two potentiometers and a push-button, so it doesn't care what voltage you feed it - the ADC reading is a ratio of the supply voltage, and the code reads it the same way at 3.3V as it would at 5V. If you DO connect the joystick's VCC to 5V on an ESP32, the button's SW pin will sit near 5V when the button is pressed, and that 5V will land on GPIO 32 - a 3.3V-only input. That's the kind of mistake that quietly stresses the chip, so just stick to 3.3V.\n\nJoystick to ESP32:\n\n- `+5V` (yes, even though it says +5V on the silkscreen) -> 3.3V rail\n- `GND` -> GND rail\n- `VRX` -> GPIO 34 (X axis, an input-only ADC pin - perfect for this)\n- `VRY` -> GPIO 35 (Y axis, input-only ADC pin)\n- `SW`  -> GPIO 32 (the click-down button under the stick)\n\nOLED to ESP32:\n\n- `VCC` -> 3.3V rail\n- `GND` -> GND rail\n- `SDA` -> GPIO 21 (default ESP32 I2C data line)\n- `SCL` -> GPIO 22 (default ESP32 I2C clock line)\n\nThe joystick button has no external pull-up resistor, so the code turns on the ESP32's internal pull-up (`INPUT_PULLUP`). That means \"released\" reads HIGH and \"pressed\" reads LOW - watch for that in the talking points.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- Move the stick: a blinking dot (the cursor) moves around the screen.\n- Click the button: the pen toggles between \"up\" and \"down\". With the pen down, the cursor leaves a trail of white pixels - that's the drawing.\n- Hold the button for one second: the screen wipes clean and the pen lifts. Good for \"oops, start over\".\n- The cursor blinks so you can see where you are even on a blank canvas.\n- The deadzone in the code (`DEADZONE = 250`) means the cursor only moves when you push the stick noticeably. Without it, ADC noise alone would make the cursor drift.\n- How the drawing survives the blinking cursor: the code keeps its own `canvas[128][64]` array of which pixels have been \"painted\". Every frame it wipes the OLED, redraws the whole canvas from that array, then stamps the cursor on top. The cursor is never stored in the canvas, so it can never leave phantom dots.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Make the pen \"thicker\": draw a 2x2 or 3x3 block of canvas cells instead of a single one when the pen is down.\n- Diagonal-only mode for a 10-year-old's first maze drawing - only commit pixels when X or Y exceeds a threshold.\n- Add a small status line in the top corner showing PEN UP / PEN DOWN (just draw text after the canvas copy, before the cursor).\n- Speed-sensitive cursor: square the stick deflection so tiny pushes nudge gently and full deflection zooms.\n- Save the drawing to LittleFS so it survives a reboot, then add a \"gallery\" mode that cycles through saved drawings.\n- Two-player Etch-A-Sketch: one joystick controls X, a second joystick controls Y, and you have to cooperate.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nThis is the first project that asks the ESP32 to read TWO analog inputs at once and translate that into something visually obvious. The joystick teaches three ideas that show up everywhere later: an analog reading is a number from 0 to 4095, a \"center\" reading is rarely exactly half (so you need a deadzone), and a butto","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"Reads joystick analog axes and drives the OLED over I2C"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"From breadboard rails to the joystick and OLED header pins"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Power and signal jumpers on the breadboard"},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1,"notes":"128x64 pixel canvas the joystick paints on"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the ESP32 and shares 3.3V/GND rails to the modules"},{"item_name":"Joystick Module (2-axis + button)","qty":1,"notes":"Main input: X/Y move the cursor, button toggles draw mode"}]},{"id":44,"name":"Laser Tripwire Alarm","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from a laptop or 5V phone charger into the Arduino Uno; the Uno's 5V rail powers the laser, photoresistor, and alarm LED.","description":"**What it is**: A real red laser shoots a thin beam across the room to a little sensor on the other side. The second someone walks through and breaks the beam, an alarm LED snaps on - just like in spy movies.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Set it up across your bedroom doorway and you'll know the instant your sister sneaks in. It's the closest thing to a real museum security system you can build on a kitchen table, and the laser dot is honestly just cool to look at.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- Arduino Uno R3 (the brain, 5V GPIO)\n- KY-008 Red Laser Module (the beam - the star of the show)\n- KY-018 Photoresistor Module (the beam catcher)\n- 5mm red LED + 330R resistor (the alarm indicator)\n- Breadboard, jumper wires\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n- **Laser (KY-008)**: three pins, labeled S (signal), middle (often unused or +), and `-` (GND). On most KY-008 boards the laser turns on whenever **S is HIGH and `-` is GND**. Wire S to Arduino pin 7 and `-` to Arduino GND. The middle pin is usually not connected, leave it floating.\n- **Photoresistor (KY-018)**: three pins - `S` (analog out), middle = VCC (5V), `-` = GND. Wire S to A0, middle to 5V, `-` to GND.\n- **Alarm LED**: long leg (anode) to Arduino pin 9 through the 330R resistor on the breadboard. Short leg (cathode) to GND. The resistor is non-negotiable - bare LEDs on a GPIO pin die fast.\n- **Aim**: with everything wired up, hold the laser so the red dot lands exactly on the small black sensor on top of the KY-018 module. The closer the dot is to the sensor, the bigger the difference between \"beam on\" and \"beam broken\" - which makes the tripwire more sensitive.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- \"Why does the laser need GND?\" Every circuit needs a complete loop. Electricity leaves the 5V/signal pin, does its work in the laser, and has to come back home through GND. Without GND, no loop, no light.\n- \"Why doesn't the laser go through my hand?\" Red 650nm laser light is visible, fairly low energy, and very directional. Your skin absorbs and scatters it, so almost none reaches the sensor on the other side - that's exactly why this works as a tripwire.\n- \"Why calibrate at startup?\" Different rooms have different ambient light. The sketch reads the photoresistor 20 times in the first second, averages those, and uses that as the \"beam is hitting the target\" baseline. Then any big drop from that baseline means something interrupted the beam.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add a buzzer or relay-driven siren in parallel with the alarm LED so it makes noise.\n- Replace `delay(50)` with a latch: once tripped, stay tripped until a button is pressed to re-arm.\n- Aim the laser at a mirror placed across the room and bounce the beam back into the photoresistor - turn a doorway into a long tripwire.\n- Log the time of each break to Serial and graph it later.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nThis is the project that makes a 10-year-old say \"this is the coolest thing I've ever built.\" A real red laser, a real invisible tripwire across the kitchen, an alarm that goes off when someone walks through. It teaches three big ideas at once - digital output (turning the laser on), analog input (reading varying light levels), and calibration (the code adapts to whatever room you're in instead of using magic numbers).\n\n## Eye-safety note\n\nThe KY-008 is a low-power Class 2 (or similar) red laser, but **never aim it at anyone's eyes**, including pets. Aim it across a flat surface like a tabletop where the only thing that can break it is a hand or a toy car driving through.","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1,"notes":"Reads photoresistor, drives laser and alarm LED"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"Connects the KY-008 and KY-018 modules to the breadboard"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Board-to-breadboard and module power/signal wiring"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"Alarm indicator that lights when beam is broken"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the LED and connects everything"},{"item_name":"KY-008 Red Laser Module (650nm)","qty":1,"notes":"Emits the red beam aimed at the photoresistor"},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current-limiting resistor for the alarm LED"},{"item_name":"KY-018 Photoresistor Module","qty":1,"notes":"Detects whether the laser beam is hitting it"}]},{"id":45,"name":"Reaction-Time Game v2 (Proto PCB Edition)","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"USB cable from a laptop or 5V phone charger into the Arduino Uno's USB-B socket - the Uno regulates and feeds the proto PCB's 5V/GND rails.","description":"**What it is**: It is your old reaction-time game, but built for keeps. Instead of wires popping out of a breadboard, the buttons and LEDs sit on a real circuit board that you can drop in a drawer, pull out next week, and it still works.\n\n**Why it's fun**: This is the first time you get to use a soldering iron, and at the end you walk away with a finished gadget that looks store-bought, not \"school project\". Hand it to your little brother and watch him try (and fail) to beat your best time.\n\n**Soldering project - up front warning.** This is the one project in the kit that uses a soldering iron. Everything else has been solderless (breadboard + DuPont jumpers). The point of this build is to take a circuit Andy already knows works - the reaction-time game that was his first build - and make it *permanent* on a double-sided proto PCB so it lives on a shelf instead of occupying the breadboard. Treat it as Andy's intermediate solo project. His son can watch and read out reaction times, but the iron stays in adult hands.\n\nThe Arduino itself is **not** soldered. We solder female header sockets onto the proto PCB and the Uno plugs into them, so the brain is reusable for the next thing. Only the buttons, LEDs, resistors, and short wire links get soldered.\n\nWorkflow (this is half the lesson):\n\n1. Rebuild the game on a breadboard one last time using these exact pins. Confirm green/red/buttons all behave.\n2. Plan the layout on the PCB *before* heating the iron - a sharpie dot on each hole that gets a part.\n3. Solder the lowest-profile parts first (resistors), then headers and buttons, LEDs last.\n4. Flip the board, route point-to-point with the stripped insides of an M-M jumper wire. Keep wires short.\n5. Inspect every joint under a lamp - should be shiny and concave, not a dull blob. Re-flow anything that looks like a \"cold\" joint.\n6. Plug the Uno in *last*. Power up. If anything draws weird current, unplug fast.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Double-Sided Prototype PCB (the star - the new home of the circuit)\n- 1x Arduino Uno R3 (sits in a socket on the PCB, not soldered in)\n- 2x Tactile push-button (START + PLAYER)\n- 1x Green 5mm LED (GO light)\n- 1x Red 5mm LED (WAIT / FAIL light)\n- 2x 330R resistor (LED current limit)\n- 2x 10K resistor (button pull-ups - belt-and-braces alongside `INPUT_PULLUP`)\n- 1x Female header strip, cut into two short rails to socket the Uno's D-pins\n- 1x Male header strip, cut for the PCB I/O block\n- Hookup wire (stripped from an M-M jumper) for point-to-point links on the underside\n- 1x Breadboard for the pre-solder dry run\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n- Both buttons are wired **active-LOW**: one leg goes to its Uno pin, the other to GND. The Uno's internal pull-up (`INPUT_PULLUP`) plus the external 10K resistor to the **5V rail** pulls the pin HIGH when the button is open. Pressing the button shorts it to GND.\n- LEDs face the same way every time: long leg (anode) toward the Uno pin via the 330R, short leg (cathode) to GND.\n- Keep the LED resistor right next to its LED on the PCB - that way if the resistor ever fries you can find it instantly.\n- The Uno's 5V and GND rails get a single soldered tie-point on the PCB, and everything else fans out from there. No daisy-chained ground hops.\n- Flux helps. Lead-free solder sticks better with extra flux dabbed on the pad.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- \"Why are we soldering this one?\" Because a breadboard contact relies on a spring. Springs lose tension, and a circuit you want to keep deserves a real metal joint.\n- A *cold solder joint* looks like crumbly grey play-dough. A *good* joint looks shiny and pulled toward the pad - like a tiny silver volcano. Re-heat anything that looks crumbly.\n- The Arduino plugs into a socket, not directly into the board. That socket is the most expensive bit of foresight in the whole build - it means we can pull the Uno back out for the next project.\n- `INPUT_PULLUP` does the same job as the external 10K in software. We use both. On a breadboard, software alone is fine. On a permanent board that might sit near a noisy power supply, the physical resistor adds confidence.\n- The first sketch already debounced the buttons in code. On the soldered board that matters more, because a flaky button starts to look like a \"ghost press\".\n- Note on the debounce helper: the v2 sketch keys its per-button state by a small index (0 and 1) passed in by the caller, not by the raw GPIO pin number. That means swapping a button to a different Uno pin only changes one constant at th","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1,"notes":"Brains of the game. Plugs into the soldered proto PCB via female header sockets (so the Uno stays reusable)."},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Used as point-to-point wire on the PCB underside (strip jacket, tin tips) plus optional external test leads."},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1,"notes":"The GO light - lights up when the player should hit the button."},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"The WAIT / FAIL light - shows while waiting and flashes if the player jumps the gun."},{"item_name":"Pin Header 1x40 Female (2.54mm)","qty":1,"notes":"Cut two short strips to make a socket on the PCB for the Uno's digital pins, so the Arduino is removable, not soldered."},{"item_name":"Pin Header 1x40 Male Breakaway (2.54mm)","qty":1,"notes":"Cut short strips to wire the PCB's I/O block - jumper leads from the Uno-socket area to the buttons/LEDs."},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Used BEFORE soldering to rebuild and re-confirm the working circuit one last time, so Andy isn't debugging a soldered board."},{"item_name":"Double-Sided Prototype PCB (assorted sizes)","qty":1,"notes":"The star of the project - the permanent home for the reaction-time game circuit. Pick a small size that fits the part count."},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":2,"notes":"Pull-up resistors for the two buttons (we use INPUT_PULLUP in code too, but external 10K is good practice on a permanent build for noise immunity)."},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":2,"notes":"Current-limit resistors for the two LEDs."},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x5mm (white pin)","qty":2,"notes":"PLAYER button (reacts to the LED) and RESET/START button. The 5mm tall ones poke through the proto PCB cleanly."}]},{"id":46,"name":"Clap-O-Meter (Live Sound Bar Graph)","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB-C cable from a laptop or 5V phone charger into the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S; the ESP32's onboard 3.3V regulator powers the KY-037 mic, the OLED, and the red LED through a 330R resistor.","description":"**What it is**: Clap your hands. A bar shoots up across the little screen and a red light flashes. Whisper and the bar barely twitches. Yell and it pegs all the way to the edge.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You finally get to SEE how loud you are, like the bouncing bars on a music speaker. Have a shouting contest with your brother and watch the screen prove who actually won.\n\nA live loudness meter built around the KY-037 microphone. The ESP32 reads the mic, scales the result onto a bar across the OLED, and flashes a red LED whenever the sound crosses a \"loud\" threshold - so a clap, a shout or a slammed door becomes a thing you can see.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- KY-037 Microphone Sensor (the protagonist) - analog audio input\n- ESP32 NodeMCU-32S - reads the mic, drives the OLED, controls the LED\n- 0.96\" OLED Display (I2C) - the bar graph and the labels\n- 5mm red LED + 330R resistor - flashes on loud sound\n- 10uF electrolytic capacitor - decoupling across the mic VCC/GND for a cleaner signal\n- Breadboard, a pack of M-M jumpers, a pack of F-M jumpers\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n- The KY-037 runs happily off the ESP32's 3.3V rail. Put VCC and GND on the breadboard power rails first.\n- Drop the 10uF electrolytic cap straight across the mic's VCC and GND pins on the breadboard. Longer leg goes to VCC, shorter leg to GND. This soaks up the little voltage wobbles the mic causes when WiFi or the OLED is busy, and the analog reading visibly settles down.\n- The mic's A0 (analog out) goes to GPIO34. That pin is input-only on the ESP32, which is exactly what you want for a sensor - nothing in your sketch can accidentally drive it.\n- The OLED is I2C: SDA -> GPIO21, SCL -> GPIO22. These are the ESP32's default I2C pins.\n- The red LED gets the usual treatment: GPIO23 -> 330R resistor -> long leg of LED -> short leg to GND. Without the resistor the LED burns out and possibly the GPIO with it.\n- Leave the D0 pin on the KY-037 unconnected. That's the built-in comparator with the blue trimpot, and we don't use it - the sketch does the threshold work in software so you can tweak it without a screwdriver.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The mic doesn't output \"loudness\" - it outputs the audio waveform, swinging above and below a quiet midpoint many hundreds of times a second. We sample on a steady 1 ms tick (~1000 Hz) and measure how far each sample is from the calibrated quiet baseline. That absolute swing is what we draw.\n- The bar uses a \"peak with decay\" trick: every new high pushes the bar up instantly, but a steady countdown drags it back down 20 ms at a time. That's why a clap shoots the bar up and then it slides back - matches how our ears feel loudness.\n- The calibration at boot is just a 1-second average of the quiet room. Cover the mic or shout at it during boot and the baseline will be wrong - reset and let the room be quiet for a second.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Lower `LOUD_THRESHOLD` until a finger snap sets the LED off, or raise it so only a real clap counts.\n- Add a second LED at a higher threshold for \"very loud\" so you have a two-stage indicator.\n- Count claps in a 1-second window and use a double-clap as a \"switch\" - feed that into a relay to make a clap-on-clap-off lamp.\n- Log the peak loudness once a second over WiFi to a tiny web page and watch the room's noise level over an evening.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nIt turns something invisible (sound) into something visible and instantly playable. A 10-year-old can clap and see the bar move - no explanation needed. Underneath it's the same picture a real audio meter on a mixing desk is drawing, which makes it a great springboard into \"how does a microphone actually work\" without any math.","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"Samples mic on GPIO34, drives OLED on I2C, flashes LED on loud sound"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"One pack to bridge the ESP32 header pins to the breadboard rails"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"One pack covers all power, ground and signal hops on the breadboard"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1,"notes":"Decoupling cap across KY-037 VCC and GND to clean the analog signal (longer leg to VCC)"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"Lights up when sound crosses the loud threshold"},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1,"notes":"Shows the live bar graph and a quiet/talking/LOUD label"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the mic, OLED, LED, resistor and decoupling cap"},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current-limiting resistor in series with the red LED"},{"item_name":"KY-037 Microphone Sensor","qty":1,"notes":"Main sensor - analog output (A0) reads the instantaneous audio waveform"}]},{"id":47,"name":"Laser Posture Coach (desk slouch detector)","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S powered via its USB-C port from a laptop or 5 V phone charger; the VL53L0X, OLED, LEDs and button are fed from the ESP32's 3.3 V rail.","description":"**What it is**: A tiny laser on your desk watches your chest while you work. Sit up straight and a green light says \"GOOD.\" Hunch toward the screen and a red light flashes \"SLOUCH!\" until you straighten back up.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You built a robot hall monitor for your own posture - it catches you slouching before your mom does. Same kind of laser ranging that robot vacuums use to dodge furniture, except yours is yelling at you to sit up.\n\nA laser-based desk posture coach. The VL53L0X sits flat on the desk pointing straight up. When you sit upright, it sees your chest at (say) 500 mm. When you slouch forward toward the screen, that distance shrinks. A green LED + \"GOOD\" message means upright; a red LED + \"SLOUCH!\" warning means you've crept too close. A button lets you recalibrate the \"upright\" distance for your chair height.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (brain, drives I2C and LEDs)\n- VL53L0X ToF distance sensor (GY-VL53L0XV2 module) - the protagonist\n- 0.96\" I2C OLED display - shows live distance and posture verdict\n- 1x green 5 mm LED + 330R resistor (good-posture light)\n- 1x red 5 mm LED + 330R resistor (slouch warning)\n- 1x tactile push-button (3x6x4.3 mm) - recalibrate\n- Breadboard + DuPont jumper wires\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n- The ESP32 is powered from a laptop USB port via the on-board USB-C jack. Everything else is fed from the ESP32's 3.3 V and GND rails.\n- Both the OLED and the VL53L0X are I2C devices. They share SDA (GPIO 21) and SCL (GPIO 22). They have different I2C addresses (OLED 0x3C, VL53L0X 0x29), so they coexist on the same two wires without any extra hardware.\n- The VL53L0X module already has on-board pull-up resistors on SDA/SCL and a 3.3 V regulator + level shifters, so it's safe to power from either 3.3 V or 5 V. We use 3.3 V because the ESP32 is a 3.3 V device.\n- LEDs: long leg (anode) to the GPIO through a 330R resistor; short leg (cathode) to GND. Green on GPIO 25, red on GPIO 26.\n- Button between GPIO 27 and GND. We turn on the ESP32's internal pull-up so we don't need an external one. Pressed = LOW.\n- Aim the sensor straight up. Sit at your normal typing position, press the button to set the target distance, then try slouching forward - the red LED should light when your chest gets ~8 cm closer than the target.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- \"Time of Flight\" really does mean what it says: this chip fires a tiny invisible 940 nm laser pulse and measures how long the photons take to bounce back. Light moves about 30 cm in 1 nanosecond - the chip times that.\n- Unlike the HC-SR04 ultrasonic sensor (which uses sound and gives ~1 cm resolution), the VL53L0X reports in millimeters and works fine at close range. That precision is what makes \"you slouched 8 cm\" detectable instead of \"you moved, maybe.\"\n- Two different chips, one pair of wires. I2C lets multiple devices share SDA and SCL by giving each a unique address. The OLED answers at 0x3C; the ToF answers at 0x29.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add a buzzer (a piezo against a GPIO) that beeps after you've been slouching for more than 10 seconds.\n- Log slouch events to the serial monitor with timestamps, then graph \"minutes slouched per hour.\"\n- Push posture stats to the Orange Pi (when it's booted) over WiFi/MQTT and build a daily report.\n- Use the second VL53L0X (we have two) mounted sideways to also detect when you lean too far back.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nThis sensor is the cheapest way an Andy-budget project can do real laser ranging - the same family of chips lives in robot vacuums and phone autofocus systems. Pairing it with an OLED on the same I2C bus is a clean first lesson in \"multiple devices share two wires,\" which is the foundation for almost every more advanced sensor project that follows.\n","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"I2C controller and display driver"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"ESP32 and modules to breadboard"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Breadboard wiring"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1,"notes":"Good-posture indicator"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"Slouch warning indicator"},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1,"notes":"Shows distance and posture status"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Project base"},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":2,"notes":"Current limiting for the two LEDs"},{"item_name":"VL53L0X ToF Distance Sensor (GY-VL53L0XV2 compatible)","qty":1,"notes":"Measures chest-to-desk distance in millimeters"},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x4.3mm (white pin)","qty":1,"notes":"Recalibrate target distance"}]},{"id":48,"name":"Magnet Swipe Counter on OLED","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from a laptop or 5V USB charger into the ESP32's USB port; the ESP32's 3.3V rail powers the KY-003 and the OLED.","description":"**What it is**: Wave a magnet past the little sensor and the number on the tiny screen jumps up by one. A green LED blinks at the exact same moment, so every swipe gives you a flash and a fresh count.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It feels like a magic trick because nothing touches anything - the magnet just floats by and the screen reacts. Race your dad to 100 swipes, or stick the sensor on a door and turn it into a secret \"who opened my room\" detector.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x KY-003 Hall Switch Sensor (the protagonist - a tiny chip that snaps LOW when the south pole of a magnet gets close)\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (reads the sensor and drives the screen)\n- 1x 0.96\" OLED Display (I2C) (shows the live count)\n- 1x Green 5mm LED + 1x 330R resistor (flashes once per swipe for instant tactile feedback)\n- 1x breadboard\n- A handful of jumper wires\n- Any small magnet from around the house (fridge magnet, motor magnet, the round neodymium ones from old hard drives)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe KY-003 module has 3 pins - usually labelled S (signal), middle (VCC) and minus (GND). The middle pin is power, even though it looks like the obvious \"data\" pin. Double-check the silkscreen before plugging in.\n\n- KY-003 `-` to ESP32 `GND`\n- KY-003 middle (`+` or `VCC`) to ESP32 `3V3`\n- KY-003 `S` to ESP32 `GPIO 14`\n\nThe OLED uses the ESP32's default I2C pins:\n\n- OLED `VCC` to ESP32 `3V3`\n- OLED `GND` to ESP32 `GND`\n- OLED `SDA` to ESP32 `GPIO 21`\n- OLED `SCL` to ESP32 `GPIO 22`\n\nThe LED sits on the breadboard:\n\n- ESP32 `GPIO 27` to the long leg (anode) of the green LED\n- Short leg (cathode) through the 330R resistor to `GND`\n\nThe KY-003 has its own pull-up resistor on board, so you don't need an external one. The sketch also enables the ESP32's internal pull-up as a safety net.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- Hall sensors are how your laptop knows the lid is closed and how your phone knows when the magnetic case is shut. They are everywhere and never wear out because nothing physically touches anything.\n- The KY-003 only reacts to one pole of the magnet (the south pole). If your magnet doesn't seem to trigger it, flip the magnet over - that is almost always the fix.\n- \"Active LOW\" means the signal pin is normally HIGH (3.3V) and drops to LOW (0V) when the sensor sees a magnet. This is backwards from how most people think about switches, so it's a great example of \"the world doesn't always agree with you\".\n- The 40 ms debounce window in the code is there because a magnet swept past quickly can make the digital line jitter for a millisecond or two. Without debouncing, one swipe could count as three.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Try different magnets - a small ceramic fridge magnet vs. a tiny neodymium - and see how close you have to get for each. That distance is the sensor's effective range.\n- Change `PIN_HALL` to a different pin (e.g. `4` or `15`) and update the wiring to match. Get into the habit of changing both at once.\n- Add a \"high score\" reset by holding the magnet near the sensor for 3 seconds: detect a long LOW instead of a short pulse and zero the counter.\n- Replace the OLED with the LCD 1602 + I2C backpack and rewrite `drawScreen()` for the LCD - same data, different display.\n- Log each swipe out over WiFi (MQTT to the Orange Pi once it boots) so you have a \"door opened\" history.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nMagnetic sensing is the cleanest \"yes / no\" the universe gives you in beginner electronics. There is no light to interfere with it, no sound to confuse it, no contact to wear out. Pair that with the OLED and you get a project where every action causes an immediate, satisfying visual change - which is the single most motivating thing when you are learning. It also sets up two real-world projects you will eventually want: a door/window open sensor, and a wheel-rotation speedometer.","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"Reads the Hall sensor and drives the OLED via I2C"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"ESP32 male pins to KY-003 / OLED female headers"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Breadboard rails for the LED and power"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1,"notes":"Blinks every time a magnet is detected, for extra tactile feedback"},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1,"notes":"Shows the live magnet count and a swipe animation"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the LED, resistor and shared rails"},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current-limiting resistor for the green LED"},{"item_name":"KY-003 Hall Switch Sensor","qty":1,"notes":"Star of the show - digital Hall switch that triggers on south-pole magnet"}]},{"id":49,"name":"TV Remote LED Color Picker","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from Andy's PC to the Arduino Uno - the Uno's onboard 5V rail powers the KY-022 and all four LEDs.","description":"**What it is**: Point your TV remote at a little dark bubble on the breadboard and press a button. A red, green, blue, or yellow LED lights up, one color per button, like you just hacked the remote to control your own tiny stoplight.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You are teaching the Arduino to listen to a TV remote that already lives in your house, which feels a bit like wizardry. Once it works, hide the remote behind your back and make the lights change from across the room while your sister tries to figure out how you are doing it.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x KY-022 IR Receiver Module (the star - a tiny 38 kHz IR demodulator on a breakout board)\n- 1x Arduino Uno R3 (5V GPIO; the `IRremote` library is rock-solid on AVR)\n- 1x Breadboard (~400 tie-points)\n- 1x F-M jumper pack (for the 3-pin KY-022 header)\n- 1x M-M jumper pack (for the LEDs and resistors on the breadboard)\n- 1x Red, 1x Green, 1x Blue, 1x Yellow 5mm LED\n- 4x 330R resistors (one per LED)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe KY-022 has three pins. Different clone boards label them slightly differently, but the order is always the same: **S** (signal), middle pin is **VCC** on most boards, and the one marked with a `-` is **GND**. If yours has the `-` on the outside instead of the signal, just look at the silkscreen - it's always labelled.\n\n- KY-022 **S** -> Arduino **D2** (the `IRremote` library can use almost any pin; D2 is conventional)\n- KY-022 **VCC** -> Arduino **5V**\n- KY-022 **GND** -> Arduino **GND**\n\nEach LED gets its own resistor between the Arduino pin and the LED's long leg (anode). The short leg (cathode) goes to the breadboard's GND rail.\n\n- D6 -> 330R -> Red LED anode; Red cathode -> GND rail\n- D7 -> 330R -> Green LED anode; Green cathode -> GND rail\n- D8 -> 330R -> Blue LED anode; Blue cathode -> GND rail\n- D9 -> 330R -> Yellow LED anode; Yellow cathode -> GND rail\n- Arduino GND -> breadboard GND rail\n\nWhen the sketch boots it briefly flashes all four LEDs in sequence as a wiring self-test. If one stays dark, that's the row to recheck before worrying about the IR side.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **Why the dome is dark plastic.** That bubble on the KY-022 is a filter that lets infrared through and blocks visible light. Without it, sunlight or a desk lamp would drown out the remote.\n- **Why 38 kHz matters.** Remotes don't send a steady \"on\" - they send the signal as a 38,000-times-a-second flicker. The receiver is tuned to that flicker, so steady light sources don't fool it.\n- **Pressing the same button gives the same code.** That's the whole point of a remote protocol. The sketch learns four of those codes and reacts when it sees them again.\n- **Pin 13 blinks for free.** The `ENABLE_LED_FEEDBACK` flag makes the Uno's built-in LED blink every time *any* IR frame arrives. Even an unknown button proves the receiver is alive.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add a fifth \"off\" button that turns every LED off, by extending the learn sequence to five buttons.\n- Swap the LEDs for the 2-channel Songle relay (low-voltage DC only - the bedside lamp stays on its own switch) and turn this into a remote-controlled fan or LED strip switch.\n- Print the raw `0x...` codes for *every* button on your remote on the Serial Monitor and make a little reference table. You'll see the same prefix on every button - that's the remote's \"address\".\n- Wire up the LCD 1602 with I2C Backpack and have it show the name of the last colour pressed instead of just lighting an LED.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nMost beginner sensor projects work in *one* direction: the world changes, the sensor responds. IR receivers flip that around - they give you a way to *send commands in* to the Arduino from across the room, using equipment (a TV remote) that's already in the house. Once you can decode a remote, an enormous chunk of consumer electronics suddenly stops being a black box. And because you're capturing real codes off a real remote, every household ends up with a slightly different demo, which keeps it fresh when Andy's son wants to show it off.","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1,"notes":"5V MCU - KY-022 works happily at 5V and IRremote library has rock-solid Uno support"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"Connects KY-022 3-pin header and Arduino pins to the breadboard"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Breadboard hops between LEDs, resistors and rails"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Blue","qty":1,"notes":"Lights when the 'blue' remote button is pressed"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1,"notes":"Lights when the 'green' remote button is pressed"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"Lights when the 'red' remote button is pressed"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Yellow","qty":1,"notes":"Lights when the 'yellow' remote button is pressed"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the LEDs and resistors"},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":4,"notes":"Current-limiting resistor, one per LED (330R is a safe middle value for 5V logic)"},{"item_name":"KY-022 IR Receiver Module","qty":1,"notes":"Star of the show - decodes IR pulses from any household remote"}]},{"id":50,"name":"Invisible Tripwire Alarm with OLED Face","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from a laptop or 5V phone charger into the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S; the ESP32's onboard regulator feeds the IR module, OLED, and LED from its 3.3V rail.","description":"**What it is**: Set up an invisible beam across a doorway. When someone walks through it, a little face on the screen jumps from calm to shocked, a red light snaps on, and a counter ticks up by one.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Sneak it onto your bedroom door and catch your sister mid-snoop - the screen literally goes \"!\" when she crosses the line. It's the same trick museums use to know when you're getting too close to the dinosaur bones.\n\nA doorway \"invisible tripwire\" that watches a small patch of air in front of the IR Obstacle Avoidance Module. The moment your hand (or your son's hand) crosses the beam, the OLED face flips from calm \"ARMED\" to shocked \"INTRUDER!\", a red LED lights up, and a trip counter on the screen ticks up. Pull your hand away and everything snaps back. It's the same satisfying \"did I really do that?\" feedback as a museum motion sensor, built in an afternoon.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x IR Obstacle Avoidance Module (the protagonist - a digital \"is something in front of me?\" sensor)\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (reads the sensor, drives the OLED and LED)\n- 1x 0.96\" OLED Display (I2C) (for the ARMED / INTRUDER faces and trip counter)\n- 1x 5mm red LED (lights while the beam is broken)\n- 1x 330R resistor (current-limit for the red LED)\n- 1x breadboard, plus F-M and M-M jumper wires\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n- The IR module has a 3-pin header: `VCC`, `GND`, `OUT`. Power it from the ESP32's `3V3` pin and `GND`. (The module accepts 3.3V-5V; 3.3V keeps logic levels safe for the ESP32.)\n- `OUT` goes to `GPIO 32`. The module has its own pull-up, so use `INPUT` (no internal pull-up needed).\n- The OLED is I2C: `SDA` -> `GPIO 21`, `SCL` -> `GPIO 22`, plus `3V3` and `GND`. These are the ESP32's default I2C pins, so `Wire.begin()` without arguments is enough.\n- Red LED: `GPIO 25` -> 330R -> LED anode (long leg) -> LED cathode (short leg) -> `GND`. Resistor first protects the LED if the wire slips.\n- The IR module has a small blue trimpot on the back. Turn it with a tiny screwdriver to adjust trigger distance from a couple of centimetres up to about 30 cm. There are also two indicator LEDs on the module itself: a power LED and an \"obstacle detected\" LED. The obstacle LED lighting up is a great sanity check before you even look at the OLED.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- *Active-LOW logic, again.* Just like the 2-ch relay board: the sensor's `OUT` line sits HIGH normally and pulls LOW when something is detected. Show your son `digitalRead(PIN_IR_OUT) == LOW` and ask him \"so... does LOW mean nothing's there, or something IS there?\" It's the classic beginner trap that's easier to learn once than to memorise forever.\n- *Why debounce?* The IR detector edge isn't perfectly clean - as a hand enters or leaves the beam you might get a few rapid HIGH/LOW flickers across a millisecond or two. Without the 40ms debounce window, one wave of the hand could log 5 trips. With it, you log exactly 1.\n- *Why a small built-in face on the OLED instead of just text?* Because feedback that feels like a reaction is more fun than feedback that looks like a log entry. Same information, completely different vibe.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add the small piezo buzzer (or a tiny servo arm that \"slams\" a paper door) so the alarm has audio or motion too.\n- Swap the IR Obstacle Avoidance Module for the HC-SR04 ultrasonic and compare: ultrasonic gives you a *distance number*, the IR module only gives you a *yes/no*. Same project shape, very different sensor character.\n- Add WiFi: have the ESP32 send a \"tripped at HH:MM:SS\" line to your laptop or to the Orange Pi (once it's booted) over MQTT, so you can see a log of every doorway crossing.\n- Mount the module sideways in a doorway with cardboard so it makes a real \"laser-style\" beam across the threshold.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nAlmost every \"smart\" presence sensor in the world - shop door chimes, automatic taps, elevator door re-open - is doing exactly what this circuit does: a beam, a detector, a digital edge, and a reaction. Building the toy version once makes the grown-up versions stop feeling like magic. It's also a near-zero-frustration project: no analog calibration, no flaky timing, no maths - just \"something is here / something is not here\" with instant visual payoff.\n","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"Reads sensor, drives OLED and LED"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"ESP32 pins to OLED and to the IR module's 3-pin header"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Breadboard wiring for the LED and rails"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"Lights up while the beam is broken"},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1,"notes":"Shows ARMED / INTRUDER faces and trip counter"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the LED, resistor and shared rails"},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current-limiting resistor for the red LED"},{"item_name":"IR Obstacle Avoidance Module","qty":1,"notes":"The tripwire sensor (digital OUT goes LOW on detection)"}]},{"id":51,"name":"Piggy Bank Coin Counter (TCRT5000 + OLED)","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB-C cable from a phone charger or laptop into the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S; the ESP32's 3.3V rail powers the TCRT5000 module, the OLED, and the indicator LED.","description":"**What it is**: Drop a coin in the slot. The little screen bumps the count up by one, flashes a green LED, and shouts a cheer just for you. Unplug it, plug it back in tomorrow - your total is still there waiting.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It's a piggy bank that actually KNOWS how rich you are, and it celebrates every single coin like you just won a prize. Race your dad to see who can fill theirs first, or sneak up on it and try to drop a coin so fast the sensor misses it (spoiler: it won't).\n\nA real-world piggy bank that knows how much you've fed it. The TCRT5000 sits in the throat of the slot pointing across the gap; whenever a coin passes between the IR LED and the phototransistor, the module's digital output drops, the ESP32 counts it, the green LED blinks, and the OLED bumps the total along with a cheerful message. The count survives reboots because it is written to the ESP32's flash memory.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the brain - reads the sensor, drives the screen, stores the count)\n- TCRT5000 IR Reflective Tracking Module (the \"eye\" that catches each coin)\n- 0.96\" OLED Display, I2C (the running tally and cheer board)\n- 5mm green LED + 330R resistor (an instant \"I saw that\" flash)\n- Breadboard + DuPont jumpers (no soldering)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n- TCRT5000 has four pins: VCC, GND, DO (digital out), AO (analog out). We only use DO.\n- Power the TCRT5000 from 3.3V on the ESP32, not 5V. The module's onboard LM393 comparator is happy at 3.3V and that keeps the DO line at 3.3V too - safe for the ESP32 GPIO.\n- DO is \"active LOW\": idle = HIGH, coin overhead = LOW. The onboard pot lets you tune the trigger distance - turn it until the on-board indicator LED just lights when a coin is in position.\n- OLED is I2C: SDA = GPIO 21, SCL = GPIO 22 (the ESP32's default I2C pins).\n- The green LED's long leg (anode) goes to GPIO 17 through the 330R resistor; the short leg (cathode) goes to GND.\n- Common ground everywhere - the sensor module, OLED, and LED all share GND with the ESP32.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The TCRT5000 is basically an IR flashlight and an IR camera sitting next to each other. The flashlight always shines forward; the camera only \"sees\" light if something in front bounces some of it back. A coin held a centimetre above the sensor is a great mirror for invisible IR.\n- Why \"active LOW\"? The comparator chip on the module is wired so that \"lots of reflected light\" pulls the output down to ground. It is the standard convention for these modules and is worth getting used to - it will come back on the IR obstacle modules and the PIR sensors.\n- The debounce timer (250 ms) is there because one coin passing through can briefly flicker the sensor twice as it tumbles. We treat anything inside 250 ms as the same coin.\n- `Preferences` is the ESP32's tiny built-in key/value store. We save the count every time it changes, so unplugging the bank does not reset the total.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Build an actual slot: a cardboard tube or a 3D-printed funnel with the TCRT5000 staring across the throat. A short, narrow chute makes the detection rock-solid.\n- Add a \"session\" counter alongside the lifetime total - reset on a button press.\n- Switch the cheer text to count up the cash value if you only feed it one coin type (\"$0.25\", \"$0.50\"...).\n- Pipe the count to MQTT on the (eventually-booted) Orange Pi so the household can race each other to fill their banks.\n- Swap DO for AO (analog out) and look at how the reflected-light reading actually changes with coin distance - a great intro to ADCs.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nIt teaches the difference between a \"raw\" sensor reading and an \"event\". The TCRT5000 gives you a continuous on/off signal, but what you really want is one count per coin - which means edge detection, debouncing, and a tiny state machine. Those are the same three ideas that show up in every button, encoder, and motion sensor project, so once they click here they keep paying off.","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"Reads sensor, drives OLED, counts coins"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"Connects TCRT5000 module pins to the breadboard"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Breadboard rails and OLED/LED wiring"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1,"notes":"Blinks each time a coin is detected for visual confirmation"},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1,"notes":"Shows running coin total and a cheering message"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the ESP32, OLED, LED, and resistor"},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current-limiting resistor for the green LED"},{"item_name":"TCRT5000 IR Reflective Tracking Module","qty":1,"notes":"Detects coin passing through the slot via IR reflection"}]},{"id":52,"name":"Porch Night-Light (KY-018 dusk-to-dawn fader)","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"Arduino Uno powered from a laptop USB cable; the KY-018 and the LED both run from the Uno's 5V rail.","description":"**What it is**: Turn off the room lights and watch a little yellow LED slowly glow brighter, like a porch light waking up at sunset. Turn the lights back on and it fades away again, all by itself.\n\n**Why it's fun**: No button, no switch, no app - the light just KNOWS when it's dark. Cup your hand over the tiny sensor and you become the sunset.\n\nA pretend porch-light that turns on by itself when the room gets dark. The KY-018 photoresistor watches the ambient light; a yellow LED on the breadboard fades up as the room darkens and fades back down at sunrise - exactly how a real dusk-to-dawn porch bulb behaves, but built on the kitchen table.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- KY-018 Photoresistor Module (the \"eye\" - an LDR on a little PCB)\n- Arduino Uno R3 (5V, 10-bit ADC - perfect for an analog sensor)\n- 1x yellow 5mm LED (stands in for a porch bulb; warm yellow looks the part)\n- 1x 220R resistor (current limit for the LED)\n- Breadboard + a handful of jumpers (3x F-M for the KY-018 module pins, the rest M-M for the LED side)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe KY-018 has a 3-pin header that reads, in most clones, `S`, `+`, `-` (signal, power, ground). Use F-M jumpers straight onto those:\n\n- `S` -> Arduino `A0`\n- `+` -> Arduino `5V` (the KY-018 wants 5V, not 3.3V - good habit to lock in now, because when you move to the ESP32 you'll need to remember the ESP32's GPIO and Vcc are 3.3V, not 5V)\n- `-` -> Arduino `GND`\n\nThe LED lives on the breadboard:\n\n- Arduino `D9` (a PWM pin - the `~` symbol is on it) -> 220R resistor -> LED anode (long leg)\n- LED cathode (short leg) -> Arduino `GND`\n\nPin `D9` matters - the sketch fades the LED, and fading needs PWM. If you put it on `D7` instead it can only go full on or full off.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- Why an analog pin (`A0`), not a digital one. The KY-018 doesn't say \"dark / not dark\"; it says \"this much light\", as a voltage from 0 to 5V. The Uno's ADC converts that voltage into a number from 0 to 1023, and that's the number we map into \"how bright should the LED be\".\n- Why smoothing. If the porch bulb just looked at the raw reading and turned on whenever it crossed a threshold, every passing shadow would flicker it. The sketch keeps a running average (an exponential moving average, or EMA) so it reacts to \"the sun went down\" but ignores \"a hand briefly waved past\". This is the single most advanced trick in the sketch - everything else is map/constrain/analogWrite.\n- Why two thresholds (`DARK_START_PERCENT` and `DARK_FULL_PERCENT`) instead of one. Two thresholds give a gentle fade-in region instead of a snap. This is what a real dusk-to-dawn sensor does inside, and it's also what makes the project feel polished instead of toy-like.\n- Calibration at startup. KY-018 clones vary - some give a high reading in the dark, some give a low one, and the absolute values depend on the room. Sampling for ~3 seconds at boot while you cover-then-uncover the sensor lets the sketch figure out what \"normal\" looks like in your room, so you don't have to hardcode magic numbers. If you miss the prompt entirely, the sketch falls back to sensible defaults (200/900) so it still works.\n- The polarity knob. Some KY-018 clones read higher as it gets darker; others read lower. The sketch has one constant near the top, `DARK_IS_HIGH`, that picks which. If the porch light is on in daylight and off in the dark, flip it from `true` to `false` and re-upload. One flag, one line - the rest of the code adapts.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Swap the yellow LED for the blue or green one and watch the same logic work unchanged - the LED colour is \"front-end\", the logic is \"back-end\".\n- Cover the KY-018 with a cupped hand. The LED should fade up smoothly over half a second or so, not snap. If it snaps, increase `SMOOTHING` toward 0.2 (faster) or decrease it toward 0.01 (slower).\n- Add a second LED on `D10` (also PWM) that comes on only when it's *really* dark - call it the \"burglar deterrent\" porch flood.\n- Once Andy has soldered headers on a relay, the LED could be swapped for the 1-channel relay driving a real low-voltage lamp. (Project as written stays solder-free.)\n- The KY-018 vs the LM393 photoresistor module: the LM393 board has a comparator on it and gives you a *digital* dark / not-dark output (and a threshold knob). The KY-018 gives you the *analog* reading and lets you decide. That's why this project uses fading - you can't fade off a digital signal.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nThis is the cleanest possible example of \"sensor in, actu","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1,"notes":"5V brain with a 10-bit ADC - ideal for reading the KY-018's analog output."},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"Three wires from the KY-018 pin header to the Uno, plus Uno-to-breadboard rails."},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Short hops inside the breadboard (LED leg, resistor jumps, GND rail)."},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Yellow","qty":1,"notes":"Stands in for the porch bulb - warm yellow looks the part."},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Hosts the LED + resistor side of the circuit."},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current-limit resistor for the yellow LED on PWM pin D9."},{"item_name":"KY-018 Photoresistor Module","qty":1,"notes":"Main light sensor - LDR + voltage divider on a little PCB. Outputs an analog voltage on the S pin."}]},{"id":53,"name":"Laser Tripwire Alarm (LDR + KY-008 pair)","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"USB cable from a laptop or 5V phone charger into the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S; the board's onboard regulator feeds 3.3V to both the laser module and the photoresistor receiver.","description":"**What it is**: A red laser shoots a thin beam across a doorway and lands on a tiny sensor on the other side. Walk through the beam and a red alarm light snaps ON and stays on until you press the button to reset it.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Set it up across your bedroom door and you instantly know when a sibling sneaks in, even hours later, because the light stays lit until you clear it. It is the closest you can get to building a real spy-movie laser tripwire with stuff that fits on a breadboard.\n\n## Eye safety - read first\n\nThe KY-008 is a real 650nm laser diode module (laser-pointer class, typically rated under 5mW but still hazardous). Even brief direct exposure or a glint off a shiny surface can damage the retina, and the damage is painless and permanent. Before powering the board:\n\n- Never look into the beam or the emitter aperture, not even \"just to check it's on\". If you need to confirm it works, look at the red dot on a matte surface (a piece of paper, a wall, the white face of the LDR module).\n- Never aim the laser at a person, a pet, a window, a mirror, polished metal, or any glossy surface that could bounce a specular reflection back at someone.\n- Aim it once with the receiver taped down, then leave it. Treat it like a loaded staple gun: it's pointed somewhere specific, and you don't pick it up to \"see where it goes\" while live.\n- The sketch turns the laser ON as soon as the board boots. Plug power in only after both modules are pointed at each other and taped in place.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Photoresistor Module (LM393 comparator) - the receiver. It has a built-in pot to set the dark/light threshold and a clean digital DO output.\n- 1x KY-008 Red Laser Module (650nm) - the emitter. The narrow beam crosses the doorway and lands on the receiver.\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S - reads the receiver, controls the laser, latches the alarm.\n- 1x red 5mm LED + 220R resistor - \"the wire's been tripped\" indicator that stays on.\n- 1x tactile push-button - re-arm button to clear the latched alarm.\n- Breadboard + DuPont jumpers.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nReminder: the laser turns on the moment the ESP32 boots. Finish all wiring, point the laser at the receiver, and tape both modules down BEFORE you plug the USB cable in.\n\n- Both modules run on 3.3V from the ESP32 (the laser module is happy at 3.3V; it's a touch dimmer than at 5V but still very visible at room range).\n- The LM393 receiver module has 4 pins: VCC, GND, DO (digital out), AO (analog, ignored here). Only DO is connected to the ESP32.\n- The DO pin is **active LOW when bright**. With the laser hitting the LDR, DO reads LOW. When something blocks the beam, DO goes HIGH. The code reflects this exactly.\n- The KY-008 has 3 pins in a row. On this clone they are:\n  - **S** (signal) -> GPIO 23 on the ESP32. Drive HIGH to turn the beam on.\n  - **middle pin** -> **NC, leave disconnected**. Do not feed 3V3 here; it is not a power input on this module.\n  - **-** (GND) -> GND rail.\n- The LED goes from GPIO 22 through a 220R resistor to GND. Standard ESP32 LED hookup.\n- The re-arm button connects GPIO 4 to GND. The internal pull-up does the rest.\n\n**Aiming and calibration**\n\n1. With the USB cable still unplugged, lay out the laser and the receiver across the doorway and tape them down so they can't drift. Point the emitter at the white LDR window on the receiver.\n2. Plug in USB. The laser turns on automatically. Do not look at the emitter; look at the receiver to confirm the red dot landed on the white window.\n3. Turn the blue trim-pot on the receiver module slowly. Its onboard signal LED will flip ON when dark, OFF when bright. Find the spot where the laser-on state is **just barely** counted as \"bright\" (LED just off). That's your most sensitive setting.\n4. Wave a finger through the beam (not your eye). The red alarm LED should latch on, and serial should print `>>> TRIPWIRE BROKEN <<<`.\n5. Press the button to re-arm.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **A tripwire is just a \"before vs after\" test.** The laser doesn't measure anything fancy. It just gives the receiver one easy thing to see. When that one thing disappears, we know something passed through.\n- **Why latch the alarm?** A real intruder is only in the beam for a few milliseconds. If the LED only lit while the beam was broken, you'd never see it. Latching means: once tripped, stay tripped until a human acknowledges it.\n- **Two modules talking through air.** There are no wires between the laser and the receiver - the beam itself is the signal. Tha","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"Brain: reads the photoresistor DO pin, drives the laser pin, and drives the alarm LED"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"One pack covers ESP32 header to breadboard and the two module pin headers"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"One pack covers all the short breadboard hops (LED, button, rails)"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"Alarm indicator that latches ON when the beam is broken"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the LED, resistor, button, and the two module headers"},{"item_name":"KY-008 Red Laser Module (650nm)","qty":1,"notes":"Emitter: shoots the narrow red beam across the doorway"},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current-limit resistor for the red alarm LED"},{"item_name":"Photoresistor Module (LM393 comparator)","qty":1,"notes":"Receiver: detects when the laser beam is interrupted (digital DO output)"},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x4.3mm (white pin)","qty":1,"notes":"Re-arm button: press to clear the latched alarm"}]},{"id":54,"name":"Coin Drop Counter (optical slot + OLED)","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"USB cable from a laptop or 5V phone charger into the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S USB-C port; the ESP32's onboard regulator feeds 3.3V to the slot sensor and OLED.","description":"**What it is**: Drop a coin through a little slot. The number on the tiny screen jumps up by one, instantly, every single time. Drop ten coins fast and watch it climb to ten without missing a single one.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It feels like the screen is reading your mind because it counts faster than your eye can follow. Challenge your dad to drop five coins faster than you can, then point at the screen when it caught every single one.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Optical Slot / Speed Sensor (the protagonist - an IR LED facing a phototransistor across a small gap)\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S\n- 1x 0.96\" OLED Display (I2C, SSD1306)\n- 1x Breadboard\n- A handful of male-male and female-male jumpers\n- Coins, popsicle-stick wedges, or strips of paper to drop through the slot\n\nNo resistors needed - the slot sensor module already has its own pull-up and current-limit resistors on board, and the OLED is I2C with internal pull-ups on the breakout. No LEDs in this build, so no current-limiting resistor either.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe slot sensor has 4 pins: `VCC`, `GND`, `D0` (digital out), and sometimes `A0` (analog). We only care about `D0`. When nothing is in the slot, the beam reaches the phototransistor and `D0` sits HIGH. The instant something blocks the beam, `D0` snaps LOW. That falling edge is what we count.\n\n- Sensor `VCC` -> ESP32 `3V3`\n- Sensor `GND` -> ESP32 `GND`\n- Sensor `D0` -> ESP32 `GPIO 4`\n- OLED `VCC` -> ESP32 `3V3`\n- OLED `GND` -> ESP32 `GND`\n- OLED `SDA` -> ESP32 `GPIO 21`\n- OLED `SCL` -> ESP32 `GPIO 22`\n\nOrient the slot **vertically** so coins fall down through the gap under gravity - tape the sensor module to the edge of a book or shoebox with the U-shape opening pointing up. A horizontal slot won't work; coins need somewhere to fall.\n\nIf drops sometimes don't register even though they clearly passed through the slot, try moving the sensor `VCC` wire from `3V3` to `VIN` (the 5V USB rail on the NodeMCU). The LM393 comparator on these slot modules is spec'd 3.3-5V, but some clones produce a cleaner, sharper falling edge with a full 5V supply. The `D0` output is still safe to feed into a 3.3V ESP32 GPIO - it's just a slow logic-level signal pulled to whatever rail the module runs on, and ESP32 inputs tolerate that fine in practice for this use.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- \"What is an interrupt?\" Most code in a loop is busy checking things. An interrupt is the chip dropping everything the moment a pin changes, running a tiny piece of code, then jumping back. For something as fast as a coin falling through a 5 mm gap, polling in `loop()` could miss it. An interrupt cannot.\n- \"Why a `volatile` keyword?\" The compiler usually assumes nothing else changes a variable behind its back. `volatile` tells it \"no, this can change at any moment because an interrupt might touch it\" so it always re-reads from memory.\n- \"What's debounce?\" A coin doesn't break the beam cleanly - it can flicker the signal a few times in a few milliseconds. We ignore any second trigger that comes within 40 ms of the previous one.\n- \"What does `readSnapshot()` do?\" An interrupt can fire halfway through `loop()` reading the shared variables, which would give us a torn, half-updated value. `readSnapshot()` briefly turns interrupts off, copies everything in one go, then turns interrupts back on. That's a \"critical section\".\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Tape a piece of card with evenly-spaced slits to the side of a spinning fan and you've turned the same sensor into a tachometer (RPM meter). Count slits per second, divide by slit count, multiply by 60.\n- Add the buzzer from the kit (or a piezo if you grab one) so each coin makes a beep.\n- Make a 10-second window: how many coins can you drop in 10 seconds? Reset on button press.\n- Log each drop's timestamp to the Serial Monitor and paste into a spreadsheet to graph drop intervals.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nThis is the first build in the kit where timing accuracy matters. Polling in `loop()` works fine for buttons and slow sensors, but the moment you start measuring fast physical events - a coin drop, a fan blade, a moving belt - you need interrupts. Once Andy sees the count tick up reliably even when he drops three coins in quick succession, the \"why use an interrupt\" lesson sticks. Same hardware later becomes the speedometer for a Lego car, the slot encoder on a robot wheel, or a beam-break alarm across a doorway.","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"Reads the sensor digital output via interrupt, drives the OLED"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"Reaches from breadboard to the sensor module pins"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Power/ground/I2C rails on the breadboard"},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1,"notes":"Shows live count, time since last drop, last gap, and best gap"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Mounts the ESP32 and OLED for tidy wiring"},{"item_name":"Optical Slot / Speed Sensor","qty":1,"notes":"Beam-break detector - the protagonist; coins (or fingers, cards) interrupting the slot are counted"}]},{"id":55,"name":"Stealth Timer - hold still or the LED dies","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S powered over its USB-C cable from a laptop or a 5 V USB phone charger; the PIR sensor, OLED and LEDs all run from the ESP32's onboard 5 V and 3.3 V rails.","description":"**What it is**: Stand in front of the little sensor and freeze. A green LED glows and the screen counts how many seconds you hold still. Twitch even a tiny bit and the red LED flashes - your streak resets to zero.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It's a real-life \"don't blink\" game you built yourself, and the sensor can spot a finger wiggle from across the room. Challenge your dad, your sister, or the dog - whoever holds the longest streak wins.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x HC-SR505 Mini PIR Motion Sensor (the star: a tiny PIR with a clear plastic dome that fires HIGH when it sees a warm body move)\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S\n- 1x 0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)\n- 1x 5mm green LED (glows while you are holding still)\n- 1x 5mm red LED (flashes when you twitch)\n- 2x 330R resistors (one in series with each LED)\n- 1x breadboard, plus a handful of M-M and F-M jumpers\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe HC-SR505 has only three pins: `VCC`, `OUT`, `GND`. It accepts 5 V on VCC and its OUT line idles at 0 V and pulses HIGH (about 3.0 V on this module) when triggered, which the ESP32's 3.3 V GPIO can read safely without a level shifter.\n\nPower the PIR and the OLED from the ESP32's `5V` pin (which is the USB rail when you have the board plugged into your laptop). Tie every ground - PIR, OLED, both LEDs - back to the ESP32's `GND`.\n\nThe OLED is I2C and goes on the ESP32's default I2C pins: `SDA = GPIO 21`, `SCL = GPIO 22`. The PIR's OUT goes to `GPIO 13`. The green LED's anode goes through a 330R resistor to `GPIO 18`, the red LED's anode goes through its own 330R resistor to `GPIO 19`, and both cathodes go to ground.\n\nImportant first-time gotcha: the HC-SR505 needs about 30 seconds after power-up to settle - it will output garbage during that window. The sketch handles this for you by showing a \"Warming up PIR\" screen and slow-pulsing the red LED. Stay out of view while that's running.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The HC-SR505 doesn't see *light*. It sees *infrared heat moving across its field of view*. That's why holding still in front of it - even with your eyes open - makes it go quiet.\n- Inside that little plastic dome is a Fresnel lens. It splits the view into zones; the sensor only triggers when warmth crosses from one zone into another.\n- The OUT pin is digital, not analog. There's no \"how much motion\" reading - just yes or no.\n- The ESP32 is reading the pin millions of times per second, but we only act once per loop with a tiny delay - that's enough.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add a buzzer that beeps softly the longer your streak gets, so you have to hold still through rising tension.\n- Show a tiny \"high score\" bar graph on the OLED that grows with your best time.\n- Save the best streak to ESP32 flash (Preferences library) so it survives a reboot.\n- Add a second HC-SR505 aimed sideways and only count \"frontal\" motion - turns the game into a stealth competition for two people.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nPIR sensors are the everyday motion detector you've seen a thousand times - in burglar alarms, in motion-activated lights at the supermarket, in automatic taps. The HC-SR505 is the tiniest, cheapest version of that hardware, and it has exactly one job: tell you whether a warm thing just moved. Turning that single bit of information into a little game is a satisfying way to feel how a real sensor behaves: the warm-up window, the lens zones, the way it doesn't care about a glass of hot water but cares a lot about your hand.","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"Reads the PIR, drives OLED + LED"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"From ESP32 header pins to breadboard rows"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Breadboard rails to ESP32 and between rows"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1,"notes":"Glows steady while you are holding still"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"Flashes when motion breaks the streak"},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1,"notes":"Shows current still time and best record"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the LEDs, resistors and PIR pin header"},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":2,"notes":"One per LED, current limiting"},{"item_name":"HC-SR505 Mini PIR Motion Sensor","qty":1,"notes":"Main sensor - detects any motion in front of it"}]},{"id":56,"name":"Storm Glass - 3-hour pressure trend forecaster","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S powered from a laptop / phone-charger USB cable; the ESP32's 3.3 V pin supplies both the BMP280 and the OLED through the breadboard rails.","description":"**What it is**: A little screen sits on your desk showing today's air pressure, a giant up or down arrow, and one word like \"Fair\" or \"Storm!\" The arrow flips direction hours before the weather actually changes outside.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You can predict the weather before the weather app does, then run around the house yelling \"I told you it would rain!\" Pick it up and walk upstairs and you can literally watch the pressure drop, because you just climbed away from a tiny bit of the sky.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- **GY-BMP280 Barometric Pressure Sensor** - the protagonist. A tiny Bosch chip that measures absolute air pressure to about +-1 Pa. Pressure is what weather forecasters actually watch: rising pressure usually means fair weather coming, falling pressure means a storm coming.\n- **ESP32 NodeMCU-32S** - reads the sensor and drives the screen.\n- **0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)** - shows the current pressure, a big up/down arrow, and a one-word forecast.\n- **Breadboard** plus **M-M** and **F-M jumper wires** - both modules share the same two I2C wires (SDA + SCL) plus 3.3 V and GND, so the breadboard just acts as a 4-rail splitter.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nBoth modules are I2C. That means four wires total from the ESP32, no matter how many I2C devices you add:\n\n- ESP32 `3V3` -> breadboard `+` rail -> BMP280 `VCC` and OLED `VCC`\n- ESP32 `GND` -> breadboard `-` rail -> BMP280 `GND` and OLED `GND`\n- ESP32 `GPIO21` (SDA) -> BMP280 `SDA` and OLED `SDA`\n- ESP32 `GPIO22` (SCL) -> BMP280 `SCL` and OLED `SCL`\n\nBoth boards already have I2C pull-up resistors on them, so no extra resistors are needed.\n\nThe BMP280 sits at I2C address `0x76` on most GY-BMP280 clones. A few clones are at `0x77`. The code tries both so you do not have to care.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **What is air pressure?** The weight of all the air above you, pressing down. At sea level on an average day it is about 1013 hPa. When a storm front moves in, that number drops; when it leaves, it rises.\n- **Why a 3-hour delta?** Weather services use the 3-hour pressure tendency as the basic forecast signal. One single reading tells you nothing - it is the *change over time* that predicts the weather.\n- **Why a ring buffer?** We keep 180 readings (one per minute for 3 hours). Instead of growing a list forever, we overwrite the oldest slot every minute. This is the same trick used in audio buffers and game replay systems.\n- **I2C is a shared bus.** The OLED and the BMP280 use the *same* two wires. They get along because they have different addresses (`0x3C` vs `0x76`).\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add a 24-hour delta too (just bump `HISTORY_MINUTES` to 1440 and add a second arrow).\n- Plot the history as a tiny line graph across the bottom of the OLED.\n- Publish the pressure to MQTT once the Orange Pi hub is online, so the Hallway Weather Window project can show it alongside temperature.\n- Calibrate to altitude: feed in the local altitude and the sketch can spit out sea-level-equivalent pressure that matches what weather.gov reports.\n- Add a piezo buzzer that beeps when the forecast crosses into \"Storm!\" so you get a warning even when you are not looking at the screen.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nThis is a real instrument, not a toy. The BMP280 is sensitive enough that you can *see* the pressure change when you walk up a flight of stairs (about -0.4 hPa per 4 metres). And the 3-hour delta really does predict weather - leave it on the kitchen counter for a week and you will start trusting the arrow more than the phone app.\n\nIt is also the cleanest example in the kit of the I2C-bus idea: two completely different chips, from two different vendors, sharing two wires. Once Andy is comfortable with this, adding a third or fourth I2C sensor is just plugging it into the same rails.","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"Reads sensor and drives OLED over I2C"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"ESP32 header pins out to the breadboard rails"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Power and I2C rails between ESP32, breadboard, sensor and OLED"},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1,"notes":"Shows pressure value, trend arrow and storm/clear forecast"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds both I2C modules and shares power rails"},{"item_name":"GY-BMP280 Barometric Pressure Sensor","qty":1,"notes":"Protagonist - reads pressure/temperature over I2C"}]},{"id":57,"name":"Pocket Battery Health Monitor (OLED + LEDs)","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"ESP32 powered by laptop USB. OLED runs from the ESP32's 3.3V rail; LEDs are driven by GPIO. The voltage module is passive (just resistors) and draws no power. The battery under test connects only to the module's input screws.","description":"**What it is**: Touch the little board's two screws to any battery's plus and minus ends. The tiny screen instantly shows the volts, a bar graph, and one big word: HEALTHY, LOW, or DEAD. A green LED or a red LED lights up too, so you know the answer from across the room.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You become the battery doctor of the house. Go hunting through every drawer, remote, and toy, zap each battery with the probes, and sort them into a \"still good\" pile and a \"throw away\" pile - your parents will actually thank you.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- Voltage Sensor Module (0-25V divider) - the star: a tiny board with two resistors (30k + 7.5k) that shrinks a DC voltage down to something the ESP32's 3.3V ADC can survive. The module is rated up to 25V at the input, but as you'll see in Talking points, the ESP32's ADC only stays linear up to about 12V of input. Above that the screen number goes wonky.\n- ESP32 NodeMCU-32S - reads the divider's output on GPIO 34 and drives the screen\n- 0.96\" OLED Display (I2C) - shows the live number, a bar graph, and the word HEALTHY / LOW / DEAD\n- 1x green LED with a 220R limiter and 1x red LED with a 330R limiter - instant \"go / no-go\" feedback even before you look at the screen. Green gets the smaller resistor on purpose - green LEDs need a higher forward voltage than red, so at 3.3V drive a 330R would leave it visibly dim.\n- Breadboard + jumpers - no soldering\n\nA 9V battery (or any low-voltage DC source up to ~12V you want to measure) is the **device under test**. You touch its terminals to the voltage module's input. The whole project itself is powered through the ESP32's USB cable. The voltage module is a passive resistor pair, so it draws no power of its own and does not need a VCC connection.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe voltage module has two sides:\n\n- **Screw-terminal side (input):** the thing you measure. The screws are labelled `VCC` (positive) and `GND` (negative). Andy connects the battery being tested here.\n- **Pin-header side (output):** three pins labelled `S` (signal, the divided-down voltage), `+` (unused, leave alone), and `-` (ground). The `-` pin **must share ground with the ESP32** or the reading is meaningless.\n\nSo the wiring story is: battery `+` and `-` go into the screw terminals. `S` goes to ESP32 GPIO 34. The module's `-` pin goes to ESP32 GND. Same-ground is the rule that makes the whole thing work.\n\nOLED is normal I2C: VCC to 3.3V (most 0.96\" I2C OLEDs have an onboard regulator and accept 3.3-5V; the ESP32's 3.3V rail is fine), GND to GND, SDA to GPIO 21, SCL to GPIO 22.\n\nThe green LED gets a 220R resistor in series with its anode (long leg). The red LED gets a 330R. Cathodes both to GND.\n\n**Do not** feed more than 25V into the module, and **do not** ever connect AC, mains, or the wall. This project is for batteries and bench DC supplies only.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The voltage module is just a **voltage divider**: two resistors in series. The \"S\" pin sits at the junction and carries a fraction of the input voltage. With 30k on top and 7.5k on the bottom, the ratio is 5:1 - feed 25V in, get 5V at S. Feed 9V in, get 1.8V at S. The ESP32 then multiplies by 5 in software to recover the original number.\n- **Why the real measurement ceiling is ~12V, not 25V.** With the attenuation setting we use (`ADC_11db`), the ESP32 ADC is only linear from about 0.15V up to about 2.45V at the pin. Multiply 2.45V by the divider's 5:1 ratio and you get ~12V at the input. Above that, the ADC saturates and the displayed number flattens out - the module isn't damaged, but the reading is no longer trustworthy. The code shows a `>12V (clip)` warning when that happens. For 9V alkalines, 12V SLA at rest, and 18650 cells, you're comfortably inside the linear band.\n- Why GPIO 34? It's an **input-only ADC pin** on ESP32 and it's also on ADC1, which (unlike ADC2) keeps working while WiFi is on. Good habit for future projects.\n- The ESP32 ADC is famously a little non-linear, so the code includes a `CAL` fudge factor (default 1.00). Once everything works, Andy can compare the OLED reading to a real multimeter on the same battery, and nudge `CAL` until they agree **inside the band he actually cares about** (e.g. 6V-10V for testing 9V alkalines).\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Change `HEALTHY_VOLTS` to suit a different battery. For a 12V lead-acid you'd use ~12.2V (right at the edge of the linear range, so multimeter-calibrate carefully). For a single Li-ion 18650 cell you'd use ~3.5V (well inside the safe ran","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"Reads divider output on GPIO 34 and drives OLED via I2C"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"Connects the voltage module's screw-terminal pigtail and the OLED header to the breadboard"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Board-to-board signal and power links"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1,"notes":"Lights when measured voltage is in the healthy band (>= 7.0V)"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"Lights when measured voltage is low (< 7.0V) - instant visual warning"},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1,"notes":"Shows live voltage number, bar graph, and status word"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the ESP32, OLED, voltage module, and LEDs"},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current limiter for the green LED (green Vf ~3.0V wants a smaller resistor at 3.3V drive)"},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current limiter for the red LED (red Vf ~2.0V is fine with 330R)"},{"item_name":"Voltage Sensor Module (0-25V divider)","qty":1,"notes":"Star of the show - 5:1 resistor divider so up to ~12V at the input maps to a value the ESP32 ADC can read linearly"}]},{"id":58,"name":"Raindrop Reporter - live wetness on OLED","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"Powered from a USB-C cable plugged into the ESP32 NodeMCU-32S. The board's 3V3 pin supplies the raindrop sensor module, the OLED, and the LED.","description":"**What it is**: Flick water onto a little comb-shaped board and the tiny screen instantly changes from DRY to MISTY to WET to SOAKED, with a blue LED that glows brighter the wetter it gets. There's a wiggling bar on the screen too, so you can watch it climb in real time as the water sits there.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It's a weather detector you build yourself - leave it on the windowsill and you'll know the second rain hits, before anyone else in the house. Try licking your finger and touching the board, or breathing on it really close - you'll see how sensitive it is and feel like a mad scientist running tests.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Raindrop Sensor (the comb board + its little LM393 amplifier board)\n- 1x ESP32 NodeMCU-32S\n- 1x 0.96\" OLED display (I2C, address 0x3C)\n- 1x Blue 5mm LED\n- 1x 330R resistor (current limit for the LED)\n- 1x Breadboard\n- Jumper wires (a mix of M-M and F-M)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe raindrop sensor comes in two pieces joined by a 2-wire lead: a flat comb-trace board (the bit that gets wet) and a small amplifier board with the 4-pin header. The amplifier board is where everything plugs in.\n\n- Sensor `VCC` -> ESP32 `3V3`. The module is happy at 3.3V; do NOT use 5V here even though the silkscreen allows it - the analog output would swing past what the ESP32 ADC tolerates.\n- Sensor `GND` -> ESP32 `GND`.\n- Sensor `AO` (analog out) -> ESP32 `GPIO34`. GPIO34 is input-only and lives on ADC1, which keeps working while WiFi is on - a small but important detail on the ESP32.\n- Ignore the `DO` (digital out) pin. The on-board pot only sets the threshold for `DO`; we read the analog signal directly so we don't have to twist the trimmer.\n- OLED: `VCC -> 3V3`, `GND -> GND`, `SDA -> GPIO21`, `SCL -> GPIO22`. These are the ESP32's default I2C pins.\n- Blue LED: long leg (anode) goes through the 330R resistor to `GPIO5`, short leg (cathode) to `GND`.\n\nThe raindrop sensor is \"wet = low value, dry = high value\". That's because the comb traces work as a variable resistor: water bridges them, the resistance drops, and the on-board divider pulls AO toward ground.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- Why GPIO34? Show Sandy the ESP32 pinout. ADC2 pins go quiet whenever WiFi turns on - we picked an ADC1 pin so we don't get bitten by that later.\n- The `classify()` thresholds were guessed. The real ones come from watching the Serial Monitor while flicking water on the board: write down the value when dry, when finger-damp, when a drop is sitting on it, and when it's fully submerged. Edit the four constants at the top to match.\n- Averaging 8 samples is a tiny but useful trick - one stray reading would make the display jitter between MISTY and WET every quarter second otherwise.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Add a buzzer that beeps when state crosses into SOAKED - then it's a basement-flood alarm.\n- Log the state changes with `millis()` over WiFi to the (future) Orange Pi MQTT broker, so you've got a record of when it rained.\n- Use the `DO` pin instead of `AO` and skip the OLED - simpler \"rain or no rain\" version for the XIAO ESP32-C3.\n- Make the wetness bar pulse faster the wetter it gets - visible feedback without needing to read numbers.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nThe raindrop sensor is the cheapest physical-world-touching sensor you have - it's literally two interleaved copper combs and a comparator. This project shows the full beginner-friendly chain: a noisy real-world analog signal, smoothed by averaging, classified into human-readable buckets, then displayed two different ways (text + bar graph + LED). It's also the simplest hook into \"act on the world\": once you can confidently detect rain, the same code pattern works for soil moisture, water level, and any other resistive/analog sensor in the kit.\n","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"Reads the sensor and drives the OLED"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":6,"notes":"ESP32 pins to sensor module + OLED module headers"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":7,"notes":"Power rails and LED leg jumps on the breadboard"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Blue","qty":1,"notes":"Visual rain alert - lights up when wet"},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1,"notes":"Shows live reading + rain status text"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the LED + resistor, distributes 3.3V/GND"},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current limit for the blue LED"},{"item_name":"Raindrop Sensor","qty":1,"notes":"Star of the show. Analog output to GPIO34"}]},{"id":59,"name":"Tub Filler Traffic Light","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"Arduino Uno powered from a laptop USB cable. The Uno's onboard 5V rail then feeds the water sensor, the LCD, and the LED resistors.","description":"**What it is**: Dip a little probe into a glass of water. A screen shows a bar graph filling up, plus a percent. Three LEDs glow like a stoplight - red when it's nearly empty, yellow when it's getting close, green when the \"tub\" is full.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It reacts instantly. Lift the probe out and the bar drops; push it back in and the green light pops on. It feels like a real gadget you'd see stuck to the side of a bathtub or a fish tank, and you built it.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- Arduino Uno R3 (the brain - reads the sensor, drives the LCD and LEDs)\n- Water Level Sensor (resistive comb - the protagonist; more water touching the combs = lower resistance = higher analog reading)\n- LCD 1602 with I2C Backpack (shows a live bar graph and a percent number)\n- 3 LEDs (red, yellow, green) - \"stoplight\" progress indicator\n- 3x 330R resistors (current-limiters, one per LED)\n- 1x 1M resistor (pull-down on the sensor's signal pin so the reading falls cleanly to 0 when dry)\n- Breadboard + a small handful of M-M and F-M jumper wires (one pack of each is plenty)\n- A glass of water (the \"tub\") to dip the sensor into\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n- The water sensor has three pins labelled `+`, `-`, `S`. `+` goes to Arduino 5V, `-` goes to Arduino GND, and `S` is the analog output that goes to Arduino A0.\n- The 1M pull-down resistor goes from A0 to GND. This is the \"why doesn't it read zero when dry?\" fix - without it, the floating input drifts and shows random numbers when there's no water.\n- LCD I2C backpack: only 4 wires. VCC -> 5V, GND -> GND, SDA -> A4, SCL -> A5. (On the Uno, A4 and A5 double as the I2C bus.)\n- LEDs: each LED's long leg (anode) goes through a 330R resistor to its Arduino pin. Short leg (cathode) goes to GND. Red on D5, Yellow on D6, Green on D7.\n- IMPORTANT - do not submerge the top half of the water sensor board. Only the gold combs should ever touch water. The exposed components above will corrode in days if they get wet.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- Why a \"resistive comb\" sensor? It's just two interleaved bare-copper tracks. Water between them conducts a tiny bit of current. More water covering the combs = more conductive paths in parallel = more current = bigger number from `analogRead`. It's the same trick the Soil Moisture Sensor uses, just shaped to read a vertical column instead of a flat patch.\n- Why the 1M pull-down? An Arduino analog input that isn't connected to anything acts like a tiny antenna - it picks up mains hum and reads random numbers. Tying it weakly to ground through 1M forces it to read 0 when the sensor is dry, but lets the sensor easily overpower it when water is present.\n- The LCD updates fast on purpose - you can dip the sensor in and out and watch the bar graph respond immediately. That tight feedback loop is what makes it fun.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Calibrate `WET_MAX` to your actual glass of water. Dip the sensor all the way and read the value over Serial - that's your real max. Beginner-typical values land between 500 and 700.\n- Add a buzzer that beeps once when the green \"FULL\" zone is reached - that turns it from a visual toy into an actual \"tub is full, come turn the tap off\" alarm.\n- Swap to the ESP32 and publish the level over WiFi to the (future) Orange Pi hub. Same sensor wiring, different brain.\n- Try the Raindrop Sensor on the same code with no changes - it's electrically identical, just a flatter comb shape.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nThis is the cheapest, simplest possible \"is there water there?\" sensor, and it teaches a real-world lesson the first time you use it: analog inputs don't read clean zero when nothing's connected. Fixing that with a pull-down resistor is a habit that carries over to almost every analog sensor you'll ever use. And visually, dipping a probe into a glass and watching a bar graph fill up is hugely satisfying - especially for a 10-year-old.","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1,"notes":"Brain; reads A0, drives LCD over I2C, lights the 3 LEDs"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"One pack covers sensor (3) and LCD (4) board-header connections"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"One pack covers breadboard rails, LED legs, Uno-to-breadboard hops"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1,"notes":"Full tier indicator - ~80% full"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"First tier indicator - lights at any water contact"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Yellow","qty":1,"notes":"Mid tier indicator - ~40% full"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the LEDs, resistors, and rails"},{"item_name":"LCD 1602 with I2C Backpack","qty":1,"notes":"Live bar graph + numeric percent readout"},{"item_name":"Resistor 1M (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Pull-down from A0 to GND so the sensor reads clean 0 when dry"},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":3,"notes":"Current limiting, one per LED"},{"item_name":"Water Level Sensor","qty":1,"notes":"The protagonist - dipped in a glass of water; resistive comb output to A0"}]},{"id":61,"name":"NE555 Touch-and-Hold Lamp (monostable one-shot)","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"5 V DC from any USB source (phone charger, laptop USB-A, or your TP4056 + 18650 pack) - the NE555 happily runs from 4.5-15 V but 5 V is the friendliest.","description":"**What it is**: Tap the button once and the yellow LED snaps on for about 11 seconds, then shuts off by itself. You can let go right away - the light keeps glowing on its own little timer.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It is like a tiny bathroom light that turns itself off, except YOU built it from a chip and a handful of parts. Race a stopwatch against it, or dare your dad to guess exactly when the LED will blink off.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x NE555 Timer IC (the brain - in monostable / one-shot mode this time)\n- 1x Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x5mm (white pin) - the trigger\n- 1x LED 5mm Yellow (lights up for the timed duration)\n- 1x Resistor 470R - in series with the LED, limits current so the LED does not burn out\n- 1x Resistor 100K - the \"R\" in the timing equation T = 1.1 * R * C\n- 1x Capacitor 100uF electrolytic - the \"C\" in the same equation\n- 1x Resistor 10K - pull-up so the trigger pin idles HIGH until the button presses it LOW\n- 1x Capacitor 1uF 50V - decoupling cap across the 555's power pins, keeps the chip from glitching\n- 1x Breadboard\n- Jumper wires (M-M)\n\nPower: 5 V from a USB cable plugged into any USB power source (phone charger, laptop USB-A, or the TP4056 + 18650 setup you already have). The 555 happily runs anywhere from 4.5 V to 15 V, but 5 V is the friendliest for a beginner project.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nNE555 pinout (looking at the chip from the top with the notch / dot at the LEFT):\n\n```\n            +----------+\n   GND  1 --|.       8 |-- Vcc (+5V)\n   TRIG 2 --|        7 |-- DISCHARGE\n   OUT  3 --|        6 |-- THRESHOLD\n   RESET 4 -|        5 |-- CONTROL\n            +----------+\n```\n\nConnections:\n\n1. **Pin 1 (GND)** to the breadboard's blue/GND rail.\n2. **Pin 8 (Vcc)** to the red/+5V rail. Also put the 1uF decoupling cap directly across pins 1 and 8 (negative lead to GND).\n3. **Pins 6 and 7 tied together** at one junction. That same junction connects to the **bottom** of the 100K resistor and to the **positive (+) lead of the 100uF cap**. The **top** of the 100K resistor goes to **+5V**. The negative lead of the cap goes to GND. (So pins 6, 7, the cap-plus, and one end of the 100K are all the same node.)\n4. Watch capacitor polarity - the white stripe / short leg is the negative side and goes to GND.\n5. **Pin 2 (TRIG)** connects to the 10K pull-up resistor, other end of which goes to +5V. So pin 2 sits at +5V by default.\n6. **One side of the push-button** connects to pin 2; **the other side** connects to GND. Pressing the button briefly pulls pin 2 to GND - that is the \"trigger\".\n7. **Pin 4 (RESET)** to +5V (so the chip never gets reset accidentally - leaving this floating causes weird behavior).\n8. **Pin 5 (CONTROL)** can be left unconnected for now (some textbooks add a 10nF cap here but it is not required).\n9. **Pin 3 (OUT)** to the **long leg (anode)** of the yellow LED. **Short leg (cathode)** of LED to the 470R resistor. Other end of 470R to GND.\n\nMath sanity check: T = 1.1 * R * C = 1.1 * 100,000 ohms * 0.000100 F = **11.0 seconds**. Press the button, the LED lights for about 11 seconds, then snaps off all at once.\n\n## Talking points\n\n**Monostable means \"one stable state\".** The 555 is sitting quietly with its output LOW and the timing cap held empty (because the internal discharge transistor on pin 7 is shorting it to ground). It will sit like that forever. That is the \"stable state\".\n\n**What does the button actually do?** The button pulls pin 2 (the TRIG input) below 1/3 of Vcc - briefly. That is the magic threshold. The instant the chip sees that, it flips the output HIGH, releases the discharge transistor, and now the cap can finally start charging up through the 100K resistor.\n\n**How does it know when to stop?** Pin 6 (THRESHOLD) is watching the same cap. When the voltage on the cap climbs past 2/3 of Vcc, pin 6 trips, and the chip snaps the output back LOW and re-shorts the cap to GND. Reset. Ready for the next press.\n\n**Why 1.1?** That number is not magic - it comes from the math of charging a capacitor through a resistor from 0V to 2/3 of Vcc. The formula t = -RC * ln(1 - 2/3) works out to about 1.0986 * RC. Engineers rounded it to 1.1 because that is plenty precise given that real-world electrolytic caps drift +/- 20% anyway.\n\n**Why \"touch-AND-hold\"?** Because letting go of the button does nothing! The 555 has latched and is going to time out on its own schedule. Holding the button down longer does not extend the time. Pressing the button again while the LED is still on also does nothing - the 5","allocations":[{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Pack of jumpers to wire up the breadboard. (Optional Arduino test rig also needs F-M jumpers from your separate pack - not allocated here.)"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1,"notes":"Timing capacitor C - charges from 0V to 2/3 Vcc to define the pulse (the C in T=1.1RC)"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 1uF 50V (electrolytic)","qty":1,"notes":"Power-rail decoupling capacitor between Vcc and GND - keeps the 555 stable"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Yellow","qty":1,"notes":"Output indicator - lights up for the duration of the timed pulse"},{"item_name":"NE555 Timer IC","qty":1,"notes":"Wired in monostable (one-shot) mode - the heart of the timer"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Build platform - no soldering. (Optional Arduino test rig uses a second breadboard from inventory qty=2, not allocated here.)"},{"item_name":"Resistor 100K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Timing resistor R - charges the cap through it (the R in T=1.1RC)"},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Pull-up on the trigger pin (pin 2) so it idles HIGH until the button presses it LOW"},{"item_name":"Resistor 470R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current-limiting resistor in series with the yellow LED"},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x5mm (white pin)","qty":1,"notes":"Trigger button - briefly pulls pin 2 LOW to start the timing cycle"}]},{"id":62,"name":"Bump Buzzer (NE555 + SW-420)","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"5V from an Arduino Uno's USB rail jumpered to the breadboard +5V and GND rails (the Uno acts as a USB-to-5V converter; no firmware needs to run on it for the alarm to work).","description":"**What it is**: Bump the table and a red light snaps on while a buzzer screams for about ten seconds, then it goes quiet and waits for the next bump. No code, no buttons - just touch the desk and it freaks out.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Hide it under your little brother's chair, or tape it to the cookie jar, and you get an instant booby trap that catches anyone who sneaks up. The best part is you can tune how jumpy it is with a tiny screwdriver - too touchy and a footstep sets it off, too chill and you can shake the table without a peep.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x NE555 Timer IC - the brain, configured as a monostable one-shot\n- 1x SW-420 Vibration Switch Module - the trigger\n- 1x 220K resistor - NE555 timing R\n- 1x 47uF electrolytic - NE555 timing C\n- 1x 10K resistor - pull-up on NE555 TRIG (pin 2)\n- 1x 330R resistor - LED current limiter\n- 1x 100uF electrolytic - rail decoupling next to the NE555\n- 1x Red 5mm LED - visual \"ALARM ACTIVE\" indicator\n- 1x Active piezo buzzer (from the spare-parts drawer - the kind with a sticker that says \"5V\" on the back; it self-oscillates when you give it DC). If you do not have one, swap it for any item you can drive with a digital HIGH and the LED-and-330R alone is still a quiet test alarm.\n- Breadboard, jumper wires\n- Arduino Uno - used ONLY as a USB-powered 5V source (no firmware running the alarm)\n\nNote: this design deliberately does NOT use the LM386 audio amplifier. Earlier drafts paired the LM386 with a passive 8 ohm speaker, which is electrically wrong here: the NE555 output is a static DC level while the timer is running, so a coupling cap into the LM386 would only produce a click at the start and a click at the end - not a continuous tone. An active piezo buzzer solves this in one part because it has its own internal oscillator and only needs DC.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe NE555 is wired as a classic monostable (\"one-shot\"):\n\n- Pin 1 (GND) -> ground rail\n- Pin 8 (VCC) -> +5V rail\n- Pin 4 (RESET) -> +5V (tie HIGH so it is always armed)\n- Pin 2 (TRIG) -> pulled up to +5V through the 10K resistor, AND connected DIRECTLY to the SW-420 DO output (no series resistor in this line - a series resistor here would form a divider with the pull-up and TRIG would never reach the 1/3 Vcc threshold needed to fire)\n- Pin 6 (THRES) joined to pin 7 (DISCH), and both connected to the top of the 47uF timing capacitor. The bottom of the 47uF goes to GND.\n- Pin 7 (DISCH) also connects through the 220K resistor up to +5V. This is the R that, with the 47uF C, sets the pulse length: T = 1.1 * R * C = 1.1 * 220000 * 0.000047 = ~11.4 seconds in theory. In practice 47uF electrolytics carry +/-20% tolerance, so expect anything from ~9 to ~14 seconds. Treat the headline \"10 seconds\" as a ballpark.\n- Pin 3 (OUT) drives two things in parallel:\n  1. The red LED through the 330R resistor to GND (visual indicator)\n  2. The active piezo buzzer (+ pin to NE555 pin 3, - pin to GND)\n- A 100uF bulk decoupling cap sits between +5V and GND right next to pin 8. The NE555 takes a brief gulp of current at the switching edges; the cap smooths that out so the rail does not dip and accidentally re-trigger the timer.\n\nThe SW-420 module:\n\n- VCC -> +5V, GND -> GND, DO -> NE555 pin 2 (TRIG) directly\n- AO (analog out) left unconnected - we only use the digital comparator output, which is open-collector and pulls cleanly to ground when triggered\n- The little blue pot on the SW-420 sets the comparator threshold. Spin it slowly with a screwdriver until tapping the desk fires it but typing nearby does not.\n\nPower-in: plug the Arduino Uno into a laptop USB port. The Uno's 5V pin and GND pin are jumpered to the breadboard rails. The Uno's microcontroller is irrelevant - it is a $5 USB-to-5V converter for this build.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- A monostable is a circuit that, when poked, produces ONE output pulse of a fixed length, then waits. That is the whole job. Poke = trigger LOW for an instant; output = HIGH for ~10 seconds; then back to sleep.\n- Why this matters: real shakes (and real alarm conditions in general) are spiky. A door slam is 50 ms. A footstep on a creaky floor is 200 ms. You do not want a buzzer that beeps for 50 ms - you want it to scream until someone walks over and notices. The 555 turns a blip into a sustained event.\n- The formula T = 1.1 * R * C is one of the most useful in hobby electronics. 220K * 47uF gives ~11 seconds nominal, but with +/-20% electrolytic tolerance and resistor tolerance stacked, expect 9 to 14 seconds in pra","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1,"notes":"Used ONLY as a regulated 5V power source from its USB - no code runs on it for the alarm itself"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":4,"notes":"From SW-420 module 4-pin header to breadboard rails"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":10,"notes":"Power rails, signal hops, NE555 pin connections"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1,"notes":"Bulk decoupling cap across +5V and GND rails next to the NE555"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 47uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1,"notes":"NE555 timing capacitor C from pins 6/7 to GND"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"Visual ALARM indicator, lit while the 555 output is HIGH"},{"item_name":"NE555 Timer IC","qty":1,"notes":"Monostable one-shot timer that stretches the SW-420 blip into a ~10s alarm"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"All wiring is solderless on the breadboard"},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Pull-up on NE555 TRIG (pin 2) to Vcc - holds TRIG HIGH until SW-420 yanks it LOW"},{"item_name":"Resistor 220K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"NE555 timing resistor R between Vcc and pin 7 (with 47uF gives ~11s pulse)"},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current-limit resistor for the red LED on NE555 pin 3"},{"item_name":"SW-420 Vibration Switch Module","qty":1,"notes":"Vibration trigger; onboard LM393 DO output pulls cleanly to GND when shaken"}]},{"id":63,"name":"LM358 Photodiode Preamp Lux Meter","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"USB cable from laptop into the Arduino Uno; the Uno's 5V rail powers the LM358, the KY-018 module, and the OLED.","description":"**What it is**: Point the little light sensor at a lamp, a window, or the inside of a closet. The tiny screen shows a number that goes UP when there's more light and DOWN when it gets dark.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Walk around the house hunting for the brightest and darkest spots like a light detective - under the couch, behind the curtains, inside the fridge when the door pops open. Cup your hands over the sensor and watch the number crash to almost zero, like you just turned off the sun.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL) - reads the amplifier output and drives the OLED\n- 1x LM358 Op-Amp (dual) - one half is the transimpedance amplifier, the other half generates a virtual ground\n- 1x KY-018 Photoresistor Module - the LDR is repurposed as a light-driven current source\n- 1x 0.96\" OLED Display (I2C) - shows the millivolts, microamps and estimated lux\n- 1x Resistor 47K (1/8W 1%) - feedback resistor Rf, sized so the LDR's mA-scale current does not saturate the op-amp (gain ~47 mV per uA)\n- 2x Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%) - equal pair forming the 2.5V virtual-ground divider\n- 1x Capacitor 1uF 50V (electrolytic) - feedback cap across Rf, rolls off noise and stops the stage oscillating (a 100nF ceramic film cap is a cleaner choice for this spot - the polar electrolytic will work but adds a little slow drift from leakage; swap if you have one in the spares drawer)\n- 1x Capacitor 10uF - decouples the virtual-ground node so it stays stiff\n- 1x Capacitor 100uF - bulk decoupling on the LM358 V+ pin\n- 1x Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points) - solderless\n- Jumper wires (M-M and F-M assortments)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n**Power.** Arduino Uno provides 5V and GND from the USB rail. The LM358 runs single-supply from those rails.\n\n**LM358 power pins (do not skip this).** The LM358 only has 8 pins and two of them are easy for a beginner to forget. Wire them first:\n- **Pin 4 -> GND rail**\n- **Pin 8 -> +5V rail**, with the 100uF cap from pin 8 straight to GND right at the chip body\n\nIf the chip has no power, nothing else in this build will do anything and you will chase a dead circuit for an hour. Confirm with a multimeter across pins 4 and 8 before going further.\n\n**Virtual ground (op-amp B = pins 5, 6, 7).** Two 10K resistors form a divider from 5V to GND, meeting at the non-inverting input (pin 5). Tie pin 6 to pin 7 to make a unity-gain buffer. Op-amp B now outputs a rock-solid 2.5V on pin 7, which we call VREF. Drop a 10uF cap from VREF to GND.\n\n**Transimpedance stage (op-amp A = pins 1, 2, 3).** Connect VREF to the non-inverting input (pin 3). The inverting input (pin 2) is the \"summing junction\". Put Rf = 47K from pin 2 to the output pin 1, with the 1uF cap (or a 100nF ceramic) in parallel with Rf.\n\n**Light input.** The KY-018 has VCC, GND, and AO (analog signal). We are NOT using its AO comparator path. Instead, power the module from 5V/GND as normal, and run a jumper from the module's AO pin (or directly off whichever LDR lead is easiest to reach on your particular module) over to the summing junction at op-amp A pin 2. On a KY-018 the LDR sits between VCC and the AO output pin (with a 10K pulldown to ground), so AO is electrically one end of the LDR and that is exactly the node we want to grab. Because pin 2 is held at VREF (2.5V) by op-amp feedback, the voltage across the LDR is roughly (5V - 2.5V) = 2.5V, and the resulting current flows into the summing junction. The op-amp swings its output below VREF by exactly (I_LDR x Rf) volts.\n\n**Why 47K (and not 1M like every photodiode datasheet shows).** A real photodiode passes nanoamps to a few microamps. An LDR is much chattier - in normal room light its resistance falls to a few kilohms, so with ~2.5V across it you get tens of microamps, sometimes hundreds. With a 1M feedback resistor you would need (say) 100 uA x 1M = 100V at the op-amp output, which is obviously impossible, so the LM358 just slams into the bottom rail and you read a pinned, useless value. 47K keeps the stage living happily inside the chip's swing range. The price is sensitivity: gain is only ~47 mV per uA instead of 1 V per uA. That is fine for a learning meter.\n\n**Reading it.** Arduino A0 reads op-amp A's output (pin 1). Arduino A1 reads VREF (op-amp B output, pin 7) directly. The sketch subtracts the two so you measure dV around the virtual ground rather than absolute volts - this cancels supply drift (a trick called ratiometric measurement).\n\n**OLED.** SDA -> A4, SCL -> A5, VCC -> 5V, GND -> GND.\n\n## Test","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1,"notes":"Reads amplified voltage on A0, VREF on A1, drives OLED over I2C"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"Connects KY-018 module header and OLED to the breadboard"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Power, ground, and signal jumpers on the breadboard"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1,"notes":"Bulk decoupling on the LM358 V+ pin (pin 8) to GND"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1,"notes":"Decouples the virtual-ground node so it stays stiff under transients"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 1uF 50V (electrolytic)","qty":1,"notes":"Feedback cap Cf across Rf - rolls off noise and stops the TIA stage oscillating (a 100nF ceramic from the spares drawer is a cleaner swap if you have one)"},{"item_name":"LM358 Op-Amp (dual)","qty":1,"notes":"Op-amp A is the transimpedance stage; op-amp B is the virtual-ground buffer"},{"item_name":"0.96\" OLED Display (I2C)","qty":1,"notes":"Shows VREF, dV in mV, photocurrent in uA, and estimated lux"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"All wiring done on breadboard, no soldering"},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":2,"notes":"Equal pair forming the 2.5V virtual-ground divider"},{"item_name":"Resistor 47K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Feedback resistor Rf - sized for the LDR's mA-scale current so the LM358 does NOT saturate in normal room light (~47 mV per uA)"},{"item_name":"KY-018 Photoresistor Module","qty":1,"notes":"LDR cell used as a light-driven current source into the summing junction (we tap the LDR via the module's VCC and signal pins)"}]},{"id":64,"name":"LM324 Quad Window Comparator 9V Battery Gauge","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"9V battery itself - the same battery being measured powers the gauge through a momentary TEST button and a L7805 regulator that creates a stable 5V reference rail.","description":"**What it is**: Hold the button and a row of four LEDs lights up - blue, green, yellow, red - to show how much juice your 9V battery has left. As the battery gets weaker, the lights drop off one by one like a fuel gauge in a car.\n\n**Why it's fun**: No code, no screen, no computer brain - just a chip and some parts that magically \"decide\" things on their own. Test every old battery in the junk drawer and find out which ones still have life left and which ones are dead.\n\nA pure-analog, zero-firmware twin of the Pocket Battery Health Monitor - same job (tell me if this 9V battery is still good), no microcontroller, no OLED, no code. Just one quad op-amp doing four comparisons at once and four LEDs that drop off as the battery drains.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x LM324 quad op-amp (the brain - four comparators in one package)\n- 1x L7805 +5V linear regulator (creates a stable reference rail that does NOT sag as the battery drains)\n- 4x 5mm LEDs: blue, green, yellow, red (one per channel)\n- 6x 10K 1% resistors (4 for the reference ladder, 2 for the battery divider)\n- 4x 1K resistors (LED current limiters - 5V/1K = ~3 mA, plenty bright on modern LEDs)\n- 1x 100uF cap (input bulk smoothing for the 7805)\n- 1x 10uF cap (output bulk on the 7805 - borderline-OK on its own at this tiny load; in a \"real\" design you'd add a 0.1uF ceramic alongside)\n- 1x 1uF cap (sits right by the LM324's V+ pin - keeps it stable)\n- 1x small tactile push-button (TEST button - holding it powers the circuit so the gauge does not silently drain the very battery it's measuring)\n- 1x breadboard, jumper wires\n- 1x 9V battery (the thing being measured AND the power source - that's the fun part)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe 9V battery is BOTH the load-under-test and the power supply. That sounds like cheating but it's exactly how a fuel gauge in a car works - same circuit, same source.\n\n**Power path:** 9V battery (+) -> TEST button -> raw 9V rail (filtered by 100uF) -> L7805 IN. L7805 OUT = stable +5V rail (filtered by 10uF). The LM324's V+ pin (pin 4) is tied to the +5V rail, NOT the raw 9V. V- (pin 11) goes to battery GND. The LEDs are also anchored to the +5V rail through their 1K resistors, so the op-amp output only ever has to swing between 0 V and ~5 V - no awkward mixed-rail story.\n\n**Reference ladder (the four thresholds):** four 10K resistors stacked between +5V and GND make a uniform 4-step divider with three taps at 4.00 V, 3.00 V, and 2.00 V, plus the bottom-of-the-stack at 1.00 V. We use those four reference voltages (4.00 / 3.00 / 2.00 / 1.00) directly - no further \"tuning,\" because uniform 10K resistors only give uniform 1.00 V steps from a 5.00 V rail. Live with that, it works.\n\n**Battery sense:** a 10K/10K divider from the raw battery rail to GND gives V_batt/2 at its midpoint. So a fresh 9 V battery presents 4.5 V there, a tired 6 V battery presents 3.0 V. That single midpoint goes to ALL FOUR negative inputs of the LM324, tied together.\n\n**The trip points** (sense input = V_batt/2):\n\n| LED    | Reference tap | Sense threshold | V_batt threshold |\n| ------ | ------------- | --------------- | ---------------- |\n| Blue   | 4.00 V        | 4.00 V          | 8.0 V            |\n| Green  | 3.00 V        | 3.00 V          | 6.0 V            |\n| Yellow | 2.00 V        | 2.00 V          | 4.0 V            |\n| Red    | 1.00 V        | 1.00 V          | 2.0 V            |\n\nIn practice on a 9V alkaline: fresh battery (9.5 V) = all four LEDs lit. As it drains: Blue drops off at 8 V, Green at 6 V (battery is now firmly \"tired\"), Yellow at 4 V (basically dead - and the L7805 also drops out of regulation around here, so the readings get unreliable). Red is effectively always lit while the circuit has power - reframe it as \"circuit power present / sanity LED\" rather than a real low-battery alarm.\n\n**The comparator trick:** with V_batt/2 on the - input and a fixed reference on the + input, the op-amp output goes HIGH (close to V+) when reference > sense, and LOW (close to GND) when sense > reference. We want LEDs to be ON when the battery is HIGH, so each LED has its CATHODE on the op-amp output and its ANODE through a 1K resistor to the +5V rail. The LED lights when the output goes LOW (the op-amp SINKS current to ground), which happens when V_batt/2 is greater than that channel's threshold. As the battery drains, V_batt/2 falls below each threshold one by one and the LEDs turn off blue -> green -> yellow.\n\n**Why a stable reference matters:** if both the reference AND the","allocations":[{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Hookup wire for the breadboard"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF 16V (electrolytic)","qty":1,"notes":"Bulk smoothing on raw 9V input to L7805"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1,"notes":"Output bulk on the L7805 +5V rail"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 1uF 50V (electrolytic)","qty":1,"notes":"LM324 V+ supply bypass next to the chip"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Blue","qty":1,"notes":"FULL indicator - first to drop off as battery drains (trips at V_batt = 8.0 V)"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1,"notes":"GOOD indicator (trips at V_batt = 6.0 V)"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"Sanity / power-present LED (trips at V_batt = 2.0 V - effectively always lit while gauge is powered)"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Yellow","qty":1,"notes":"LOW indicator (trips at V_batt = 4.0 V)"},{"item_name":"LM324 Op-Amp (quad)","qty":1,"notes":"Four comparators in one 14-pin DIP - the star of the show"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"All wiring lives here, no soldering"},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":6,"notes":"4x reference ladder between +5V and GND (uniform 1V steps) + 2x battery divider (V_batt to V_batt/2)"},{"item_name":"Resistor 1K (1/8W 1%)","qty":4,"notes":"LED current-limiting (one per LED - 5V/1K = ~3 mA, plenty visible)"},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x4.3mm (white pin)","qty":1,"notes":"Momentary TEST button - hold to power the gauge so it does not drain the battery it is measuring"},{"item_name":"L7805 Linear Regulator (+5V)","qty":1,"notes":"Stable 5V reference rail (battery would otherwise drift the threshold)"}]},{"id":65,"name":"No-MCU Dark Detector (LM358 Schmitt Trigger)","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"9 V wall supply into an L7805 linear regulator that makes the +5 V rail. 100 uF input cap (35 V), 100 uF output cap (25 V) - polarity matters, striped lead to GND on both. No microcontroller is involved.","description":"**What it is**: Cover the little sensor with your hand. A red LED snaps on and you hear the relay click. Uncover it and a moment later it clicks off. No code, no Arduino - the board just knows it got dark.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Wire the relay to a desk lamp and you've built a thing that turns the light on by itself when the sun goes down. Wave your hand over the sensor at twilight and listen - a cheap circuit would chatter click-click-click, but yours clicks once and stays put, because you taught it to make up its mind.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x LM358 Op-Amp (dual) - channel A is the Schmitt comparator, channel B is a unity-gain buffer that drives the indicator LED\n- 1x KY-018 Photoresistor Module - the eye\n- 1x 1-channel Relay Module - the muscle (low-voltage DC only) - driven directly from the LM358 output through a 1K series resistor\n- 1x L7805 Linear Regulator (+5V) - TO-220, produces the +5 V rail from a 9 V wall supply\n- 1x Resistor 100K (1/8W 1%) - top of the threshold divider\n- 1x Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%) - bottom of the threshold divider\n- 1x Resistor 220K (1/8W 1%) - positive-feedback resistor (the project rests on this one - it sets the dead-band)\n- 1x Resistor 1K (1/8W 1%) - series resistor on the relay-module IN line\n- 1x Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%) - LED current limit\n- 1x LED 5mm Red - visible DARK indicator\n- 1x Capacitor 100uF 35V (electrolytic) - L7805 INPUT cap (9 V side)\n- 1x Capacitor 100uF 25V (electrolytic) - +5 V rail decoupling, absorbs the inrush surge when the relay coil energises\n- 1x Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm\n- Jumper wires (M-M and F-M, one pack each)\n\nThere is NO Arduino, no ESP32 and no microcontroller anywhere on the board. That's the whole pedagogical point - we earn the +5 V rail honestly with an L7805 and a 9 V supply.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nPower comes in from a 9 V wall supply into the L7805 (pin 1 = IN, pin 2 = GND, pin 3 = OUT, tab is GND). A 100 uF 35V electrolytic sits across the input pins and a 100 uF 25V electrolytic sits across the output pins. Polarity matters - the striped / short-lead side of each cap goes to GND. Reversed electrolytics will vent. Check before power-on.\n\nLM358 pinout (top view, dot top-left): 1=OUT_A, 2=IN-_A, 3=IN+_A, 4=GND, 5=IN+_B, 6=IN-_B, 7=OUT_B, 8=Vcc.\n\n- **Sense path:** KY-018 'AO' -> LM358 pin 2 (inverting input). Bright -> high voltage; dark -> low voltage.\n- **Threshold divider:** +5 V -> 100K -> node X -> 10K -> GND. Node X sits at 5 * 10/(100+10) = 0.4545 V nominal.\n- **The hysteresis:** node X also feeds LM358 pin 3. From pin 1 (channel-A output) a 220K resistor runs back to pin 3. THIS is the Schmitt trick - the output tugs the threshold up or down depending on which state it is in.\n- **Relay drive:** LM358 pin 1 -> 1K series -> relay module IN pin. The 1-ch relay module's input is a high-impedance optocoupler-style stage with its own onboard transistor and its IN-pin threshold sits around 2 V. The LM358 output swings ~0.6 V low to ~3.5 V high, which is comfortably outside that threshold in BOTH directions, so it drives the module directly with no BJT buffer. The module is active-LOW: when the LM358 is LOW (room bright) the module energises the relay; when the LM358 is HIGH (room dark) the module releases. Wire the load across COM and NC so that 'dark = load on' matches the indicator LED.\n- **Indicator LED:** the second LM358 channel is wired as a unity-gain buffer (pin 5 tied to pin 1, pin 6 tied to pin 7). When channel A goes HIGH (dark), channel B also goes HIGH and pushes current out pin 7 through 330R to the red LED to GND. This keeps the LED off the relay-module IN pin entirely, so the LED and the relay module never fight each other for current.\n\n## Talking points\n\nThis is the circuit you build to understand WHY twilight makes plain comparators misbehave. A bare LM393 or a plain LM358 with no feedback will sit right at the trip point as the sun sets and the output will chatter - flicker on, off, on, off - because the input is wobbling through the threshold a hundred times a second. A Schmitt trigger fixes that by having two thresholds: one to turn on, a different one to turn off. The gap between them is called the hysteresis window.\n\nHow does it create two thresholds out of one circuit? Positive feedback. The 220K resistor takes a fraction of the output and adds it back to the threshold on pin 3. When the output is LOW (room is lit, relay off), pin 3 sits just a hair above the nominal 0.455 V. When the output is HIGH (room is dark, relay on), pin 3 gets t","allocations":[{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"Connects the KY-018 and 1-channel relay modules to the breadboard"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Breadboard hookup wires between the L7805, LM358, resistors, and LED"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF 25V (electrolytic)","qty":1,"notes":"Bulk decoupling across the +5 V rail - smooths the inrush surge when the relay coil energises. NOTE polarity: long lead / unmarked side = +5 V, short lead / striped side = GND. Reversed it will pop."},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF 35V (electrolytic)","qty":1,"notes":"Input bulk cap for the L7805 (between 9 V input and GND). NOTE polarity: striped side = GND."},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"Visible 'DARK' indicator driven by the SECOND LM358 channel (pin 7) so it never fights the relay-module input stage"},{"item_name":"LM358 Op-Amp (dual)","qty":1,"notes":"Half A wired as a Schmitt comparator with positive feedback; half B drives the indicator LED"},{"item_name":"1-channel Relay Module","qty":1,"notes":"Driven DIRECTLY from LM358 pin 1 through a 1K series resistor. Low-voltage DC loads only. Module is active-LOW but its onboard transistor sinks the coil current - so when our op-amp output goes HIGH (dark) the module's IN is HIGH and relay is OFF; we wire the relay's COM/NC pair to the load so 'dark = load on'."},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the L7805, LM358, resistors, LED and the wired-up modules"},{"item_name":"Resistor 100K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Top of the reference divider; sets nominal threshold ~0.45 V"},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Bottom of the reference divider on the non-inverting input"},{"item_name":"Resistor 1K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Series resistor between LM358 pin 1 and the relay module IN pin (current limit, also handy probe point)"},{"item_name":"Resistor 220K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Positive-feedback resistor from op-amp output back to pin 3 - SETS the hysteresis (~115 mV dead-band with this divider)"},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current-limiting resistor for the red 'DARK' indicator LED"},{"item_name":"KY-018 Photoresistor Module","qty":1,"notes":"Light sensor - analog output (AO) feeds the op-amp's inverting input (pin 2)"},{"item_name":"L7805 Linear Regulator (+5V)","qty":1,"notes":"TO-220 regulator producing the +5 V rail from a 9 V wall supply - this is the 'no microcontroller in sight' clean-source for the analog circuit"}]},{"id":66,"name":"LM386 Pocket Speaker Amp (matchbox half-watt)","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"5V from a USB-C phone charger or power bank via the USB-C pigtail. LM386 happily runs 4-12V so a 9V battery works too if you want it portable - just keep the pin 7 BYPASS cap in place at the higher voltage.","description":"**What it is**: Plug your phone into the breadboard, hit play, and a tiny speaker the size of a coin blasts your music loud enough to fill the room. The whole amplifier fits in a matchbox.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You built a real boombox out of one black chip and a handful of parts, no batteries or apps required. Crank it up at the dinner table and watch everyone wonder where the song is coming from.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x LM386 Audio Power Amp (8-pin DIP)\n- 1x 1uF electrolytic - input DC blocker\n- 2x 10uF electrolytic - one is the gain-set cap between pins 1 and 8, one is the pin 7 BYPASS cap to GND\n- 1x 47uF electrolytic - supply bypass\n- 1x 100uF electrolytic - bulk reservoir on the 5V rail\n- 1x 220uF electrolytic - output DC blocker to speaker\n- 1x 10K resistor - series input attenuator (acts as a fixed volume control)\n- 1x breadboard\n- 1x USB-C female pigtail for 5V power in (any phone charger / power bank)\n- Hookup wires\n- *You supply:* a small 8-ohm speaker (anything from a dead toy or old set of earbuds will do), a 3.5mm headphone jack or a chopped aux cable, and the phone to play music from\n\n> The textbook LM386 circuit adds a 10R + 47nF ceramic snubber (a \"Zobel network\") from pin 5 to GND to damp ultrasonic ringing. We skip it because we don't stock 47nF ceramics, and on a short breadboard the LM386 is usually stable without it. If you hear a faint high-pitched whine after everything else works, that's why - the proper fix is to add the Zobel, not to substitute a 47uF electrolytic (a factor-of-1000 different value, which would short the output through the 10R resistor across the entire audio band and cook the chip).\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nLM386 pinout (notch at top):\n\n```\n    +-----+\n1 --|     |-- 8     1 = GAIN\n2 --|     |-- 7     2 = -IN (to GND)\n3 --|     |-- 6     3 = +IN  (audio from phone)\n4 --|     |-- 5     4 = GND\n    +-----+         5 = OUT\n                    6 = Vcc\n                    7 = BYPASS (10uF to GND - required at high gain)\n                    8 = GAIN\n```\n\nOn the breadboard:\n\n1. Drop the LM386 across the centre groove.\n2. **Power.** USB-C pigtail: red wire to the breadboard's `+` rail, black to the `-` rail. Wire LM386 pin 6 to `+`, pin 4 to `-`. Drop the 100uF (long leg to `+`) and the 47uF (long leg to pin 6) across the rails right next to the chip.\n3. **Gain = 200x.** This is the famous trick: a 10uF cap from pin 1 to pin 8 bypasses an internal 1.35K resistor and pushes the gain from the default 20x all the way up to 200x. Long leg of the 10uF goes to pin 1.\n4. **BYPASS cap (non-optional at high gain).** Second 10uF: long leg to pin 7, short leg to the `-` rail. Pin 7 is the LM386's internal reference-voltage pin. Without this cap to ground the chip hums audibly and is far more likely to break into oscillation. Every datasheet \"typical application\" circuit at gain=200 shows this cap - omitting it is the most common reason a beginner LM386 build sounds noisy.\n5. **Input.** Phone jack tip -> 1uF cap (orient the `+` leg toward the phone to match convention - the phone output sits near 0V DC, but `+` toward the higher DC potential is the standard rule) -> 10K resistor -> LM386 pin 3. Phone jack sleeve -> `-` rail. Pin 2 -> `-` rail.\n6. **Output.** LM386 pin 5 -> 220uF (long leg to pin 5) -> one speaker terminal. Other speaker terminal -> `-` rail.\n\nPlug the USB-C, plug the phone, hit play, and turn the phone's volume to about half.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The LM386 has been in production since 1983. It is the audio version of the NE555 - cheap, indestructible, taught in every electronics class, and still in current designs.\n- The chip has a built-in feedback network that fixes its gain at 20 by default. Pins 1 and 8 are the two ends of that internal resistor divider. Drop a capacitor across them and at audio frequencies the cap looks like a short, removing the resistor from the loop and shoving gain to 200.\n- Pin 7 (BYPASS) connects to an internal reference voltage that biases the input stage. Without a cap from pin 7 to ground, any noise on the supply rail rides straight onto that reference and gets amplified 200x. That is why this cap is the difference between \"music\" and \"music with a loud 60 Hz hum behind it.\"\n- Everything else on the chip is \"auxiliary\" - input bias, output coupling, supply bypass. The amplification itself is one transistor stage with feedback. That is the entire story of an op-amp / power-amp.\n- The 220uF output cap exists because the LM386's output sits at roughly half the s","allocations":[{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Hookup wires between phone jack, breadboard rails, and speaker"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1,"notes":"Bulk supply reservoir near LM386 power pins"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":2,"notes":"One between pins 1 and 8 to set gain to 200x; one from pin 7 (BYPASS) to GND to stop hum at high gain"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 1uF 50V (electrolytic)","qty":1,"notes":"Input DC-blocking cap from phone audio to pin 3"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 220uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1,"notes":"Output DC-blocking cap to speaker (pin 5)"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 47uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1,"notes":"Supply bypass between Vcc (pin 6) and GND"},{"item_name":"USB-C Female Chassis Connector (2P pigtail)","qty":1,"notes":"5V supply input from any USB-C phone charger or power bank"},{"item_name":"LM386 Audio Power Amp","qty":1,"notes":"8-pin DIP audio amp - the whole project in one chip"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"All wiring on a single solderless breadboard"},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Series input attenuator from phone to pin 3 (acts as fixed volume)"}]},{"id":67,"name":"LM386 Telegraph-Style Code Practice Oscillator","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"Powered from an Arduino Uno used purely as a regulated 5V brick over USB (USB jack -> Uno's 5V/GND pins -> breadboard rails). No code is uploaded to the Uno and no GPIO is used.","description":"**What it is**: Press and hold the button and a red LED starts blinking, about once a second, like a tiny lighthouse on your breadboard. Let go and it goes dark. Tap out short and long presses and you are flashing real Morse code.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Sailors used to flash messages between ships at night with big lamps just like this, and now you have a pocket-sized version on your desk. Learn the dots and dashes for your name, then flash it across the room to your dad and see if he can read it back.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x LM386 audio power amp (the star - rewired as a slow relaxation oscillator that flashes an LED in Morse-code rhythm, NOT as an audio amp this time)\n- 1x Tactile push-button 3x6x5mm (the \"telegraph key\")\n- 1x 10uF electrolytic cap (the timing cap - this, together with the 100K feedback resistor, sets the flash rate)\n- 1x 100uF electrolytic cap (Vcc bypass - keeps the supply quiet)\n- 1x 100K resistor (the feedback resistor - the cap charges through this; with the 10uF it gives a roughly 1-second flash period)\n- 2x 10K resistors (a half-supply reference divider on pin 2)\n- 1x 470R resistor (current limit for the LED on the output)\n- 1x 5mm red LED (the visible \"telegraph lamp\")\n- 1x Breadboard\n- M-M jumper wires\n- 1x Arduino Uno (used ONLY as a regulated 5V/GND brick through its USB jack - no code uploaded, no GPIO touched)\n\nNote on the \"telegraph\" angle: the inventory has no speaker or buzzer, so this build is a *visible* code-practice oscillator - a blinking lamp instead of a beep. That's historically real too: in the 1800s the Royal Navy and the army practised Morse on Aldis lamps and heliographs (mirror-reflected sunlight). Same code, same rhythm, light instead of sound.\n\n## Safety first - read this before plugging in\n\nElectrolytic caps have a polarity stripe marked on the side. **Backwards = they can heat up, vent, and occasionally pop.** Both caps in this build are electrolytic. Before you connect 5V:\n\n- 10uF timing cap: **plus (long leg) toward LM386 pin 3, stripe (short leg) toward GND.**\n- 100uF supply cap: **plus (long leg) toward LM386 pin 6 (Vcc), stripe (short leg) toward GND.**\n\nEyeball both stripes BEFORE you plug the USB cable in. If you smell anything warm once power is on, unplug immediately.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nLM386 pinout (notch up, looking down at the chip):\n\n```\n        +---v---+\n   1 GAIN | 1   8 | GAIN  8\n   2 -IN  | 2   7 | BYPASS 7\n   3 +IN  | 3   6 | VCC    6\n   4 GND  | 4   5 | OUT    5\n        +-------+\n```\n\n1. Plant the LM386 across the breadboard's centre channel, notch to the LEFT. Pin 1 is top-left, pin 4 is bottom-left, pin 5 is bottom-right, pin 8 is top-right.\n2. **Power rails:** Uno's 5V pin to the breadboard's red rail, Uno's GND to the blue rail. The Uno is just a tidy regulated 5V source - we never upload anything.\n3. **The \"telegraph key\":** the tactile push-button breaks the 5V line BEFORE it reaches LM386 pin 6. One leg of the button to the +5V rail, the other leg to pin 6. Press = chip powered = LED starts blinking. Release = silent and dark.\n4. **Vcc bypass:** 100uF cap from LM386 pin 6 (+ long leg) to GND rail (stripe / short leg). Plant it close to the chip.\n5. **GND:** LM386 pin 4 to the GND rail.\n6. **Half-supply reference on pin 2 (-IN):** two 10K resistors in series from +5V to GND. The middle of that pair sits at +2.5V. Wire that midpoint to pin 2. This gives the chip's inverting input a fixed \"decision line\" at half-supply.\n7. **The timing cap:** 10uF from pin 3 (+ long leg) to GND (stripe). This is the cap whose voltage will slowly swing up and down - the swinging voltage is what eventually drives the LED.\n8. **The feedback resistor (the slow part):** 100K from pin 5 (output) back to pin 3 (+IN). This is the wire that charges the 10uF cap from the output. When the output is high, the cap charges up. When the output flips low, the cap discharges back through the same 100K. The roughly 1-second cap voltage charge/discharge against the 2.5V threshold on pin 2 is what makes the output flip flip flip at about 1 Hz - a visible flash rate.\n9. **LED output:** pin 5 -> 470R resistor -> LED anode (long leg). LED cathode (short leg) to GND. Pin 5 will swing roughly between 0V and ~4V, so the LED visibly turns on and off.\n\nWhy this topology actually oscillates (and the old draft didn't): the LM386 here is acting like a comparator with hysteresis. Pin 2 is held at a fixed 2.5V reference. Pin 3 watches the cap voltage. When the cap charges above 2.5V the output slams ","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1,"notes":"Used ONLY as a tidy regulated 5V/GND source via its USB jack. No code uploaded, no GPIO touched."},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Hookup wire between rows on the breadboard."},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1,"notes":"Vcc bypass. Plus leg to pin 6, stripe to GND. Keeps the supply quiet during output transitions."},{"item_name":"Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1,"notes":"Timing cap. Plus leg to LM386 pin 3, stripe to GND. With the 100K feedback resistor this sets the ~1 second flash period."},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"The visible telegraph lamp. Anode (long leg) to the 470R, cathode (short leg) to GND."},{"item_name":"LM386 Audio Power Amp","qty":1,"notes":"Used as a comparator-with-hysteresis, not as an audio amp. Positive feedback through the 100K plus the 10uF timing cap makes pin 5 flip at ~1 Hz."},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the LM386, button, caps, resistors, and LED."},{"item_name":"Resistor 100K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Feedback resistor from pin 5 (output) to pin 3 (+IN). Together with the 10uF timing cap this sets the oscillation rate."},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":2,"notes":"Two of them, in series from +5V to GND, form a half-supply divider. Their midpoint feeds pin 2 (-IN) with a fixed 2.5V reference."},{"item_name":"Resistor 470R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current-limit resistor in series with the LED on pin 5 output."},{"item_name":"Tactile Push-button Switch 3x6x5mm (white pin)","qty":1,"notes":"The telegraph key - breaks the +5V line between the rail and LM386 pin 6 (Vcc). Press = chip powered = LED blinks."}]},{"id":68,"name":"TDA2822D Stereo Earbud Amp","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"Single 9V PP3 battery via a snap clip feeds Vcc (pin 5) and GND (pin 4) of the TDA2822D, with a 100uF 16V bulk decoupling cap across the rails close to the chip.","description":"**What it is**: Plug your phone into one end and your earbuds into the other. Hit play and the music comes out loud and clear in both ears, left and right, powered by a tiny 9V battery.\n\n**Why it's fun**: This is the first thing you build that turns into a real piece of audio gear you can actually use. Pull out a battery-powered headphone amp you made yourself at the kitchen table, plug in, and listen to your own music through it.\n\n## Parts list\n\nAllocations below are the canonical parts list. A few items are NOT in the kit inventory and you'll need to bring your own:\n\n- **Bring-your-own (not in kit inventory):**\n  - 9V PP3 battery with snap clip (the power source).\n  - 3.5mm stereo input jack or a sacrificial 3.5mm cable (tip = L, ring = R, sleeve = GND).\n  - A pair of earbuds (32 ohm typical). Do NOT plug in big 4 ohm bookshelf speakers - the TDA2822D is a small-signal chip.\n\n- **From the kit (see allocations):**\n  - 1x TDA2822D dual audio power amp.\n  - 1x breadboard.\n  - 1x 100uF 16V cap (bulk supply decoupling).\n  - 2x 10uF caps (input DC blocking).\n  - 2x 100uF 16V caps (AC bypass at IN-).\n  - 2x 470uF 16V caps (output DC blocking).\n  - 2x 4.7R resistors (output series damping).\n  - 1x Arduino Uno + 2x 10K resistors - ONLY for the optional test-tone procedure. Skip them if you go straight to a phone as the audio source.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n### TDA2822D pinout (DIP-8, looking down at the chip with the notch at the top)\n\n```\n        +--U--+\nOUT1  1 |     | 8  OUT2\nIN1-  2 |     | 7  IN2-\nIN1+  3 |     | 6  IN2+\nGND   4 |     | 5  VCC (9V)\n        +-----+\n```\n\nPin reference for the build:\n- Pin 1 OUT1, Pin 8 OUT2 - speaker outputs (left and right).\n- Pin 3 IN1+, Pin 6 IN2+ - non-inverting (signal) inputs.\n- Pin 2 IN1-, Pin 7 IN2- - inverting inputs. We use these to set AC gain.\n- Pin 4 GND, Pin 5 VCC - power.\n\n**Important correction vs older \"bootstrap cap\" tutorials:** the TDA2822 family does NOT have bootstrap pins. Pins 2 and 7 are the inverting inputs. The 100uF caps you'll see in this design go from each IN- pin to GND - they're AC bypass caps that short the IN- pin to ground for audio frequencies, giving the chip its maximum closed-loop gain (~39 dB) while leaving the internal DC bias path intact. Different role, different physics from the bootstrap caps used on a TDA2003 or TDA2030.\n\n### Power\n\n- 9V battery (+) -> Vcc rail. Battery (-) -> GND rail.\n- 100uF 16V bulk cap across the rails, leads as short as possible, sitting between pin 5 and pin 4. Long leg (+) goes to the Vcc rail.\n\n### Per channel (mirror image for left and right)\n\nFor the LEFT channel (the RIGHT channel uses pin 6, pin 7, pin 8 in the same shape):\n\n1. Audio source -> 10uF input cap (+ side toward the chip) -> chip pin 3 (IN1+).\n2. Chip pin 2 (IN1-) -> 100uF 16V cap (+ side toward the chip pin) -> GND rail. This is the AC-bypass cap that sets gain.\n3. Chip pin 1 (OUT1) -> 4.7R resistor -> 470uF 16V output cap (+ side toward the chip) -> LEFT earbud (+).\n4. LEFT earbud (-) -> GND rail.\n\nFor the RIGHT channel: pin 6 IN2+, pin 7 IN2-, pin 8 OUT2. Otherwise identical.\n\n### Input wiring from a 3.5mm jack\n\n- TIP -> LEFT 10uF input cap -> pin 3.\n- RING -> RIGHT 10uF input cap -> pin 6.\n- SLEEVE -> GND rail.\n\n### Electrolytic polarity rules of thumb\n\n- Bulk cap (100uF on Vcc): + to Vcc, - to GND.\n- Input caps (10uF): + toward the chip pin (the chip side sits at a slight positive bias due to the internal DC path; the source side is near 0V).\n- IN- AC-bypass caps (100uF): + toward the chip pin (the chip side sits at the internal bias voltage, the GND side is 0V).\n- Output caps (470uF): + toward the chip (chip side at ~4.5V, earbud side at 0V).\n\nGet any of these backwards and the cap will leak, heat up, or eventually pop. Voltage rating: every electrolytic in this build is rated for 16V or higher, which is comfortable headroom on a 9V supply.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **This is your first stereo amp.** Up to now you've only built mono amps (LM386). Stereo is just \"do it twice in one package.\"\n- **What you actually save with a dual chip.** The TDA2822D is two complete amplifiers sharing one Vcc/GND pair. The savings aren't the transistors inside (those still doubled) - it's that the package, the supply pins, and the ground pin are shared. You go from two chips plus two sets of supply wiring to one chip plus one set.\n- **DC blocking caps everywhere.** Audio is AC riding on top of a DC level. The chip biases its outputs to mid-supply (~4.5V) so the signal has ","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1,"notes":"Optional - only used by the test-tone sketch to verify channel separation. Not part of the amp itself."},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF 16V (electrolytic)","qty":3,"notes":"AC-bypass caps from each inverting input (IN-) to GND. They set max AC gain by shorting IN- to ground for audio while leaving the DC bias path intact. One per channel: pin 2 -> GND, pin 7 -> GND.; Bulk supply decoupling across the 9V rail near pin 5 of the chip. Voltage-rated >= 16V for the 9V supply."},{"item_name":"Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":2,"notes":"Input DC-blocking caps, one per channel (audio source -> chip IN+ pin)."},{"item_name":"Capacitor 470uF 16V (electrolytic)","qty":2,"notes":"Output DC-blocking caps in series with each earbud so no DC bias reaches the speaker coil."},{"item_name":"TDA2822D Audio Power Amp","qty":1,"notes":"Dual audio power amp IC in DIP-8. One package = two channels sharing Vcc/GND."},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Hosts chip, caps, resistors, and signal routing."},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":2,"notes":"Series resistors between Uno D9/D10 and the amp's L/R inputs during the test-tone procedure."},{"item_name":"Resistor 4.7R (1/8W 1%)","qty":2,"notes":"Series damping resistor on each output (between OUT pin and the 470uF output cap). Defensive: limits the spike if an earbud lead shorts."}]},{"id":69,"name":"NE5532 Hi-Fi Headphone Preamp","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"9V battery (or 9V DC wall-wart, centre-positive) feeding the breadboard rails directly. The op-amp draws under 10 mA, so a single 9V will last many hours of listening. If the rail sags below ~6.5V the NE5532 starts to clip - swap the battery.","description":"**What it is**: Plug your phone or laptop into one end and your headphones into the other. Music goes in, music comes out, but cleaner and louder, with no static or hiss in the quiet parts.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You built the same kind of audio booster that lives inside real recording studio gear, from a handful of parts on a breadboard. Hand the headphones to your dad, play his favourite song, and watch his face when he realises YOU made the thing the sound is coming through.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x NE5532 Op-Amp (audio dual) - the \"U1\" in the schematic; both halves used (one per stereo channel)\n- 4x Capacitor 1uF 50V (electrolytic) - AC coupling caps (2 input, 2 output)\n- 4x Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%) - 2 input bias resistors to virtual ground, 2 feedback path resistors\n- 2x Resistor 100R (1/8W 1%) - output build-out resistors to protect against headphone-cable capacitance\n- 2x Resistor 22K (1/8W 1%) - virtual-ground bias divider\n- 1x Capacitor 100uF 25V (electrolytic) - power-rail bulk decoupling\n- 1x Capacitor 100uF 16V (electrolytic) - virtual-ground filter cap (kills supply hum on the mid-rail)\n- 1x LED 5mm Red + 1x Resistor 470R (1/8W 1%) - power-on indicator (470R picks the dim end of the inventory's 220R-470R LED range)\n- 1x Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm - build surface\n- Jumper wires M-M\n\nYou also supply: a 9V battery (or 9V wall-wart with the centre pin positive), a 3.5mm stereo audio input (e.g. a cut-up old aux cable), a 3.5mm stereo headphone jack for the output, and a pair of headphones (32-ohm or higher impedance is happiest - see talking points for why).\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThis is a pure-analog project - no microcontroller, no code on a chip. You will validate it with a multimeter (see the \"code\" field) before you plug in headphones.\n\nPower: connect the 9V battery + to the top rail and battery - to the bottom rail. The bottom rail IS ground for the whole circuit. Drop the 100uF 25V bulk cap straight across these rails as close to where the battery wires arrive as possible - it stops the rail sagging when the op-amp briefly draws current.\n\nVirtual ground: the NE5532 runs from a single supply, so we fake a second rail by splitting +9V in half with the two 22K resistors. The junction of those resistors is \"virtual ground\" - it sits at about 4.5V and behaves like a quiet 0V reference for the op-amp. The 100uF 16V cap sits between that node and real ground - it shunts any leftover ripple to ground so the virtual rail is glass-smooth. Mark that breadboard row as **TP2** with a Sharpie or a flag - you'll come back to it during testing.\n\nNE5532 pinout (top view, pin 1 dot at top-left):\n- Pin 1 = Output A (Left channel out)\n- Pin 2 = Inverting input A\n- Pin 3 = Non-inverting input A (Left channel in)\n- Pin 4 = V- (ground)\n- Pin 5 = Non-inverting input B (Right channel in)\n- Pin 6 = Inverting input B\n- Pin 7 = Output B (Right channel out)\n- Pin 8 = V+ (+9V)\n\nEach channel is a unity-gain non-inverting buffer:\n1. Input audio -> 1uF coupling cap -> NE5532 pin 3 (or pin 5 for right) AND a 10K resistor to virtual ground (this gives the input something to bias against without loading the source).\n2. Output pin 1 (or pin 7) wired straight to inverting input pin 2 (or pin 6) - this is the unity-gain feedback path. The 10K from inverting input to virtual ground sets the DC reference.\n3. Output pin 1 (or pin 7) -> 1uF coupling cap -> 100R build-out resistor -> headphone jack tip (left) or ring (right).\n4. Headphone jack sleeve goes to real ground.\n\nThe two 1uF coupling caps are polarised: the + leg of the INPUT cap faces the op-amp input, the + leg of the OUTPUT cap faces the op-amp output. Both op-amp pins sit at ~4.5V, source and headphones sit at 0V DC, so + faces the higher-voltage side.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **Why an op-amp here?** A modern phone's headphone output can already drive headphones, so what is this thing for? It is a *buffer* - it gives the source a low-current, low-impedance load to look at, and presents the headphones with an even lower-impedance source. Long cables and weird passive volume pots can mess with sound; a buffer immunises against that.\n- **Why this specific chip?** The NE5532 has been in pro studio mixing desks since the 1970s. Two op-amps in one 8-pin package, very low noise (~5 nV/sqrt(Hz)), high output current (can drive 600-ohm loads cleanly), and it slews fast enough to handle audio without slewing-induced distortion. It is the \"default\" audio op-amp.\n- **Why single supply with a fake ground?** Op-amps ","allocations":[{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Breadboard hookup - rails, input/output runs, IC pinout"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF 16V (electrolytic)","qty":1,"notes":"Filter cap across the virtual-ground node - keeps the mid-rail quiet so hum doesn't leak into the audio"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF 25V (electrolytic)","qty":1,"notes":"Power-rail bulk decoupling - sits directly across +9V and GND right where the battery wires arrive, stops the rail sagging on transients"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 1uF 50V (electrolytic)","qty":4,"notes":"AC coupling caps - 2 at each input (block DC from source), 2 at each output (block any op-amp DC offset from headphones)"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"Power-on indicator (optional but useful to see when the 9V battery is connected)"},{"item_name":"NE5532 Op-Amp (audio dual)","qty":1,"notes":"The star. Both halves used: U1A buffers Left, U1B buffers Right"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Build surface - one breadboard fits both channels with the NE5532 straddling the centre gutter"},{"item_name":"Resistor 100R (1/8W 1%)","qty":2,"notes":"Output build-out resistors - protect the op-amp from capacitive headphone-cable loads and limit short-circuit current"},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":4,"notes":"Input bias resistors to virtual ground (2x) and feedback path to set unity gain (2x)"},{"item_name":"Resistor 22K (1/8W 1%)","qty":2,"notes":"Virtual-ground bias divider - splits the 9V single supply into a stable mid-rail reference for both channels"},{"item_name":"Resistor 470R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current-limiting resistor for the power LED - inside the 220R-470R inventory convention. Picks the dim end of the range (~17 mA at 9V) to save battery and avoid distracting brightness in a listening setup"}]},{"id":72,"name":"LM317 USB-C 3V3 Breadboard Brick","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"USB-C charger or laptop port (5V) feeds the 2-pin pigtail. The LM317T regulates it down to ~3.3V via a 220R/390R divider on the ADJ pin, with electrolytic bypass caps on Vin, Vout and ADJ.","description":"**What it is**: Plug in a USB-C cable from any phone charger. A green LED glows on the breadboard and the red (+) rail becomes a clean 3.3V power source you can run your ESP32 boards from.\n\n**Why it's fun**: This is your own pocket-sized power station. Once you build it, every future project on the breadboard just plugs in next to it and works, no batteries, no soldering, no buying a special supply.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x LM317T Adjustable Regulator - the workhorse, set to ~3.3V via two resistors on the ADJ pin\n- 1x USB-C Female Chassis Connector (2P pigtail) - 5V input from any USB-C source\n- 1x Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%) - R1 in the LM317 divider (Vout to ADJ)\n- 1x Resistor 390R (1/8W 1%) - R2 in the LM317 divider (ADJ to GND)\n- 1x Capacitor 22uF 50V (electrolytic) - input bulk cap, sits across Vin and GND right at the LM317 legs\n- 1x Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage) - output cap, sits across Vout and GND right at the LM317 legs\n- 1x Capacitor 4.7uF 50V (electrolytic) - ADJ-to-GND bypass cap, boosts ripple rejection\n- 1x LED 5mm Green - power-good indicator on the 3.3V rail\n- 1x Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%) - current-limit resistor for the green LED\n- 1x Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points) - everything lives here, no soldering\n- 1x Pin Header 1x40 Male Breakaway (2.54mm) - break off a 2-pin piece to anchor the USB-C pigtail\n- 1x Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc - short jumpers to route output to the breadboard's (+) rail\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe LM317 in TO-220 has three legs viewed from the front (text side facing you, legs pointing down): **pin 1 = ADJ (left), pin 2 = Vout (middle), pin 3 = Vin (right).** This is the OPPOSITE of the L7805 pinout - the regulator family is not pin-compatible. Get this wrong and the input voltage hits the ADJ pin directly and the part dies.\n\nLayout on the breadboard:\n\n1. Plant the LM317 across three adjacent rows in the middle of the board.\n2. **Input cap (22uF):** plug it across pin 3 (Vin) and a (-) rail row. **Stripe (the marked leg) is the negative leg - it goes to GND.** Reverse-polarity electrolytics heat up, sometimes loudly. The datasheet's minimum input cap is 0.1uF, but a bigger electro holds the rail up during ESP32 radio bursts.\n3. **Output cap (10uF):** across pin 2 (Vout) and a (-) rail row. Stripe to GND. The LM317 datasheet specifically calls for 1uF tantalum *or* 10uF electrolytic on the output for loop stability. The bigger value is the right pick when using electrolytics because their ESR makes the smaller cap less effective.\n4. **Resistor divider (the bit that sets Vout):** R1 (220R) goes from pin 2 (Vout) to pin 1 (ADJ). R2 (390R) goes from pin 1 (ADJ) to the (-) rail. The LM317 always works to keep 1.25V across R1, which forces a known current through R2, which sets Vout. Math: Vout = 1.25 * (1 + R2/R1) = 1.25 * (1 + 390/220) = 3.47V theoretical. Real-world resistor tolerance plus the LM317's own Vref tolerance puts the actual reading somewhere in the 3.30 to 3.45V band - all comfortably inside the ESP32-C3's 3.0 to 3.6V supply spec.\n5. **ADJ bypass cap (4.7uF):** across pin 1 (ADJ) and (-) rail, stripe to GND. This one isn't strictly required but it lifts the LM317's power-supply ripple rejection by about 15dB - the divider would otherwise pass input ripple straight into the ADJ pin and back out to Vout.\n6. **USB-C pigtail:** red goes straight to pin 3 (Vin). Black goes to the (-) rail. Anchor the flimsy pigtail wires by jamming the bare ends into a 2-pin male header pressed into the breadboard.\n7. **Power LED:** pin 2 (Vout) -> 220R -> LED anode (long leg) -> LED cathode (short leg, flat side of the dome) -> (-) rail. ((3.3V - 2.0V Vf for green) / 220R = ~5.9mA, comfortably visible.)\n8. **Rail jumpers:** short M-M jumper from pin 2 row to the (+) rail; another from the LM317 had-no-pin-2-no-this-is-Vout row... actually, just from pin 2 to (+) rail and from any GND row to the (-) rail. The (+) rail now sits at ~3.3V.\n\nThere is intentionally no series fuse-resistor on the input. A 10R in series with VBUS would drop 0.5V at typical ESP32 idle currents and a destructive 2V at 200mA bursts - that defeats the entire point of building a clean rail. Polyfuses are the right answer for inrush/short protection and they aren't in this kit, so this build trusts the USB-C source's own current-limiting (every modern charger has it).\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **Why dropout actually matters here:** Linear regulators need a minimum voltage between Vin and Vout to function (called the dropo","allocations":[{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Short jumpers to route Vout to the (+) rail and GND to the (-) rail"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1,"notes":"Output cap on the Vout pin. Datasheet calls for 1uF tantalum or 10uF electrolytic on the output for stability - the bigger value is the correct pick when using electros."},{"item_name":"Capacitor 22uF 50V (electrolytic)","qty":1,"notes":"Input bulk cap on the Vin pin. Datasheet wants 0.1uF minimum but a larger electro holds the rail up during ESP32 radio bursts."},{"item_name":"Capacitor 4.7uF 50V (electrolytic)","qty":1,"notes":"ADJ-to-GND bypass cap. Improves ripple rejection by ~15dB by stopping the divider from re-injecting input ripple into the ADJ pin."},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1,"notes":"Power-good indicator on the 3.3V output rail"},{"item_name":"Pin Header 1x40 Male Breakaway (2.54mm)","qty":1,"notes":"Break off a 2-pin strip to anchor the flimsy USB-C pigtail wires into the breadboard"},{"item_name":"USB-C Female Chassis Connector (2P pigtail)","qty":1,"notes":"5V input from any USB-C charger or laptop port (2-pin = VBUS + GND only, no CC negotiation needed for legacy 5V default mode)"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Everything lives on this breadboard; the (+) rail becomes the regulated 3.3V output"},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":2,"notes":"R1 in the LM317 divider, between Vout and ADJ. Sets the reference current (1.25V / 220R = 5.7mA). Substituted for the textbook 240R - same E12 family, output shifts to 3.30V instead of 3.32V.; Current-limit resistor for the green power LED ((3.3V - 2.0V Vf) / 220R = ~5.9mA, comfortably visible)"},{"item_name":"Resistor 390R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"R2 in the LM317 divider, between ADJ and GND. Vout = 1.25 * (1 + 390/220) = 3.47V theoretical; bench tolerance lands in the 3.30-3.45V band, comfortably inside ESP32-C3 spec (3.0-3.6V)."},{"item_name":"LM317T Adjustable Regulator","qty":1,"notes":"Adjustable 3-terminal regulator in TO-220. Set to 3.30V via R1/R2 divider on ADJ pin."}]},{"id":73,"name":"LM317T Constant-Current LED Driver","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"6.5 V to 12 V DC from a 9 V battery or bench supply, fed to the +V rail of the breadboard (a 9 V battery is the easy choice; the LM317 needs ~3 V of dropout above the LED + Rset stack, so anything under ~6.5 V will fall out of regulation).","description":"**What it is**: Hook up a 9V battery and the LED glows at the perfect brightness. Now swap in a fresh battery, or a weaker one, or wiggle the wires around - the LED stays the exact same brightness. It refuses to get any brighter or dimmer.\n\n**Why it's fun**: This is your first taste of a real engineering trick: a circuit that fights back to keep itself steady, no code required. Try to make the LED flicker by tapping the battery on and off - you can't, and that's the magic.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x LM317T Adjustable Regulator (TO-220, the star of the show)\n- 1x Resistor 56R (1/8W 1%) - this is \"Rset\", the only resistor that matters\n- 1x LED 5mm Red - the load we are driving (at a safe ~22 mA)\n- 1x Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, rated >=16V) - input bulk decoupling\n- 1x Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm\n- A handful of M-M jumper wires\n- A 6.5 V to 12 V supply (a 9 V battery is perfect, or a bench supply)\n\nNo microcontroller. No code. Just one IC, one resistor, and a multimeter to prove it works.\n\n**LED derating note (important):** a generic 5mm through-hole LED is rated for ~20 to 30 mA continuous. We deliberately target ~22 mA with a 56R Rset to stay inside that envelope. If you ever see a tutorial that calls for 100 mA or more through a plain 5mm LED, ignore it - the LED will dim within hours and die within days. Higher currents belong on 1W \"power LEDs\" with a proper heatsink, not on indicator LEDs from a bag of 100.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe LM317T has three legs. Looking at the front (text side), they are, left to right: **ADJ (pin 1), Vout (pin 2), Vin (pin 3)**.\n\nThe trick is that the LM317 always tries to hold exactly **1.25 V between its Vout pin and its ADJ pin**. In the normal \"voltage regulator\" hookup you use two resistors to bleed that reference into a divider. In this hookup you put a single resistor between Vout and ADJ, and the LED hangs off the ADJ pin.\n\nResult: the LM317 forces 1.25 V across that one resistor, which by Ohm's law forces a fixed current through it. And since the LED is in series with that resistor, the LED gets that same fixed current. The current is `I = 1.25 V / R`. With `R = 56R`, that is **~22 mA**, a safe, indicator-LED-friendly value.\n\nBuild order on the breadboard:\n\n1. Drop the LM317T into the board so its three legs land on three separate rows.\n2. Wire **Vin (pin 3)** to the +V rail.\n3. Wire the 10uF cap (rated **>=16V**, since we are running up to 12V on the rail) from +V rail to GND rail, observing polarity (long leg / no stripe = +). If you have a 0.1uF ceramic handy, dropping it in parallel with the electrolytic right at the Vin pin is conventional LM317 hygiene - not required, but it cleans up high-frequency noise the electrolytic is too slow to handle.\n4. Wire the **56R resistor between Vout (pin 2) and ADJ (pin 1)**.\n5. From **ADJ (pin 1)**, jumper to the **LED anode** (the long leg).\n6. **LED cathode** (short leg, flat side) goes to the GND rail.\n7. Connect your power: +V rail to +6.5V to +12V, GND rail to supply ground.\n\nThere is no separate current-limiting resistor for the LED in this build - the whole circuit is the current limiter. That is the point.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The LM317 has a tiny internal reference, about 1.25 V, that it works very hard to hold across two of its pins (Vout and ADJ). Everything else in the LM317 - the pass transistor, the error amp, the protection - is in service of that one number.\n- In the textbook hookup (two resistors making a divider on ADJ) you trick that 1.25 V into becoming a higher output voltage. Here we skip the divider entirely. With only a resistor across the reference, the LM317 has no choice but to push exactly `1.25 V / R` amps through it. That current then flows through whatever you stick in series with it - in our case, an LED.\n- Why is this better than a plain resistor in series with an LED? Because LED forward voltage changes. It changes with temperature (around -2 mV/degC), it changes between parts in the same bag, and it changes as the LED ages. With a plain resistor driver, those Vf shifts become current shifts, and current is what determines LED brightness and lifespan. With a current source, the LM317 absorbs the Vf shifts in its own dropout headroom and the LED keeps the same current. That is the whole game.\n- Headroom matters: the LM317 needs Vin to be at least about 3 V above its own Vout pin to stay in regulation. With a red LED (Vf ~2 V) and 1.25 V across Rset, the LM317's Vout pin sits at ~3.25 V above ground, so Vin needs to b","allocations":[{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Short hookup leads between LM317 legs, resistor, LED, and rails"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1,"notes":"Input bulk cap on Vin - pick a part rated >=16V (we are running 6.5-12V)"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"Load - driven at ~22 mA, well within the 20-30 mA continuous rating of a standard 5mm LED"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"All the wiring lives here - no soldering on this build"},{"item_name":"Resistor 56R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Rset - sets the current at I = 1.25V / 56R = ~22 mA, safe for a generic 5mm LED"},{"item_name":"LM317T Adjustable Regulator","qty":1,"notes":"Wired as a constant-current source - ADJ pin tied through Rset to LED return"}]},{"id":75,"name":"ULN2803 Knight Rider Light Bar","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"Arduino Uno powered from laptop USB (5V). The 5V rail also feeds the ULN2803 COM pin (10) and the LED anodes through 220R resistors; the chip sinks each LED cathode to GND when its input pin goes HIGH.","description":"**What it is**: Eight LEDs in a row light up one at a time, with a glowing dot that zooms left, then right, then left again, forever. It looks exactly like the front of KITT, the talking car from Knight Rider.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You built the same effect that 80s TV used on a real car prop, and once it works you can crank the speed dial in the code to make the dot bounce slow and lazy or so fast it almost blurs into a solid line. Show it to your friends with the lights off and watch them lean in.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Arduino Uno R3 (the brain - sends the \"which LED\" pattern)\n- 1x ULN2803APG Darlington Array - the star of the show: eight transistor pairs in one chip\n- 1x Breadboard (~400 tie-points) - everything lives here, no soldering\n- 8x 5mm LEDs - 5 Red + 3 Yellow (we only have 5 red in stock, the yellows finish the bar)\n- 8x 220R resistors - one per LED to keep them from burning out\n- Jumper wires (M-M for the breadboard, F-M from Uno to breadboard)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe ULN2803 is the trick that makes this clean. It has 18 pins:\n\n- Pins 1-8 on the left are INPUTS (IN1-IN8). Wire these to Arduino pins D2 through D9.\n- Pins 11-18 on the right are OUTPUTS (OUT1-OUT8). Each one connects to the CATHODE (short leg, flat side) of one LED.\n- Pin 9 is GND - connect to Arduino GND and the breadboard ground rail.\n- Pin 10 is COM - connect to the breadboard +5V rail (this is the \"load supply\").\n\nFor each LED:\n\n- Anode (long leg) -> one end of a 220R resistor\n- Other end of the 220R -> the breadboard +5V rail\n- Cathode (short leg, flat side) -> ULN2803 output pin (OUT1 for LED1, OUT2 for LED2, etc.)\n\nArrange the eight LEDs in a row on the breadboard so the chase is visible as a moving dot.\n\nPower rails: Arduino 5V -> breadboard red rail. Arduino GND -> breadboard blue rail. ULN2803 pin 9 to blue rail, pin 10 to red rail.\n\nWhen the Arduino sets D2 HIGH, the ULN2803 pulls OUT1 to ground - current flows from +5V through the resistor, through the LED, through the chip to ground. LED on. When D2 goes LOW, the chip opens the circuit. LED off. Same for the other seven.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- The whole point of the ULN2803 is replacement of eight separate transistor circuits. If you wanted to drive eight LEDs with discrete BC547s, you would need 8 transistors plus 8 base resistors - sixteen extra parts and a maze of wires. Here it is one chip.\n- \"Darlington\" means two transistors in a row inside the chip, which gives huge current gain - a tiny 5V signal from the Arduino can switch loads up to 500 mA per channel.\n- The chip is a SINK driver, not a source driver. It pulls outputs DOWN to ground, it does not push UP to +5V. That is why the LED anodes go to +5V and the cathodes go to the chip. This is \"current sinking\" or \"low-side switching\" and it is the most common way driver ICs work.\n- Pin 10 (COM) has internal flyback diodes built in. We do not need them for LEDs, but they exist so the same chip can drive relays and motors without exploding from coil kickback. Free feature.\n- The \"Knight Rider\" scan pattern - dot moves left, then right, then left - is one of the first 80s TV special effects done with simple electronics. Same trick was used for the actual car prop, just with more LEDs.\n\n## Things to change once it works\n\n- Change the scan speed. Lower `STEP_MS` from 80 to 30 - lightning fast. Raise to 200 - lazy bouncing ball.\n- Add a fade trail. Instead of just on/off, PWM the LEDs so the previously-lit ones glow dimly for a frame or two. (The Uno only has 6 PWM pins, so this needs software PWM - good intermediate challenge.)\n- Swap to a different pattern: all-on/all-off blink, alternating odd/even, random sparkle.\n- Build the same circuit with eight discrete BC547 transistors instead of the ULN2803 to feel why driver ICs were invented.\n- Drive a real load. The ULN2803 can handle ~500 mA per channel - swap LEDs for small 5V relays, small DC motors, or solenoids (one per channel) and you have an 8-output controller.\n\n## Why this is interesting\n\nThis is the \"one chip swallows eight transistors\" moment. You wire it once and suddenly your breadboard is uncluttered, your code is clean, and the same circuit pattern scales to relays, motors, stepper coils, and segment displays. The ULN2803 is one of those parts that has been in continuous production since 1980 because nothing replaced its sweet spot: lots of cheap medium-current low-side switches in one DIP package. Knowing how to use it unlocks a huge category of projects with almost no","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1,"notes":"Drives the eight ULN2803 inputs from D2..D9"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"Arduino D2..D9 and 5V/GND from Uno headers to the breadboard"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Power rails, chip-to-LED runs, and resistor jumpers"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":5,"notes":"Five of the eight scanning LEDs (uses entire stock of red 5mm)"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Yellow","qty":3,"notes":"Three substitutes to fill the bar to 8 LEDs (red stock is only 5)"},{"item_name":"ULN2803APG Darlington Array (8-ch)","qty":1,"notes":"Eight low-side sink drivers in one chip - the whole point of the project"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the chip, eight LEDs, and eight resistors"},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":8,"notes":"One per LED, between +5V rail and LED anode"}]},{"id":76,"name":"PC817 Optoisolated Relay Trigger Board","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S powered from USB; load-side 5V tapped from the ESP32 Vin rail for the first build (shared ground, no real isolation yet - cut later for a separate brick).","description":"**What it is**: Tell the ESP32 to flip a tiny switch, and CLICK - a real relay snaps shut and a green LED lights up. You hear the click, you see the light, and the chip in the middle controls it all without ever touching the LED's wires.\n\n**Why it's fun**: That click sound is the same one in dishwashers, garage doors, and old arcade machines - now you can make it happen on command. Even cooler: the two halves of the circuit never touch each other, they only talk through a flash of invisible light inside a little black chip.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- ESP32 NodeMCU-32S (the controller side - 3.3V logic)\n- PC817 optocoupler (the isolation barrier - an LED facing a phototransistor in a 4-pin DIP)\n- BC337 NPN BJT in TO-92 (the muscle that switches the relay-module input)\n- 1-channel Relay Module (active-LOW; we treat it as the \"load\" stage)\n- 1K resistor (limits current into the PC817 input LED)\n- 10K resistor (base pull-down so the BC337 stays off when the opto is dark)\n- 330R resistor + green 5mm LED (a visible \"load\" the relay switches so you can see the result)\n- Breadboard, M-M and F-M jumper wires\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe whole point of this board is that there are two electrical \"sides\" that should not share copper. **In the first build they DO share copper** - we cheat and power both sides off the ESP32's USB rail so you only need one cable. That means this initial build is a wiring exercise, not a real isolation demo. The PC817 is sitting in the signal path ready to do its job; \"Things to change once it works\" walks you through cutting the one jumper that makes the isolation real.\n\n- **Controller side (left):** ESP32 GPIO 23 -> 1K resistor -> PC817 pin 1 (LED anode). PC817 pin 2 (LED cathode) -> ESP32 GND. That is all the MCU sees.\n- **Load side (right):** PC817 pin 4 (collector) ties to the load-side +5V rail. PC817 pin 3 (emitter) drives the BC337 base **directly** (no series resistor), with a 10K from base to load-side GND as a pull-down. BC337 emitter sits on load-side GND. BC337 collector ties to the relay module's IN pin. The relay module's VCC and GND go to the load-side +5V and GND. The relay then switches COM/NO into a 330R + green LED loop.\n\n**Why no series base resistor?** The PC817's phototransistor is already current-limited - it can only deliver as much collector current as its CTR (current transfer ratio) lets through for the input LED current we're using. Stacking a 10K series resistor in front of the BC337 base would form a divider with the 10K pull-down and cut available base drive roughly in half. At 2.1 mA forward current through the PC817 LED (3.3V GPIO minus the LED's ~1.2V Vf, divided by 1K), the phototransistor's output is on the low end of its CTR curve, so we want every milliamp of base drive reaching the BC337. Direct emitter-to-base, with only the 10K pull-down for safety, is the cleaner topology.\n\nThe 10K base pull-down is the safety belt: if the phototransistor is dark, the BC337 base must be at a clean GND. Without it, the base floats and ambient light leaking into the PC817 (or even capacitive coupling) can twitch the relay.\n\nThe relay module is active-LOW. BC337 saturated pulls IN close to load-side GND, which the module reads as \"energize\". When the opto is dark, BC337 is off, IN floats high through the module's own onboard pull-up, and the relay sits open.\n\n**Note on the relay module:** most off-the-shelf 1-channel relay modules already contain their own onboard PC817 (or similar) on the IN pin. Adding our own discrete PC817 + BC337 in front of it gives \"double isolation\" that the project does not strictly need. We are doing it anyway because the whole point is to **build the topology by hand** instead of trusting a hidden chip on someone else's PCB - you cannot probe what you cannot see.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- A PC817 is just an LED pointed at a phototransistor inside a black 4-pin package. There is no wire between them - the only connection is light. That is what \"galvanic\" means: no shared metal.\n- This matters because the load side can be at a totally different voltage, polarity, or ground reference, and nothing bad can flow back into the ESP32.\n- The BC337 is here because the PC817 phototransistor, at the low LED current we are driving (2.1 mA), is operating well below its rated test point (typically 5 mA) - so the available collector current is modest, maybe a few mA. The BC337 amplifies that into the tens of mA the relay module's IN pin can pull cleanly. (The PC817 phototransistor's ","allocations":[{"item_name":"ESP32 NodeMCU-32S","qty":1,"notes":"Drives the PC817 LED input via 3.3V GPIO 23"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"ESP32 header to breadboard, relay module logic header to breadboard"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Breadboard rail and component jumpers"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Green","qty":1,"notes":"Visual load indicator wired across relay COM/NO via current-limit resistor"},{"item_name":"PC817 Optocoupler","qty":1,"notes":"Galvanic isolation barrier - LED side on MCU, phototransistor side on load"},{"item_name":"1-channel Relay Module","qty":1,"notes":"Active-LOW relay module; BC337 collector pulls IN to GND to energize. Note: module has its own onboard optocoupler"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Builds the isolation circuit and BC337 driver stage"},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"BC337 base pull-down: keeps base at clean GND when PC817 phototransistor is dark"},{"item_name":"Resistor 1K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current-limit for PC817 input LED (~2.1mA from 3.3V GPIO, Vf~1.2V)"},{"item_name":"Resistor 330R (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"Current-limit for the green LED demo load"},{"item_name":"BC337 BJT (TO-92)","qty":1,"notes":"NPN switch driven by PC817 phototransistor emitter; sinks current into relay module IN pin"}]},{"id":78,"name":"BC327 High-Side Load Switch for the Laser","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"USB-C into the XIAO ESP32-C3; the board's 5 V rail powers the laser and the 3.3 V GPIO drives the BC547 inverter.","description":"**What it is**: Type a command on your laptop and a real red laser beam clicks on. Type another and it clicks off. Wave your hand through it in a dusty room and you can see the line cutting through the air.\n\n**Why it's fun**: It is your first actual laser, and YOU are the switch. Stick it in a cardboard box with a mirror and you have the start of a spy-movie tripwire for your bedroom door.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3 (3.3 V GPIO control signal)\n- BC327 PNP BJT in TO-92 (high-side pass transistor for the 5 V rail)\n- BC547 NPN BJT in TO-92 (small inverter that pulls the BC327 base down)\n- KY-008 red laser module (the load being switched)\n- 1x 10K resistor (base pull-up keeping BC327 off by default)\n- 2x 1K resistor (one for the XIAO -> BC547 base, one for BC547 collector -> BC327 base)\n- Breadboard, jumper wires (USB cable powers everything off the XIAO's 5 V rail)\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n**Pin label sanity check first.** On the XIAO ESP32-C3 silkscreen, the pin printed \"D2\" is GPIO4 internally (the silk numbering starts at D0=GPIO2, D1=GPIO3, D2=GPIO4, and so on). This project uses the pin physically labelled **D2**, which the sketch refers to as `CTRL_PIN = 4`. Wire to the silk-labelled D2 pin and the code is correct.\n\n**KY-008 pin identification.** Flip your KY-008 over and read the silk. You should see three letters: `S`, `+` (or no label / middle), and `-`. KY-008 clones genuinely disagree about whether the middle pin is VCC, NC, or the cathode. Treat your own module's silk as the ground truth and only connect what is explicitly labelled. The safe routing for this project is: BC327 collector to the **S** pin, GND rail to the **-** pin, and the third pin left **disconnected**. Do not tie the middle pin to +5 V unless your specific module's silk clearly says so - on the clones where S already has a built-in current-limit resistor, paralleling another +5 V into a mislabelled middle pin will bypass the limiter and burn the laser diode.\n\nThe whole point of the circuit is to switch power on the *high side* of the load (between +5 V and the laser) rather than the low side (between the laser and GND). Low-side switching with an NPN works, but it lifts the laser's ground above true GND while it's off, which makes shared-ground setups misbehave. High-side switching keeps the laser's GND tied solidly to system GND at all times.\n\nA PNP transistor (BC327) turns ON when its base is pulled *below* its emitter. The emitter sits at +5 V. To turn it on, you have to pull the base down to roughly +4.3 V or lower. A 3.3 V ESP32 GPIO going HIGH cannot do that directly: HIGH is already 1.7 V below +5 V, but it can't sink current from a 5 V rail through the base on its own without back-feeding the GPIO protection diodes. So we use a small NPN (BC547) as an inverter:\n\n1. XIAO 5V pin -> breadboard +5V rail. XIAO GND -> GND rail.\n2. XIAO D2 (GPIO4) -> 1K -> BC547 base. BC547 emitter -> GND. (Classic NPN low-side switch driving the base of the PNP.)\n3. BC547 collector -> 1K -> BC327 base. This 1K limits base current when the NPN saturates and pulls the PNP base near GND.\n4. 10K from BC327 base to +5V. This is the default-off pull-up: with the GPIO low and the BC547 off, the 10K holds the BC327 base at +5 V, so V_BE = 0 and the PNP stays off.\n5. BC327 emitter -> +5V. BC327 collector -> KY-008 S pin. KY-008 - pin -> GND.\n\n**Boot-safety: who actually keeps the laser off when the board powers up?** The hero here is the **10K base pull-up**. The instant the XIAO is plugged in, GPIO4 is a high-impedance input (it's not driving anything), but the 10K is already holding the BC327 base at +5 V, so the laser is guaranteed off. The `digitalWrite(CTRL_PIN, LOW)` call *before* `pinMode(CTRL_PIN, OUTPUT)` is a complementary trick that makes the GPIO's very first driven state a clean LOW (no momentary HIGH glitch at the instant the pin flips from input to output). The 10K is the safety net; the digitalWrite-before-pinMode is good hygiene on top.\n\n**BC327 V_EB headroom (worth knowing).** When the BC547 saturates it pulls the BC327 base down to roughly +0.2 V, while the emitter sits at +5 V. That's V_EB ~ 4.8 V against the BC327 datasheet's V_EBO(max) = 5 V. It works fine off USB +5 V (which actually sags closer to 4.8 - 5.1 V at the board), but the margin is thin. If you ever swap in a 5.5 V supply or move to a higher rail, raise the BC547 collector via a small additional series resistor or insert a base-voltage clamp so the BC327 base never sits below ","allocations":[{"item_name":"Seeed XIAO ESP32-C3","qty":1,"notes":"3.3V GPIO source for the control signal"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"XIAO header to breadboard, KY-008 pigtail to breadboard"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Power rails and signal hops"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Build surface"},{"item_name":"KY-008 Red Laser Module (650nm)","qty":1,"notes":"Load being switched on the 5V high side"},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":1,"notes":"BC327 base pull-up to +5V keeping laser off by default"},{"item_name":"Resistor 1K (1/8W 1%)","qty":2,"notes":"One on XIAO GPIO to BC547 base, one from BC547 collector to BC327 base"},{"item_name":"BC327 BJT (TO-92)","qty":1,"notes":"PNP high-side pass transistor switching 5V to the laser"},{"item_name":"BC547 BJT (TO-92)","qty":1,"notes":"NPN level-shifter/inverter pulling BC327 base low"}]},{"id":82,"name":"ULN2003 Servo Sweep Sentinel","difficulty":"beginner","power_supply":"Two separate USB 5V supplies sharing only GND: USB-to-Uno feeds the logic, LEDs, ULN2003 and PIR; a second USB-C into a TP4056 board feeds the SG90 servo so its current spikes cannot brown out the Uno.","description":"**What it is**: Walk past it and a little flag on a stick swings back and forth while a row of five red lights pulses like a warning sign. Stand still and everything goes quiet again, waiting for the next person to wander by.\n\n**Why it's fun**: Hide it around a corner and prank your sister when she walks past. It is your very own motion-triggered alarm, like the ones in spy movies, except you built it.\n\n## Parts list\n\n- 1x Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL) - the 5V brain\n- 1x ULN2003AN Darlington Array (7-ch) - the muscle that sinks current for the LED row\n- 1x HC-SR501 PIR Motion Sensor - the trigger\n- 1x SG90 Servo (9g micro) - waves the warning flag, on its OWN 5V supply\n- 5x LED 5mm Red - the \"warning bank\"\n- 5x Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%) - one current limiter per LED (see math in Talking points)\n- 1x 470uF and 1x 100uF electrolytic - bulk reservoir on the servo rail, local decoupling on the ULN2003\n- 1x TP4056 USB-C charger/protection board - second, independent 5V source for the servo\n- 1x Breadboard, M-M and F-M jumpers\n\n## Wiring notes\n\n**Two power rails, one ground.** This is the single most important idea in the build.\n\n- *Rail A (Uno rail):* USB feeds the Uno, the Uno's `5V` pin feeds the breadboard's left `+5V` rail. That rail powers the LEDs, the ULN2003 (`COM` pin 9), and the HC-SR501.\n- *Rail B (Servo rail):* A second USB-C cable goes into the TP4056 board; its `OUT+` becomes the breadboard's right `+5V` rail. The SG90's red wire is the ONLY thing on that rail.\n- *Grounds:* The Uno's `GND`, the TP4056's `OUT-`, the PIR's GND, the LED cathodes' return path through the ULN2003 `GND` pin (8), and the SG90's brown wire ALL meet at the breadboard's shared `GND` rail. Without this common ground the servo signal makes no sense to the Uno.\n\nWhy bother? The SG90 yanks 600-700 mA when it stalls or changes direction, and the Uno's onboard 5V LDO can only deliver ~500-800 mA total. Sharing the rail will brown out the Uno - the 470 uF cap from the original draft just delays the moment of failure. Splitting the rails fixes it for real, and the optocouplers... wait, no, the ULN2003 has no opto stage; the *split rails* are what isolates the noise here.\n\n**ULN2003 pinout (16-pin DIP):**\n- Pins 1..7 = inputs IN1..IN7\n- Pin 8 = GND\n- Pin 9 = COM (tie to the Uno's +5V rail - this is the return path for the internal flyback diodes)\n- Pins 10..16 = outputs OUT7..OUT1 (mirror image of the inputs)\n\n**Connections:**\n- Uno `D7` -> ULN2003 IN1, IN2, IN3, IN4, IN5 all tied together (one GPIO drives five channels in parallel - the whole point of the project)\n- LED anodes -> Uno's `+5V` rail through a 220R each\n- LED cathodes -> ULN2003 OUT1..OUT5 (pins 16, 15, 14, 13, 12)\n- ULN2003 pin 8 (GND) -> shared GND rail\n- ULN2003 pin 9 (COM) -> Uno's `+5V` rail\n- 100 uF cap across the Uno's `+5V`/GND right next to the ULN2003\n- HC-SR501: VCC -> Uno's `+5V`, GND -> shared GND, OUT -> Uno `D2`\n- SG90: red -> TP4056's `+5V`, brown -> shared GND, orange -> Uno `D9` (PWM-capable)\n- 470 uF cap across the TP4056's `+5V`/GND right next to the servo header\n\n**Electrolytic polarity - DO NOT SKIP.** Both caps are polarised. The *longer* lead (and the side without the white stripe) is `+` and goes to the +5V rail. The *shorter* lead (white stripe) is `-` and goes to GND. Backwards-installed electrolytics get hot, leak, and occasionally pop loudly. If you're not sure which lead is which, look for the printed stripe down one side of the can.\n\n**HC-SR501 jumper.** Set its onboard jumper to **repeat-trigger (H)** mode. In this mode `DOUT` stays HIGH for the whole time motion is being seen, so the sketch's `lastMotionAt` keeps refreshing naturally. The code also works in single-trigger (L) mode, just feels slightly twitchier - the `ALARM_HOLD_MS` window bridges the gaps between pulses. The two pots: time delay fully anticlockwise (shortest), sensitivity around mid-travel.\n\n## Talking points\n\n- **Why a Darlington array at all?** A single Uno pin is only spec'd for 20 mA continuous (40 mA absolute max). Five LEDs at ~9 mA each is ~45 mA - over budget for one pin. The ULN2003 lets the Uno *say* \"on\" with a tiny logic-level signal while seven internal Darlington pairs do the actual current-sinking work. Each output can sink up to 500 mA, but the package's total dissipation limits the sum to roughly 230 mA sustained at room temp - which is still plenty for a 45 mA LED bank.\n- **It is a sink driver, not an \"inverter\".** When `D7` goes HIGH, an NPN Darlington inside","allocations":[{"item_name":"Arduino Uno R3 (CH340 clone, XL)","qty":1,"notes":"5V brain reading PIR and driving ULN2003 + servo signal"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire F-M, 20pc","qty":1,"notes":"Connecting PIR header, servo header, and Uno pin headers to breadboard"},{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Breadboard wiring (rails, ULN2003 pins, LED anodes)"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF 16V (electrolytic)","qty":1,"notes":"Local decoupling next to the ULN2003 VCC/COM pin on the Uno's 5V rail"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 470uF 16V (electrolytic)","qty":1,"notes":"Bulk reservoir on the servo's dedicated 5V rail near the servo header"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":5,"notes":"Warning LED row, each in series with a 220R resistor, anode to +5V"},{"item_name":"ULN2003AN Darlington Array (7-ch)","qty":1,"notes":"Sinks current for the LED bank from a single Uno GPIO via paralleled channels"},{"item_name":"18650 Charger / Protection Board (TP4056, USB-C)","qty":1,"notes":"Provides a second, independent 5V supply for the servo so its current spikes cannot brown out the Uno"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Holds the ULN2003 DIP, LED row, and tie points for sensor/servo leads"},{"item_name":"SG90 Servo (9g micro)","qty":1,"notes":"Sweeps the 'Do Not Enter' flag back and forth while alarm is active"},{"item_name":"Resistor 220R (1/8W 1%)","qty":5,"notes":"One per LED, current limiting (gives ~9 mA after ULN2003 Vce(sat) drop)"},{"item_name":"HC-SR501 PIR Motion Sensor","qty":1,"notes":"Detects motion, raises DOUT HIGH when triggered"}]},{"id":83,"name":"NE555 Two-Tone Police Siren","difficulty":"intermediate","power_supply":"9V battery (or 9V bench supply) into the breadboard + rail; one shared rail powers both NE555s and the LM386, with a 100uF bulk cap right next to the chips to keep the amplifier from upsetting the timers.","description":"**What it is**: Flip the switch and a tiny speaker on your breadboard wails \"wee-ooo, wee-ooo\" exactly like a police car driving by. A red LED blinks in time with the sound, so you can see the siren as well as hear it.\n\n**Why it's fun**: You built a real siren out of a handful of parts and zero code. Hide it in a shoebox, sneak up behind your sister, flip the switch, and watch her jump.\n\n## Required external parts (you supply)\n\n- **8 ohm speaker, 0.5-1 W**. Any salvaged small speaker works. Not in the kit - this is a hard prerequisite. Without it the project does not finish.\n- **9V power source.** Either a fresh 9V battery + barrel/snap clip wired to the breadboard rails, or a 9V bench supply.\n\n## Parts list (from inventory)\n\n- 2x NE555 Timer IC (U1 = slow sweep oscillator, U2 = fast audio oscillator)\n- 1x LM386 Audio Power Amp (U3 = drives the speaker)\n- 4x 10K resistors (R1/R2 timing pair for U1, R5 modulation series, R6 LM386 input series)\n- 3x 470R resistors (R3/R4 timing pair for U2, R_LED current-limit for the sweep-indicator LED)\n- 1x 22uF electrolytic (C1, slow timing cap)\n- 2x 10uF electrolytic (C2 modulation coupling, C3 LM386 input coupling)\n- 3x 100uF electrolytic (C4 rail decoupling, C5 LM386 output coupling, C6 LM386 pin-7 bypass)\n- 1x 1uF 50V electrolytic (C7 fast 555 timing cap)\n- 1x 5mm red LED (visual sweep indicator - so you can SEE the woop-woop)\n- 1x 400-point breadboard\n- M-M jumper wires\n\n## The math (so you can trust the parts choice)\n\nNE555 astable frequency: **f = 1.44 / ((R_A + 2 R_B) C)**.\n\n- **U1 slow stage** with R1=R2=10K, C1=22uF: f = 1.44 / (30K * 22uF) approx **2.2 Hz**. That's the \"wee-ooo\" rate.\n- **U2 fast stage** with R3=R4=470R, C7=1uF: f = 1.44 / (1.41K * 1uF) approx **1020 Hz** centre. When U1's square wave is AC-coupled into U2's pin 5 (CV), the comparator thresholds get pushed up and down, sweeping the audio frequency roughly across **500-1500 Hz** - the classic siren range.\n\nThe original draft used R3=R4=10K with C7=1uF, which gives only 48 Hz (below the audio band, you'd hear a buzz not a tone). The 470R choice is the fix.\n\n## Wiring notes\n\nThe whole circuit lives on one breadboard and runs from a single +9V rail. There is no microcontroller - the two NE555s and the LM386 do everything in pure analog hardware. This is the same trick used in real toy sirens.\n\n**U1 - the slow modulator (~2.2 Hz):**\n- pin 1 to GND, pin 8 to +9V\n- pin 4 (reset) to +9V\n- pin 7 to pin 8 through R1 = 10K\n- pin 7 to pin 6 through R2 = 10K\n- pin 6 tied to pin 2 (standard astable jumper)\n- pin 6/2 to GND through C1 = 22uF (long leg toward pin 6/2, short leg to GND)\n- pin 5 (CV) left floating (the kit has no ceramic cap to bypass it; the slow stage doesn't need one)\n- pin 3 (output) drives two things in parallel: the red LED via R_LED = 470R to GND (so you can SEE the sweep), AND through C2 = 10uF + R5 = 10K into U2's pin 5\n\n**U2 - the fast audio oscillator (~500-1500 Hz, swept):**\n- pin 1 to GND, pin 8 to +9V, pin 4 to +9V\n- pin 7 to +9V through R3 = 470R\n- pin 7 to pin 6 through R4 = 470R\n- pin 6 tied to pin 2\n- pin 6/2 to GND through C7 = 1uF (this is what sets audio frequency)\n- pin 5 (CV) receives the modulation signal from U1 via C2 + R5. R5 is there as a series limiter so the modulation doesn't slam pin 5 all the way to the rails - it sets the sweep DEPTH (smaller R5 = wider sweep)\n- pin 3 (output) is the raw siren waveform - feed it through C3 = 10uF straight into the LM386 input\n\n**C2 polarity** matters here. U1 pin 3 (the cap's \"in\" side) swings between 0 V and ~8 V, average ~4 V. The CV side of R5 sits at ~6 V (2/3 of 9 V). So the U1 side is, on average, the LOWER-DC side. Put the **negative** (short) leg of C2 toward U1 pin 3 and the **positive** (long) leg toward R5. If you find yourself second-guessing this, a non-polar workaround is two 22uF electrolytics back-to-back negative-to-negative - but a single 10uF oriented as above is fine.\n\n**U3 - the LM386 amplifier:**\n- pin 2 (-IN) to GND\n- pin 3 (+IN) receives the audio through C3 = 10uF and R6 = 10K series (R6 sets input impedance and slightly attenuates - the LM386 already has internal 50K bias to GND on each input, so no external pull-down is needed)\n- pin 4 to GND\n- pin 6 to +9V (this is the supply pin - getting 4 and 6 swapped instantly destroys the chip, double-check)\n- pin 7 to GND through C6 = 100uF (bypass, kills hiss and \"farting\")\n- pin 5 (output) through C5 = 100uF **directly to the speaker + terminal** (no series resistor - ","allocations":[{"item_name":"Jumper Wire M-M, 40pc","qty":1,"notes":"Breadboard hookup wires"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 100uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":3,"notes":"C4 power-rail decoupling next to the 555s; C5 LM386 output DC-blocking cap straight to speaker (positive leg toward LM386 pin 5); C6 LM386 bypass on pin 7"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 10uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":2,"notes":"C2 modulation coupling between U1 pin 3 and the R5+U2-pin-5 node (positive leg toward U1 pin 3, since U1 output is the higher-DC side); C3 LM386 input coupling (positive leg toward U2 pin 3)"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 1uF 50V (electrolytic)","qty":1,"notes":"C7 fast-555 timing cap, paired with R3/R4=470R lands centre at approx 1 kHz"},{"item_name":"Capacitor 22uF (electrolytic, mixed voltage)","qty":1,"notes":"C1 slow-555 timing cap, sets sweep at approx 2.2 Hz (long leg toward pin 6/2 of U1)"},{"item_name":"LED 5mm Red","qty":1,"notes":"Visual sweep indicator on U1 output (pin 3) - blinks approx 2 Hz so you can SEE the modulation"},{"item_name":"LM386 Audio Power Amp","qty":1,"notes":"U3 audio power amp drives the 8 ohm speaker directly through C5"},{"item_name":"NE555 Timer IC","qty":2,"notes":"U1 slow modulator (~2.2 Hz sweep), U2 fast audio oscillator (~1 kHz centre)"},{"item_name":"Breadboard 4.5x9.5cm (~400 tie-points)","qty":1,"notes":"Hosts both NE555s, the LM386, and all RC parts"},{"item_name":"Resistor 10K (1/8W 1%)","qty":4,"notes":"R1/R2 slow 555 timing pair (with C1=22uF gives f = 1.44/(30K*22uF) approx 2.2 Hz); R5 modulation series; R6 LM386 input series"},{"item_name":"Resistor 470R (1/8W 1%)","qty":3,"notes":"R3/R4 fast 555 timing pair (with C7=1uF gives f = 1.44/(1.41K*1uF) approx 1020 Hz centre; CV sweeps it through 500-1500 Hz); R_LED current limit for the sweep indicator LED"}]}]

const DSL_GUIDE = [
  'BREADBOARD LAYOUT DSL',
  '',
  'Target board: half-size, 30 columns (1-30), rows A-J. Top half rows A-E are one electrical node per column;',
  'bottom half rows F-J are another node per column; the centre gully separates them.',
  'Power rails: +5V and GND, available on both top (_T) and bottom (_B) sides of the board.',
  '',
  'Position format: "{ROW}{COL}" e.g. "E5", "B12", "J27". For rails: "+5V", "GND", or qualified "+5V_T", "GND_B".',
  'The renderer auto-picks top or bottom rail based on which is closer to the other end of the wire.',
  '',
  'COMPONENT TYPES (always use "type" + the specific fields shown):',
  '',
  '- ic: { type:"ic", name, pin1_at, pins }',
  '    DIP chip straddles the gully. pin1_at is the BOTTOM-LEFT pin (in row E or F).',
  '    Standard placement: pin 1 in row E (so chip body sits in cols pin1.col..pin1.col+pins/2-1 with',
  '    pins on rows E and F). DIP-8: pin1_at "E5" -> pin 1 at E5 (top-half col 5), pin 8 at F5.',
  '    Pin counting: bottom-left = 1, counter-clockwise from notch.',
  '',
  '- led: { type:"led", color, anode, cathode }',
  '    color is red / green / blue / yellow / white. anode is the LONG leg (+), cathode the short (-).',
  '    Anode and cathode should be in adjacent columns in the same half.',
  '',
  '- resistor: { type:"resistor", value, from, to }',
  '    value is the human label ("220R", "10K", "4.7K", "1M").',
  '',
  '- capacitor: { type:"capacitor", value, positive, negative, polarised? }',
  '    Set polarised:false for ceramic caps. Default polarised:true (electrolytics).',
  '    IMPORTANT: do NOT place capacitor legs in the same columns as a nearby IC body - the cap bulb',
  '    renders above the legs and will overlap the chip. Route the signal out via a small wire jumper',
  '    to a free column first, then drop the cap there.',
  '',
  '- wire: { type:"wire", from, to, color? }',
  '    Colour convention: red ("#ef4444") for +V power, dark ("#1f2937") for GND, yellow ("#f59e0b")',
  '    for signal jumpers, sky-blue ("#0ea5e9") for short data hops.',
  '',
  '- button: { type:"button", at, label? }',
  '    4-pin tactile. `at` is the TOP-LEFT pin. The other 3 pins auto-render at +2 cols across and 2',
  '    rows down. Stagger your button so the body straddles two columns in the same half.',
  '',
  '- transistor: { type:"transistor", at, name, pinout? }',
  '    TO-92 with three legs. `at` is the centre (base) pin. pinout default "CBE" (left to right',
  '    looking at the flat side, e.g. BC547). For BC557 (PNP) use "EBC".',
  '',
  '- pot / trimmer: { type:"pot", at, value?, label? }',
  '    Three pins, `at` is the centre wiper. Pins flank at +/- 1 column.',
  '',
  '- module: { type:"module", name, pins:[{label,at},...] }',
  '    Generic green-PCB sensor module (DHT11, HC-SR04, HC-SR501, KY-*, etc.). Lay each pin out',
  '    on the breadboard and label it ("VCC", "GND", "DAT", "TRIG", "ECHO", "OUT", ...).',
  '',
  '- servo: { type:"servo", name, signal, power, ground }',
  '    Three positions (sig / +5V / GND). The motor itself sits off-board (mention in `external`).',
  '',
  'OFF-BOARD:',
  '  external:[{from, to}, ...] lists every connection that lives outside the breadboard - the',
  '  Arduino Uno, NodeMCU-32S, power supplies, mains-side of a relay, big modules like the',
  '  4-digit display. Use plain English: { "from": "Arduino Uno pin 9", "to": "B5" }.',
  '',
  'PLACEMENT GUIDELINES:',
  '- LEDs ALWAYS get a series resistor.',
  '- For Arduino Uno / ESP32 NodeMCU-32S / XIAO ESP32-C3: put the MCU in `external` and connect',
  '  named pins to breadboard positions. Do NOT try to render those MCU boards on the breadboard.',
  '- Keep components spread out - aim to use 15-25 columns of the 30 available so the kid can read it.',
  '- Cap legs and IC bodies must not share columns - route a wire jumper out to clear space first.',
  '- Power flows: rail -> jumper -> component leg. Use red for +V wires, dark for GND.',
  '- It is fine to draw "off-board" connections (sensor modules, relay outputs) as short module',
  '  glyphs sitting in their own column block.',
  '',
  'SKIP CRITERIA:',
  '- If the project has no breadboard parts at all (e.g., just an MCU + USB cable showing a web page),',
  '  return skip:true with a brief skip_reason. Do not invent a layout for an MCU-only project.',
].join('\n')

const SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    id: { type: 'integer' },
    skip: { type: 'boolean' },
    skip_reason: { type: 'string' },
    layout: {
      type: 'object',
      properties: {
        components: {
          type: 'array',
          items: { type: 'object' },
        },
        external: {
          type: 'array',
          items: {
            type: 'object',
            properties: {
              from: { type: 'string' },
              to:   { type: 'string' },
            },
            required: ['from', 'to'],
          },
        },
      },
    },
  },
  required: ['id', 'skip'],
}

phase('Layout')

const results = await parallel(PROJECTS.map(p => () => agent(
  'You are authoring a breadboard layout for an existing electronics project for a 10-year-old. Read the project carefully, work out which parts plug into the breadboard, and produce the layout JSON.\n\n' +
  'PROJECT id:' + p.id + '\n' +
  'NAME: ' + p.name + '\n' +
  'DIFFICULTY: ' + (p.difficulty || 'beginner') + '\n' +
  'POWER SUPPLY: ' + (p.power_supply || '(unspecified)') + '\n\n' +
  'ALLOCATED PARTS (these and only these are on hand):\n' +
  (p.allocations || []).map(a => `  - ${a.qty}x ${a.item_name}${a.notes ? ' (' + a.notes + ')' : ''}`).join('\n') + '\n\n' +
  'DESCRIPTION (the wiring notes section is the most relevant):\n' + (p.description || '').slice(0, 4500) + '\n\n' +
  DSL_GUIDE + '\n\n' +
  'OUTPUT:\n' +
  '  Return JSON with id=' + p.id + '. Either:\n' +
  '    skip:true + skip_reason ("No breadboard parts - just MCU+USB", etc.), OR\n' +
  '    skip:false + a complete layout {components:[...], external:[...]}.\n\n' +
  'Quality bar:\n' +
  '- Every allocated breadboard part appears in components (or, for MCU boards, in external).\n' +
  '- LED current-limiting resistors actually allocated AND drawn in series.\n' +
  '- Cap legs do not share columns with IC bodies.\n' +
  '- All wires have colours per the convention (red/dark/yellow/sky-blue).\n' +
  '- Component placement spread across ~15-25 columns.',
  { label: 'layout:' + p.name.slice(0, 30), phase: 'Layout', schema: SCHEMA }
)))

return { results: results.filter(Boolean), count: results.filter(Boolean).length }
